/* ============================================================================
   NUR 504 Exam 2 - Respiratory & Renal Critical Care Question Bank
   300 NCLEX-style questions across six 50-question sets:
     1 Week 4 - Respiratory assessment, pleural effusion & pneumothorax
     2 Week 4 - Chest tubes: drainage systems, care & troubleshooting
     3 Week 4 - ABG interpretation & acid-base disturbances
     4 Week 5 - Acute respiratory failure, ARDS, PAH & COVID-19
     5 Week 5 - ET intubation, mechanical ventilation, VAP & sedation
     6 Week 6 - CKD, hemodialysis, peritoneal dialysis, CRRT & transplant

   Written to the brief in NUR504/Exam2/questionprompt.txt: every item is drawn
   ONLY from the uploaded Exam 2 material - the Week 4/5/6 lecture decks (slides
   and speaker notes), the Week 4 study chart, the ARF/ARDS, ABG and ventilation
   key-highlight sheets, the weekly objectives, and the in-class activities and
   case studies (ARDS, pneumothorax/trauma, Renal NGN, Respiratory COVID NGN).

   Multiple choice (4 options, 1 best answer) and SATA (5 options, 2-5 correct).
   All items are Application level or higher. Distractors are plausible and drawn
   from adjacent concepts in the same material; every rationale states why the
   correct answer is right and why the credible distractors are not. The correct
   answer is spread evenly across all four MCQ positions and SATA items vary
   between 2 and 5 correct options, so neither position nor count is a clue.

   Question object: { id, exam(1-6), topic, type("mcq"|"sata"), bloom,
                      stem, options[], correct[idx], rationale }
   ========================================================================== */
window.NUR504E2_DATA = {
  meta: {"title": "NUR 504 Exam 2 - Respiratory & Renal Critical Care", "total": 300, "exams": 6, "examTitles": {"1": "Week 4 - Respiratory Assessment & Pleural Disorders", "2": "Week 4 - Chest Tubes: Care & Troubleshooting", "3": "Week 4 - ABGs & Acid-Base Disturbances", "4": "Week 5 - Acute Respiratory Failure, ARDS, PAH & COVID-19", "5": "Week 5 - Intubation, Mechanical Ventilation, VAP & Sedation", "6": "Week 6 - Renal: CKD, Dialysis, Transplant & Nephrectomy"}},
  questions: [
  {"id": "n504e2s1q01", "exam": 1, "topic": "Age-related changes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "An 84-year-old admitted with community-acquired pneumonia has been quiet and cooperative all shift. At 0300 the nurse finds the client pulling at the bedsheets, repeatedly asking where the car is parked, and unable to be redirected. Vital signs are 99.2°F (37.3°C), HR 96, RR 22, BP 128/74, SpO2 93% on 2 L nasal cannula. What is the nurse's best interpretation of this change?", "options": ["The client is experiencing expected sundowning and should be reoriented and offered a bedtime routine", "The low-grade temperature indicates the antibiotic is failing and the provider should be notified about the fever", "The SpO2 of 93% is within acceptable limits, so the behavior is unrelated to the respiratory status", "The agitation is an early indicator of impaired gas exchange and warrants a focused respiratory assessment"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "In the older adult, a change in mental status - agitation, irritability, restlessness - is identified as the primary and earliest sign of impaired gas exchange, and the materials also flag it as an early sign of sepsis. A new, unredirectable behavior change in a client with pneumonia therefore demands a focused respiratory assessment, not reassurance. Calling it sundowning assumes a benign cause and delays assessment of the very problem the behavior is signaling. Treating the SpO2 of 93% as reassuring is the key error: oxygen saturation reflects peripheral oxygenation and can lag, which is why mental status is taught as the earlier indicator. Focusing on the temperature misses that 99.2F is not the abnormal finding here - the acute behavior change is."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q02", "exam": 1, "topic": "Age-related changes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse admits an 88-year-old with severe kyphosis that curves the spine and bends the client forward. Which change in respiratory function should the nurse anticipate as a result of this age-related change?", "options": ["Decreased gas exchange due to reduced mobility of the airways", "Decreased gas exchange due to ineffective chest movement", "Reduced pulmonary perfusion due to decreased alveolar perfusion", "Reduced pulmonary perfusion due to decreased venous blood return"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Aging reduces chest wall compliance and mobility along with respiratory muscle strength; a fixed kyphotic curve mechanically restricts how far the chest can expand, so the ventilation half of gas exchange suffers. The two perfusion options describe blood-flow problems, but kyphosis is a structural chest-wall problem that limits air movement, not arterial flow or venous return. Reduced airway mobility is not the mechanism - the deformity is in the thoracic cage, and the resulting deficit is ineffective chest movement."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q03", "exam": 1, "topic": "Thoracentesis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Two hours after a thoracentesis, the nurse notes that the skin around the puncture site is swollen and a crackling sensation is felt and heard when pressure is applied to the area. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": ["Obtain a prescription to culture the puncture site", "Document the finding as the only action", "Assess the client's SpO2 at two different sites", "Notify the health care provider"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Swelling with a crackling sensation under the fingertips is crepitus - subcutaneous emphysema - which is a known complication after thoracentesis and signals that air is escaping into the tissues. It represents a change in the client's condition and must be reported. Checking SpO2 at two sites assesses peripheral oxygenation but does nothing about the air leak and delays notification. Culturing the site treats the finding as infection; crepitus is air, not exudate, and there is no drainage or fever described. Documenting alone is unsafe because the finding may herald an ongoing pleural air leak or pneumothorax."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q04", "exam": 1, "topic": "Thoracentesis", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a large right-sided pleural effusion is scheduled for a bedside thoracentesis. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Maintain sterile technique throughout the procedure", "Position the client supine with the head of the bed flat during the procedure", "Assess for crepitus around the insertion site after the procedure", "Position the client upright and leaning forward over a bedside table", "Instruct the client to cough forcefully while the needle is being advanced"], "correct": [0, 2, 3], "rationale": "Thoracentesis is the sterile aspiration of fluid or air from the pleural space, so sterile technique is essential and the needle must enter the pleural space, not the lung. An upright, forward-leaning position widens the intercostal spaces and lets fluid settle at the base where it can be reached. Subcutaneous emphysema is a documented post-procedure risk, so assessing for crepitus is required, and imaging afterwards confirms there is no pneumothorax. Coughing forcefully during needle advancement is unsafe - chest movement while the needle is in the pleural space risks lacerating the lung; the client is asked to remain still. Lying flat is also wrong, and it directly contradicts the upright forward-leaning position that widens the intercostal spaces and lets fluid settle where the needle can reach it."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q05", "exam": 1, "topic": "Respiratory history", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is completing an admission history on a client with a chronic cough and progressive dyspnea. Which findings in the history increase this client's respiratory risk and should be explored further? Select all that apply.", "options": ["The client's father and sister both have asthma", "The client walks two miles most mornings", "The client has panic attacks several times a month", "The client reports waking at night needing to sit upright to breathe", "The client worked 22 years in an underground coal mine"], "correct": [0, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The respiratory history targets particulate-matter and occupational exposure (coal miners, wood workers and rubber manufacturers are the examples given), family and genetic risks such as asthma, allergies, emphysema and cystic fibrosis, and current problems including cough, sputum, hemoptysis, chest pain, dyspnea and orthopnea. Needing to sit upright to breathe at night is orthopnea. The psychosocial portion of the assessment specifically screens for anxiety and panic attacks because hyperventilation produces respiratory alkalosis. Regular walking is a protective activity-tolerance finding, not a risk factor to explore."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q06", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pleural effusion", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with decompensated heart failure reports increasing shortness of breath. On assessment the nurse finds decreased chest wall movement on the right, diminished breath sounds over the right base, and dullness to percussion in that area. Which finding best supports the nurse's suspicion of a pleural effusion rather than pneumonia?", "options": ["Dullness to percussion with diminished breath sounds and a dry, nonproductive cough", "Purulent sputum with a temperature of 101.8°F (38.8°C)", "Hyperresonance to percussion over the right base", "Coarse crackles over the right base that clear somewhat with coughing"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Fluid sitting in the pleural space transmits a dull percussion note and blocks sound transmission, producing diminished breath sounds; because the fluid is outside the lung rather than in the alveoli, the cough is dry and nonproductive. Crackles arise from fluid or secretions within the alveoli and airways, which points toward pneumonia or pulmonary edema rather than a pleural collection. Hyperresonance indicates trapped air and is a pneumothorax finding, the opposite of an effusion. Purulent sputum with fever describes an infectious parenchymal process; this client's heart failure makes a transudative effusion the more likely explanation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q07", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pleural effusion", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse reviews pleural fluid results for four clients. Which result should the nurse recognize as an exudative effusion requiring investigation for infection or malignancy?", "options": ["Cloudy fluid with a protein ratio of 0.7 and an LDH ratio of 0.8", "Clear fluid from a client with nephrotic syndrome and generalized edema", "Clear fluid with a protein ratio of 0.3 and a specific gravity of 1.008", "Clear straw-colored fluid from a client with cirrhosis and hypoalbuminemia"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Exudative effusions are caused by infection or inflammation and are cloudy; by Light's criteria an exudate shows a protein ratio greater than 0.5, an LDH ratio greater than 0.6, and a specific gravity above 1.012. A protein ratio of 0.7 with an LDH ratio of 0.8 meets those thresholds. The clear fluid with a protein ratio of 0.3 and specific gravity of 1.008 falls below every exudate threshold and is a transudate. Cirrhosis with hypoalbuminemia and nephrotic syndrome both alter hydrostatic or oncotic pressure, which is the definition of a transudative cause, and both produce clear fluid."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q08", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pleural effusion", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a recurrent malignant pleural effusion is scheduled for pleurodesis through an existing chest tube. The client asks the nurse, \"What is this going to do?\" Which response is most accurate?", "options": ["\"A section of the pleural lining is surgically removed so fluid has nowhere to accumulate\"", "\"A medication is instilled that irritates the lining so the lung sticks to the chest wall and fluid cannot collect again\"", "\"The fluid is drained with a needle so your breathing improves right away\"", "\"A permanent tunneled catheter is placed so you can drain the fluid yourself at home\""], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Pleurodesis instills a mildly irritant drug such as talc or doxycycline into the pleural space, usually through a chest tube already in place, so the visceral and parietal surfaces adhere and no further collection of fluid or air can form. Surgical removal of part of the pleura describes pleurectomy, a different procedure used for mesothelioma. Needle drainage describes thoracentesis, which relieves symptoms but does not prevent reaccumulation - which is exactly the problem in a recurrent effusion. A tunneled catheter is a real option for malignant effusions managed at home, but it drains fluid rather than preventing it from forming, and it is not what pleurodesis does."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q09", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A 19-year-old tall, thin man who smokes presents with sudden sharp left-sided chest pain and dyspnea that began while he was watching television. Assessment reveals absent breath sounds and hyperresonance over the left upper chest, HR 108, RR 26, SpO2 92%, trachea midline, and no jugular vein distention. Which problem is most consistent with these findings?", "options": ["A tension pneumothorax requiring immediate needle decompression", "A large pleural effusion compressing the left lung", "A primary spontaneous pneumothorax from a ruptured bleb", "An exacerbation of undiagnosed asthma"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Sudden unilateral pleuritic pain and dyspnea at rest with absent breath sounds and hyperresonance describe air in the pleural space, and primary spontaneous pneumothorax classically occurs from ruptured blebs in tall, thin young men who smoke. The trachea is midline, there is no jugular vein distention, and the blood pressure is not reported as low, so the pneumothorax has not become a tension pneumothorax - those findings are what distinguish the two. An effusion produces dullness to percussion and diminished rather than absent sounds, the opposite percussion note from the one found here. Asthma causes bilateral wheezing with prolonged expiration, not unilateral absent sounds with hyperresonance."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q10", "exam": 1, "topic": "Tension pneumothorax", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client on mechanical ventilation suddenly becomes difficult to ventilate. Which assessment findings would support that the client has developed a tension pneumothorax? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Jugular vein distention", "Tracheal deviation toward the unaffected side", "Decreased heart rate with decreased respiratory rate", "Hyperresonance to percussion with the trachea remaining midline", "Hypotension with tachycardia"], "correct": [0, 1, 4], "rationale": "In a tension pneumothorax air enters the pleural space but cannot escape, so intrathoracic pressure climbs, venous return is impeded, and the mediastinum is pushed away from the injured side. That produces jugular vein distention, tracheal deviation toward the unaffected side, hypotension with compensatory tachycardia, absent breath sounds on the affected side, and asymmetric chest expansion. Mechanical ventilation causing barotrauma is a listed cause. Bradycardia with a falling respiratory rate is not the pattern - the obstructive shock physiology drives the heart rate up and the client into severe dyspnea; that option describes a late pre-arrest picture rather than the findings that identify the problem. Hyperresonance does occur with air in the pleural space, but a midline trachea is precisely what rules tension physiology out - once the mediastinum shifts, the trachea deviates toward the unaffected side."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q11", "exam": 1, "topic": "Tension pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a chest tube for a pneumothorax suddenly develops severe dyspnea, BP 78/44, HR 136, absent breath sounds on the right, and a trachea deviated to the left. The nurse notes the chest tube has been clamped by a staff member for transport. What is the nurse's priority action?", "options": ["Prepare the needle decompression tray and wait for the provider", "Obtain a stat portable chest x-ray to confirm the diagnosis", "Unclamp the chest tube immediately", "Place the client in high Fowler's position and apply a non-rebreather mask"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A clamped or occluded chest tube is a listed cause of tension pneumothorax: air kept entering the pleural space with no route out. Removing the obstruction is the one action that reverses the cause immediately and is within the nurse's independent scope - the materials state directly that if a chest tube is clamped it should be unclamped. Repositioning and oxygen support oxygenation but leave the rising intrathoracic pressure untouched. Waiting for a chest x-ray is unsafe because tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis and treatment should not be delayed for imaging. Setting up for needle decompression is appropriate preparation but passive waiting wastes the seconds in which unclamping could relieve the pressure."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q12", "exam": 1, "topic": "Tension pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The provider is preparing for emergency needle decompression of a right-sided tension pneumothorax. Which action should the nurse anticipate next, immediately after the needle is placed?", "options": ["Removing the needle once the rush of air is heard and applying an occlusive dressing", "Obtaining arterial blood gases before any further intervention", "Clamping the catheter to prevent air from re-entering the chest", "Setting up for chest tube insertion because the pressure will rebuild"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Needle decompression at the second to third intercostal space, midclavicular line on the affected side buys time but does not fix the defect; pressure re-accumulates, so a chest tube must follow immediately. Removing the needle and sealing the site would re-create the one-way valve that caused the tension in the first place. Arterial blood gases are useful but are a diagnostic step, and delaying definitive decompression for labs is unsafe in a client with obstructive shock. Clamping the catheter would again trap air in the pleural space, reproducing the emergency."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q13", "exam": 1, "topic": "Open pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client arrives in the emergency department after a stabbing with a sucking chest wound on the left. Paramedics have applied an occlusive dressing taped down on all four sides. The client's BP is 70/30, HR 160, RR 42, and the client is cyanotic. What should the nurse do first?", "options": ["Reinforce the dressing and prepare the client for immediate chest x-ray", "Release one side of the dressing to allow trapped air to escape", "Increase the oxygen to 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask", "Insert a second large-bore IV and begin rapid fluid resuscitation"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "An occlusive dressing secured on all four sides converts an open pneumothorax into a closed one: air still enters the pleural space from the injured lung but has no exit, producing a rapid build-up and a tension pneumothorax, which this client's profound hypotension, tachycardia and cyanosis already suggest. The dressing must be taped on only three sides so it acts as a flutter valve. Oxygen and fluids are both needed, but neither relieves the rising intrathoracic pressure that is obstructing venous return - the client will keep deteriorating while they are given. Reinforcing the dressing worsens the trapping, and imaging delays treatment of a clinically evident emergency."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q14", "exam": 1, "topic": "Open pneumothorax", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is preparing the trauma room for a client arriving with a gunshot wound to the chest and abdomen. Which preparations are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Set up a thoracostomy tray and chest drainage system", "Stock the room with crystalloid fluids only and defer blood products until laboratory results return", "Prepare blood warmers and patient warming lights", "Delay setting up the thoracostomy tray until a chest x-ray confirms a pneumothorax", "Ensure intubation equipment is available and suction is working"], "correct": [0, 2, 4], "rationale": "Preparation for a penetrating chest and abdominal trauma includes calling a trauma code and setting up a thoracostomy tray and chest drainage system, because a chest tube is anticipated; having intubation equipment and working suction ready, because airway control is the first priority; preparing blood warmers and warming lights to prevent hypothermia with massive transfusion; and setting up for arterial and central venous access. Deferring blood products until laboratory results return is wrong - the materials describe having warmed blood and high-flow trauma IV tubing ready in advance, since hemorrhagic shock cannot wait for a hemoglobin result. Waiting for imaging before setting up the thoracostomy tray defeats the purpose of preparation; in penetrating chest trauma the tray and drainage system are ready before the client arrives, because a tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis that cannot wait for a film."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q15", "exam": 1, "topic": "Open pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Paramedics report they are having difficulty ventilating a client with a left-sided sucking chest wound. Which explanation best accounts for this difficulty?", "options": ["The left bronchus has been transected, so delivered breaths escape through the wound", "Blood loss has reduced the hemoglobin available to carry the oxygen being delivered", "Pain from the chest wall injury is preventing the client from tolerating assisted breaths", "Air accumulating in the left pleural space has shifted the mediastinum, placing pressure on the right lung"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "In a sucking chest wound, negative intrathoracic pressure on inspiration draws air through the hole rather than down the trachea. Air collects in the pleural space, collapses the lung on the injured side and raises intrapleural pressure enough to shift the mediastinum toward the opposite side, which compresses the remaining good lung - that is why bagging becomes difficult. Bronchial transection is not the described injury. Anemia from blood loss impairs oxygen carriage but would not make the chest physically hard to inflate. Pain matters in a spontaneously breathing client but does not explain resistance during assisted ventilation in a lethargic, cyanotic patient."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q16", "exam": 1, "topic": "Hemothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client who sustained multiple left rib fractures in a motor vehicle crash has decreased breath sounds and dullness to percussion over the left base, BP 88/52, HR 124, and cool clammy skin. Which problem should the nurse suspect?", "options": ["Simple pneumothorax", "Hemothorax", "Chylothorax", "Empyema"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Blood in the pleural space produces decreased breath sounds with a dull percussion note, and because the blood is lost from the circulation it is accompanied by signs of hypovolemic shock - hypotension, tachycardia, and cool clammy skin - which is what distinguishes it here. Trauma and coagulopathy are the listed causes. A simple pneumothorax gives hyperresonance rather than dullness and does not itself cause hypovolemia. Empyema is pus in the pleural space and develops with fever, chills and weight loss most often after pneumonia, not minutes after trauma. Chylothorax is lymph from thoracic duct injury and drains milky fluid; it does not present as acute shock."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q17", "exam": 1, "topic": "Empyema", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client being treated for an empyema has a chest tube in place and the provider has prescribed instillation of tPA into the pleural space. Which nursing action is correct?", "options": ["Clamp the chest tube for one to two hours after instillation, then allow it to drain", "Instill the tPA and immediately increase the suction to -40 cm H2O", "Flush the tube with sterile saline every hour to keep the tPA moving", "Disconnect the drainage system and cap the tube until the next shift"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "For an empyema the chest tube is used both to drain pus and to deliver tPA, which needs dwell time in the pleural space to break up loculated material; the tube is therefore clamped for one to two hours and then opened to drain. This is one of the specific, provider-ordered exceptions to the general rule against clamping chest tubes. Increasing suction immediately would pull the medication straight out before it could work. Routine flushing of a chest tube is not a nursing intervention. Disconnecting and capping the tube for an entire shift breaks the closed system and risks both infection and a tension pneumothorax."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q18", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pleural anatomy", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nursing student asks why a client with fluid in the pleural space has diminished breath sounds, while a client with fluid in the alveoli has crackles. Which explanation by the nurse is correct?", "options": ["Crackles occur only with infection, and pleural fluid is never infected", "Fluid in the pleural space blocks sound transmission because no air moves there, while fluid in the alveoli produces sound as air moves through it", "Fluid in the pleural space is thicker, so it muffles sound more than alveolar fluid does", "Diminished sounds indicate the lung has been destroyed, while crackles indicate the lung is intact"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The pleural space is outside the lung; fluid there separates the lung from the chest wall and there is no airflow through that fluid to generate sound, so breath sounds are diminished and percussion is dull. Alveolar fluid is inside the air-conducting units, so air bubbling through it creates crackles. The difference is the location of the fluid relative to airflow, not the viscosity of the fluid. Pleural fluid certainly can be infected - that is an exudate and may become an empyema - so the claim that crackles mean infection and pleural fluid never is, is doubly wrong. Diminished sounds reflect blocked transmission or re-accumulation of fluid or air, not destroyed lung tissue."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q19", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pneumothorax teaching", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is preparing discharge teaching for a young woman treated for a spontaneous pneumothorax. The nurse should teach her to seek medical attention for which symptoms? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Episodes of palpitations", "Dizziness when standing up", "Severe shortness of breath", "Prominence of one side of the chest", "Substernal chest pain"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Discharge teaching after a pneumothorax centers on recognizing recurrence and its hemodynamic consequences. Substernal chest pain and severe shortness of breath are the cardinal symptoms of air re-accumulating in the pleural space. Palpitations and dizziness on standing reflect the tachycardia and falling venous return that occur as intrapleural pressure rises. Prominence or asymmetry of one side of the chest is the visible sign of asymmetric chest expansion from a collapsed lung. All five are listed symptoms the client must be taught to report, so no option here can be safely excluded."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q20", "exam": 1, "topic": "Diagnostics", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client is scheduled for a bronchoscopy to evaluate a persistent cough and hemoptysis. Which preparation is most important for the nurse to confirm?", "options": ["A unit of packed red blood cells has been cross-matched", "The client has received a dose of a bronchodilator", "Arrangements for conscious sedation are in place", "The client has been NPO for at least 2 hours"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Bronchoscopy passes a tube into the bronchi to view the airways for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes, and the materials specify it requires conscious sedation and is generally performed in interventional radiology rather than the operating room. Planning for sedation - and the monitoring and recovery that go with it - is therefore the essential preparation. An NPO period is required but two hours is far too short to be the standard, and that interval is not identified in the materials. Cross-matching blood is not a routine requirement for diagnostic bronchoscopy. Bronchodilators treat airway obstruction but are not listed as standard pre-bronchoscopy preparation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q21", "exam": 1, "topic": "Diagnostics", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse reviews pulmonary function testing for a client and notes a total lung capacity of 6 L, a functional residual capacity of 3 L, and a residual volume of 1.2 L. How should the nurse interpret these values?", "options": ["They are within the expected ranges identified for adult pulmonary function testing", "The residual volume is markedly elevated, indicating air trapping", "The total lung capacity is reduced, indicating a restrictive pattern", "The functional residual capacity is reduced, indicating loss of elastic recoil"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The reference values given for pulmonary function testing are a total lung capacity of approximately 6 L, a functional residual capacity of approximately 3 L, and a residual volume of approximately 1.2 L - this client matches all three, so the correct interpretation is that the study is normal. Reading the residual volume as elevated, the total lung capacity as reduced, or the functional residual capacity as reduced each requires the value to fall outside the stated reference, and none of them do. Recognizing a normal result and not acting on it is as important as recognizing an abnormal one."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q22", "exam": 1, "topic": "Assessment technique", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is performing a physical respiratory assessment using the IPPA sequence on a client with a suspected right pleural effusion. Which finding would the nurse expect to obtain during the percussion step?", "options": ["A tympanic note over the right lower posterior chest", "Resonance equal to the left side throughout", "A dull note over the right lower posterior chest", "A hyperresonant note over the right lower posterior chest"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Percussion over fluid produces a dull note, which is the classic effusion finding paired with diminished breath sounds. Hyperresonance indicates trapped air and belongs to pneumothorax. Tympany is the note expected over a gas-filled abdominal viscus, not the thorax. Equal resonance throughout would be a normal finding and would argue against an effusion, so it cannot be what the nurse expects in a client whose effusion is suspected."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q23", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pleural effusion", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a symptomatic pleural effusion is awaiting thoracentesis. Which nursing interventions are appropriate at this time? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Keep the client flat to promote even distribution of the pleural fluid", "Encourage coughing and deep breathing", "Restrict the client to strict bed rest until the effusion resolves", "Administer oxygen as needed and monitor pulse oximetry", "Elevate the head of the bed"], "correct": [1, 3, 4], "rationale": "Nursing care of a pleural effusion includes a focused respiratory assessment with vital signs and pulse oximetry, elevating the head of the bed, encouraging coughing and deep breathing to prevent atelectasis and pneumonia, giving oxygen as needed, allowing activity as tolerated, and preparing the client and family for thoracentesis. Lying flat is contraindicated: dyspnea from an effusion is characteristically worse when supine, and the flat position reduces the chest expansion the client needs. Strict bed rest is also incorrect: activity as tolerated is encouraged, and immobility promotes the atelectasis the coughing and deep breathing are meant to prevent."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q24", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Four clients are on the unit. Based on the mechanism described, which client is at highest risk for an iatrogenic pneumothorax?", "options": ["A client who had a subclavian central line placed one hour ago", "A client with COPD admitted for an exacerbation", "A client who fell from standing and bruised the left flank", "A client with a 40 pack-year smoking history and a chronic cough"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Iatrogenic pneumothorax is defined as a complication of medical procedures, and central line insertion, thoracentesis and biopsy are the listed examples. A subclavian line placed an hour ago puts a needle directly adjacent to the pleura. COPD and heavy smoking raise the risk of a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax, which is a real risk but a different category and not procedure-related. A fall with a flank bruise raises the possibility of a traumatic pneumothorax, but a bruise from a standing-height fall is a far weaker mechanism than a needle recently passed near the apex of the lung, and it would not be classified as iatrogenic."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q25", "exam": 1, "topic": "Respiratory assessment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a long history of anxiety presents to the emergency department reporting numbness and tingling around the mouth and in both hands after an argument. RR is 32 and deep, SpO2 is 99% on room air, and the lungs are clear. Which explanation should the nurse consider first?", "options": ["Hyperventilation is producing respiratory alkalosis", "A pulmonary embolism is causing sudden dyspnea", "An acute asthma exacerbation is causing air hunger", "Early acute respiratory failure is developing"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The psychosocial component of the respiratory assessment specifically links panic and anxiety to respiratory alkalosis: a rapid, deep respiratory rate blows off carbon dioxide, and the resulting alkalosis produces the numbness and tingling of the extremities and perioral area described here. The lungs are clear and the saturation is 99%, which argues against asthma, where wheezing and prolonged expiration would be expected. Pulmonary embolism can cause hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, but it typically presents with hypoxemia and sudden pleuritic dyspnea rather than a normal saturation following an emotional trigger. Acute respiratory failure is defined by abnormal gas exchange, which a SpO2 of 99% on room air does not support."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q26", "exam": 1, "topic": "Gas exchange", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a large saddle pulmonary embolism has clear lung sounds, a normal respiratory rate on the ventilator, and a PaO2 of 52 mm Hg. Which component of gas exchange is primarily impaired?", "options": ["Perfusion", "Ventilation", "Acid-base buffering", "Diffusion across the alveolar membrane"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Gas exchange depends on ventilation, which is movement of air into and out of the lungs; diffusion, which is movement of gases across the alveoli; and perfusion, which is arterial blood flow. A pulmonary embolism mechanically obstructs blood flow to ventilated alveoli, so air is moving normally and the membrane is intact but there is no blood there to pick up oxygen - a perfusion problem. Ventilation is intact, evidenced by the normal rate and clear sounds. Diffusion impairment would involve a thickened or fluid-filled membrane, as in ARDS or pulmonary edema, which clear lung sounds argue against. Acid-base balance is a function the lungs help regulate, but it is a consequence here rather than the impaired step."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q27", "exam": 1, "topic": "Crepitus", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Four hours after chest tube insertion, the nurse palpates a small area of crackling under the skin just above the insertion site. The client is comfortable, SpO2 is 96%, and breath sounds are present bilaterally. What is the nurse's most appropriate action?", "options": ["Notify the provider immediately and prepare for tube removal", "Outline the border of the area with a skin marker and continue to monitor it", "Apply firm pressure over the area to express the trapped air", "Remove the dressing and leave the site open to air"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "A small amount of crepitus after chest tube insertion is expected and not immediately concerning; the instruction is to mark the border with a skin pen so that any spread can be objectively detected, and to notify the provider if it spreads. Immediate notification and tube removal is an overreaction to a stable, expected finding in a client whose oxygenation and breath sounds are normal, and removing the tube would make matters worse. Pressing on the area does not move subcutaneous air anywhere useful and causes pain. Removing the dressing breaks the airtight seal the petrolatum gauze provides and could allow air into the pleural space."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q28", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pleural effusion", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a massive left pleural effusion is found to have the trachea deviated to the right. The nurse understands this occurs because:", "options": ["Fluid in the pleural space pulls the mediastinum toward the affected side as the lung collapses", "Tracheal position is unrelated to pleural fluid and suggests a separate neck mass", "The deviation indicates a tension pneumothorax has developed on the right", "A large volume of fluid pushes the mediastinal structures away from the affected side"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "A large effusion occupies space in the hemithorax, and the materials state that with a large effusion the trachea shifts away from the affected side - here, to the right when the fluid is on the left. Mediastinal shift is defined as the movement of central thoracic structures toward one side, and a space-occupying collection pushes rather than pulls. A tension pneumothorax also pushes the trachea away from the affected side, so a rightward shift would mean a left-sided tension pneumothorax, not a right-sided one. Dismissing tracheal position as unrelated ignores one of the key assessment findings taught for pleural-space disease."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q29", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pleural effusion", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is reviewing pleural fluid volumes. A client's chest x-ray shows a small effusion estimated at 40 mL. Which statement reflects the correct interpretation?", "options": ["This volume indicates an empyema until culture results prove otherwise", "This exceeds the normal pleural fluid volume but the client may still be asymptomatic", "This is within the normal volume of pleural fluid and requires no further thought", "This volume is high enough that the client should be symptomatic and need drainage"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The pleural space normally holds only 5 to 15 mL of lubricating fluid to prevent friction; an effusion is defined as more than 25 mL, while clients generally become symptomatic above about 300 mL. Forty milliliters is therefore an abnormal collection but well below the symptomatic threshold, so an asymptomatic client is entirely consistent. Calling 40 mL normal ignores the 5 to 15 mL baseline. Expecting symptoms and drainage at this volume misapplies the 300 mL threshold. Nothing in the volume alone suggests pus; empyema is defined by the character of the fluid, not the amount."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q30", "exam": 1, "topic": "Chylothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client who underwent thoracic surgery three days ago has chest tube output that has become milky white. Which laboratory test should the nurse anticipate to confirm the suspected complication?", "options": ["Pleural fluid triglyceride level", "Pleural fluid glucose and pH", "Pleural fluid culture and sensitivity", "Pleural fluid cytology"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Milky drainage after thoracic surgery suggests a chylothorax, which is lymph in the pleural space from thoracic duct injury or cancer, and elevated triglycerides in the fluid are the confirming finding. Culture and sensitivity would be sent if infection were suspected, but infected fluid is purulent and cloudy rather than milky. A low glucose with a low pH is the pattern described for empyema, again an infectious picture. Cytology screens for malignant cells and is appropriate in the workup of an effusion of unknown cause, but it does not identify lymph."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q31", "exam": 1, "topic": "Empyema", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted with pneumonia two weeks ago now has fever, chills, pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea and weight loss. Thoracentesis yields cloudy fluid. Which findings would support a diagnosis of empyema? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Pleural fluid protein ratio below 0.5", "Positive pleural fluid culture", "Low pleural fluid glucose", "Low pleural fluid pH", "Milky fluid with an elevated triglyceride level"], "correct": [1, 2, 3], "rationale": "Empyema is pus in the pleural space, most commonly following pneumonia, and is accompanied by fever and chills, pleuritic pain, dyspnea, cough and weight loss. The fluid shows a low glucose and a low pH, cultures are positive, and the drainage is cloudy or frankly purulent - all of which must be reported. A protein ratio below 0.5 would place the fluid in the transudative category, which is driven by hydrostatic or oncotic pressure changes rather than infection, and so argues against rather than for empyema. Milky fluid with elevated triglycerides identifies a chylothorax, which is lymph from thoracic duct injury rather than infection, so it points away from empyema."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q32", "exam": 1, "topic": "Trauma care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client arrives by ambulance with gunshot wounds to the chest and abdomen. Which question is most important for the nurse to ask the paramedics in order to predict the extent of injury?", "options": ["\"Where are the assumed entry and exit wounds?\"", "\"Do you have the weapon that was used?\"", "\"What was the reason this incident occurred?\"", "\"How long ago was the client shot?\""], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Entry and exit wound locations let the team predict the trajectory of the projectile and therefore which organs and body cavities are likely injured, which directs the resuscitation. Time since injury matters for resuscitation planning but does not tell the team what is damaged. Possession of the weapon and the circumstances of the shooting are law enforcement and forensic concerns; they do not change the immediate clinical assessment, even though the nurse does have forensic responsibilities for evidence handling."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q33", "exam": 1, "topic": "Trauma care", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is caring for a client who sustained a gunshot wound and must preserve forensic evidence. Which nursing actions are correct? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Leave the client's skin unwashed until evidence collection is complete", "Wash the skin around the wounds before documentation so the injuries are clearly visible", "Cut clothing along seam lines away from the bullet holes", "Place each garment in a separate paper bag", "Cut through the bullet hole so the wound can be visualized quickly"], "correct": [0, 2, 3], "rationale": "Evidence is preserved by cutting clothing along seams as far from the defect as possible, bagging each garment separately in paper, handling items as little as possible, keeping the skin unwashed until documentation and collection are finished, and documenting findings accurately and without bias, including injury locations. Cutting through a gunshot hole destroys the physical characteristics of the defect that forensic examiners use, which is precisely what the guidance warns against. Washing the skin before documentation and evidence collection destroys residue and other findings; the skin is deliberately left unwashed until collection is complete."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q34", "exam": 1, "topic": "Trauma care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse notes ecchymotic discoloration around the umbilicus in a client with abdominal distention and rigidity after penetrating trauma. What does this finding most likely indicate?", "options": ["Intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal hemorrhage", "Subcutaneous emphysema tracking from the chest", "An expected finding after abdominal surgery", "Localized bruising from the seat belt"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Periumbilical ecchymosis, called Cullen sign, indicates intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal hemorrhage, and in this client it appears alongside distention and rigidity, which support ongoing bleeding. A seat belt mark is a transverse band across the lower abdomen with a different mechanism, and this client sustained penetrating rather than restrained deceleration trauma. The discoloration is not an expected postoperative finding and should never be dismissed as one. Subcutaneous emphysema is palpable crackling air under the skin, not a discoloration."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q35", "exam": 1, "topic": "Trauma care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A focused abdominal sonography for trauma (FAST) is ordered for a client with penetrating chest and abdominal wounds. The nurse explains that the advantage of this test in this situation is that it:", "options": ["Provides detailed imaging of solid organ lacerations better than a CT scan", "Rapidly and noninvasively detects blood in the peritoneum, pericardium and pleural spaces", "Definitively identifies the source of bleeding so surgery can be avoided", "Measures the volume of blood lost so transfusion needs can be calculated"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The FAST examination is described as a rapid, noninvasive assessment tool that detects blood not only in the peritoneum but also in the pericardium and pleural spaces, which makes it ideal for an unstable penetrating-trauma client who cannot wait for or travel to other imaging. It is a screening study, not a definitive one; in this client it served as a precursor to laparotomy, which is the gold standard for penetrating intra-abdominal injury, so it does not avoid surgery. CT provides the detailed organ imaging, as it did when it revealed the liver laceration. FAST detects the presence of free fluid but does not quantify blood loss for transfusion calculations."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q36", "exam": 1, "topic": "Trauma care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client requiring emergency surgery after penetrating trauma is unconscious and no family can be reached. Which action regarding consent is correct?", "options": ["The charge nurse may sign the consent form on the client's behalf", "Surgery must be delayed until a family member can be located to sign the consent", "Emergency procedures may proceed without written consent, and a second surgeon's opinion can verify the need", "Telephone consent is invalid, so only a court order can authorize the procedure"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "When a client is unconscious or unable to consent and emergency surgery is needed, an attempt is made to reach immediate family; telephone permission is acceptable with two witnesses on extension lines. If no family can be found, emergency procedures may take place without written consent, and the opinion of a second surgeon can be obtained to verify the procedure is needed emergently. Delaying life-saving surgery to find a signature would be unsafe. The charge nurse has no authority to consent for a client. Telephone consent is explicitly described as valid with two witnesses, so the claim that a court order is the only route is incorrect."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q37", "exam": 1, "topic": "Tension pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is assisting with needle decompression for a left-sided tension pneumothorax. At which location should the nurse anticipate the needle will be inserted?", "options": ["Fifth to sixth intercostal space, midaxillary line, on the right", "Fifth to sixth intercostal space, midaxillary line, on the left", "Second to third intercostal space, midclavicular line, on the right", "Second to third intercostal space, midclavicular line, on the left"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Needle decompression is performed at the second to third intercostal space in the midclavicular line, on the same side as the pneumothorax - air rises, so the needle goes high and anterior. The fifth to sixth intercostal space at the midaxillary line is a low, lateral site used for draining fluid, not for emergently releasing trapped air. Either option naming the right side would decompress the wrong hemithorax, puncturing a healthy lung while the client's trapped air continues to build."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q38", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pneumothorax", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse reviews the histories of several clients. Which clients have a risk factor for a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A client who sustained rib fractures in a fall", "A tall, thin 19-year-old man who smokes", "A client with cystic fibrosis", "A client who underwent a thoracentesis this morning", "A client with severe COPD"], "correct": [2, 4], "rationale": "Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax is associated with underlying pulmonary disease, and COPD, asthma, cystic fibrosis, tuberculosis and cancer are the listed conditions. A thoracentesis is a medical procedure, so a pneumothorax that followed it would be classified as iatrogenic rather than spontaneous. The distinction matters because it is based on cause, and the client who just had a thoracentesis is genuinely at risk for a pneumothorax - just not of the type the question asks about. The tall, thin young man who smokes is the classic picture of a PRIMARY spontaneous pneumothorax from ruptured blebs, not a secondary one, and rib fractures from a fall would produce a traumatic pneumothorax. All three of these clients are at risk for a pneumothorax, but only COPD and cystic fibrosis are the underlying lung diseases that define the secondary spontaneous category."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q39", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a small spontaneous pneumothorax measuring less than 2 cm is hemodynamically stable with mild dyspnea. Which management approach should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Immediate large-bore chest tube insertion", "Bed rest with supplemental oxygen to speed reabsorption of the air", "Pleurodesis during this admission to prevent recurrence", "Emergency needle decompression followed by a chest tube"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "A small spontaneous pneumothorax in a stable client is managed conservatively with bed rest and oxygen, which speeds reabsorption of the pleural air, and it may resolve without invasive treatment. A chest tube is indicated for a closed pneumothorax that is large - greater than 3 cm is the threshold given - or for a client who is unstable, neither of which applies here. Needle decompression is reserved for tension pneumothorax, and this client has no hypotension, tracheal deviation or jugular vein distention. Pleurodesis is used for recurrent pneumothorax, not a first, small, stable episode."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q40", "exam": 1, "topic": "Respiratory assessment", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is caring for a client whose respiratory status is deteriorating. Which assessment findings indicate increased work of breathing and should prompt escalation? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Inability to speak in full sentences", "Use of accessory muscles", "Oxygen saturation of 97% on room air", "Tripod positioning", "Respiratory rate of 16 with easy symmetric chest expansion"], "correct": [0, 1, 3], "rationale": "Accessory muscle use, tripod positioning, the inability to complete a sentence, and a sense of impending doom are all listed signs of acute respiratory failure and signal that compensation is failing. Confusion or a change in level of consciousness belongs on the same list. A respiratory rate of 16 with easy symmetric chest expansion is a normal, unlabored pattern and is the finding that argues against escalation, which is what makes it the one option to exclude. A saturation of 97% on room air is a reassuring finding that argues against escalation, so like the normal unlabored respiratory pattern it is one of the findings to exclude."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q41", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pleural effusion", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client reports that shortness of breath occurs with walking to the bathroom but improves with rest, and is noticeably worse when lying flat. The nurse suspects a pleural effusion. Which additional finding would most strongly support this suspicion?", "options": ["Thick green sputum with a productive cough", "Hyperresonance with absent breath sounds at the apex", "Bilateral expiratory wheezing", "A dry, nonproductive cough with pleuritic pain on inhalation"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Pleural effusion produces dyspnea on exertion that improves with rest and worsens lying flat, a dry nonproductive cough because the fluid is outside the lung, and pleuritic chest pain on inhalation as the inflamed surfaces rub. Purulent sputum indicates an infectious airway or parenchymal process, which would be pneumonia rather than a pleural collection. Expiratory wheezing reflects airway narrowing, as in asthma or COPD. Hyperresonance with absent sounds at the apex describes air in the pleural space, which is a pneumothorax and produces the opposite percussion note."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q42", "exam": 1, "topic": "Hemothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a traumatic hemothorax is about to have a chest tube placed. Which aspect of the plan should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["A small-bore Seldinger catheter will be used because it is less painful", "Drainage will be discarded without measurement since it is blood rather than fluid", "A large-bore tube will be used, and drainage output must be monitored with hemodynamic status", "The tube will be clamped intermittently to slow the rate of blood loss"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A hemothorax requires a large-bore chest tube, which is used for trauma and blood because it drains viscous fluid without clogging, and the nurse monitors both output and hemodynamic status because the drainage represents circulating volume the client has lost and may need replaced. Small-bore catheters are preferred for most pneumothoraces but clog more easily, making them a poor choice for blood. Clamping a chest tube to slow bleeding is dangerous and would allow blood and air to accumulate under pressure. All chest tube drainage is measured and recorded as output on the intake and output record."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q43", "exam": 1, "topic": "Respiratory assessment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse is reviewing four clients' assessments. Which client requires the most immediate attention?", "options": ["A client with a resolving pneumothorax whose water seal chamber has stopped bubbling", "A client with a pleural effusion reporting pain of 4 out of 10 on inspiration", "A client 2 hours post-thoracentesis with a small marked area of crepitus that has not changed", "A client with a chest tube whose trachea has shifted from midline since the last assessment"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Tracheal deviation is identified as an emergency requiring a rapid response because it signals a mediastinal shift, most ominously from a tension pneumothorax. Pleuritic pain of 4 out of 10 in a client with an effusion is an expected symptom requiring analgesia, not rescue. Bubbling that has stopped in the water seal of a resolving pneumothorax is the expected course - it means the pleural air has been evacuated. A small area of crepitus that has been marked and has not spread is the benign finding the materials describe as usually not a concern after a procedure."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q44", "exam": 1, "topic": "Diagnostics", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is preparing to obtain an arterial blood gas from a client's radial artery. Which assessment must be completed before the puncture?", "options": ["Capillary refill in the contralateral hand", "Deep tendon reflexes in the affected arm", "A Doppler study of the brachial artery", "Allen's test to assess collateral ulnar blood flow"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Allen's test must be performed before a radial arterial blood gas; it assesses collateral blood flow to the hand through the ulnar artery, so that if the radial artery is damaged or occluded by the puncture, the hand remains perfused. Checking capillary refill in the opposite hand evaluates the wrong limb. Deep tendon reflexes assess neurologic function and have nothing to do with arterial patency. A Doppler of the brachial artery examines inflow above the wrist but does not establish the dual blood supply to the hand that Allen's test confirms."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q45", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client involved in a motor vehicle crash has sudden onset of asymmetric chest expansion, anxiety, cyanosis, tachycardia and a falling SpO2. The trachea remains midline and the blood pressure is 118/70. Which conclusion should the nurse draw?", "options": ["A tension pneumothorax has developed and needle decompression is required immediately", "The findings are explained by anxiety and should be managed with reassurance", "The findings indicate a pleural effusion developing from chest wall trauma", "A traumatic pneumothorax is present and the client needs a chest tube and close monitoring"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Blunt chest trauma with asymmetric chest expansion, cyanosis, tachycardia and falling saturation indicates a traumatic pneumothorax, which is managed with a chest tube or surgical repair. The midline trachea and normal blood pressure are the discriminating findings: a tension pneumothorax would deviate the trachea toward the unaffected side and cause hypotension from impeded venous return, so needle decompression is not indicated yet - though the client must be watched closely because a simple pneumothorax can progress. Attributing cyanosis and a falling saturation to anxiety ignores objective hypoxemia. An effusion develops over time and produces dullness rather than this acute post-traumatic picture."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q46", "exam": 1, "topic": "Anatomy", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is explaining to a student why the tip of an endotracheal tube must sit above the carina. Which rationale is correct?", "options": ["The carina contains the vocal cords, which would be damaged by the cuff", "The carina is the narrowest point of the airway and a tube there causes immediate obstruction", "The carina is where the trachea divides, so a tube advanced past it would ventilate only one lung", "The carina marks the top of the trachea, so a tube past it would be in the esophagus"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The carina is the bifurcation of the trachea into the right and left mainstem bronchi. If the tube tip passes beyond it, the tube enters one bronchus and only that lung is ventilated, which is why the tip is positioned at least 2 cm above it so both lungs are oxygenated. The vocal cords sit above the trachea, not at the carina; the tube is placed between them on the way in. The carina is at the distal end of the trachea, not the top, and a tube there is in the airway rather than the esophagus. The carina is not described as the narrowest point of the airway."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q47", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pleural effusion", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which client is most likely to have a transudative pleural effusion?", "options": ["A client with acute pancreatitis and epigastric pain", "A client with bacterial pneumonia and a fever of 102°F (38.9°C)", "A client with metastatic breast cancer and pleural nodules", "A client with cirrhosis, ascites and a serum albumin of 2.1 g/dL"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Transudative effusions arise from conditions that alter hydrostatic or oncotic pressure in the pleural space, and the listed causes include heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, hypoalbuminemia, renal failure and myxedema. Cirrhosis with a low albumin fits that mechanism exactly. Bacterial pneumonia produces an exudate through infection, metastatic cancer produces an exudate through malignant inflammation and pleural involvement, and pancreatitis is specifically listed among exudative causes - all three involve inflammation or infection rather than a pressure imbalance."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q48", "exam": 1, "topic": "Pneumothorax", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a traumatic pneumothorax is anxious and frightened while awaiting chest tube insertion. Which nursing interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Encourage the client to breathe into a paper bag to slow the respiratory rate", "Administer a sedative to slow the client's respiratory rate", "Administer oxygen as needed", "Perform a focused respiratory assessment", "Prepare the client for chest tube insertion and explain what to expect"], "correct": [2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Nursing care for pneumothorax includes a focused respiratory assessment, oxygen as needed, preparing the client for chest tube insertion or needle aspiration, patient education, and addressing anxiety - which in this client is both a symptom of the pneumothorax and a source of distress. Rebreathing into a paper bag is an intervention for respiratory alkalosis from psychogenic hyperventilation; using it in a hypoxemic client with a collapsed lung would raise carbon dioxide in someone who already cannot oxygenate, making it unsafe here. Sedating a hypoxemic client with a collapsed lung is unsafe, because it suppresses the respiratory drive they are using to compensate; the anxiety is addressed by staying with the client and explaining what is happening."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q49", "exam": 1, "topic": "Respiratory assessment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse auscultates a client's chest and finds diminished breath sounds at the right base where they were clear four hours ago. The client has a chest tube in place for a hemothorax. What should the nurse suspect first?", "options": ["Fluid is re-accumulating in the pleural space", "The findings represent normal variation between assessments", "The lung has fully re-expanded", "The client has developed ventilator-associated pneumonia"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Diminished breath sounds in a client with a chest tube are specifically taught to suggest re-accumulation of air or fluid in the pleural space, and in a client whose indication was a hemothorax, re-bleeding is the concern. Full lung re-expansion would improve breath sounds, not diminish them; the sign of re-expansion in the drainage system is the loss of tidaling. Ventilator-associated pneumonia occurs in intubated clients and presents with fever, increased secretions, rhonchi and rising oxygen requirements rather than an isolated loss of sound at one base. A new unilateral change in a client with a chest tube is never dismissed as normal variation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s1q50", "exam": 1, "topic": "Open pneumothorax", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client has an open chest wound from an impaled object that was removed at the scene. The nurse observes frothy blood at the wound with an audible hissing sound on inspiration. Which action takes priority?", "options": ["Pack the wound with sterile gauze to stop the air movement", "Irrigate the wound with sterile saline and cover it loosely with gauze", "Apply a sterile occlusive dressing taped on three sides", "Apply a bulky absorbent dressing and secure it circumferentially"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Hissing and frothy blood at a chest wound are the classic findings of an open pneumothorax, where air moves through the defect instead of the trachea. The immediate intervention is a sterile occlusive dressing taped on three sides so it acts as a flutter valve, letting air escape on expiration while preventing entry on inspiration, followed by a chest tube. A circumferential dressing seals all sides and causes a tension pneumothorax. Irrigating and covering loosely leaves the communication open, so air continues to enter the pleural space. Packing the wound drives material into the chest and does not create the needed one-way valve."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q01", "exam": 2, "topic": "Water seal chamber", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse assesses a client whose chest tube was placed yesterday for a pneumothorax and observes continuous vigorous bubbling in the water seal chamber that was not present at the start of the shift. What should the nurse do first?", "options": ["Add sterile water to the water seal chamber to raise the level", "Increase the suction until the bubbling resolves", "Briefly clamp the tubing close to the chest wall and observe whether the bubbling stops", "Document the finding as expected for a client with a pneumothorax"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Intermittent bubbling in the water seal is normal with a pneumothorax while air is being evacuated, but continuous or excessive bubbling indicates an air leak. The troubleshooting sequence is to clamp briefly at the chest wall: if the bubbling stops, the leak is at the client or insertion site and the dressing is reinforced and the provider notified; if it continues, the clamp is moved down the tubing to localize the leak and the unit is replaced. Raising the suction does not fix a leak and forces more air through it. Adding water to the water seal addresses evaporation, not a leak, and the water seal level does not cause bubbling. Calling continuous bubbling expected confuses it with the intermittent bubbling that is normal."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q02", "exam": 2, "topic": "Water seal chamber", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse notes that tidaling has stopped in the water seal chamber of a client's chest tube. The client's breath sounds are clear and equal bilaterally, SpO2 is 97%, and there is no dyspnea. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": ["The client has developed a tension pneumothorax", "The lung has fully re-expanded", "The suction has been set too high", "There is a large air leak in the system"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Tidaling is the rise of the water column on inhalation and fall on exhalation, and its absence means either that the lung has fully re-expanded or that something is obstructing the tube, such as a kink or clot. With clear equal breath sounds, a normal saturation and no dyspnea, re-expansion is the explanation that fits, and it is confirmed with a chest x-ray. A tension pneumothorax would produce severe dyspnea, absent breath sounds and hemodynamic collapse, none of which are present. A large air leak causes continuous bubbling rather than loss of tidaling. Excessive suction is not a listed cause of absent tidaling."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q03", "exam": 2, "topic": "Water seal chamber", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is orienting a new graduate to chest tube assessment. Which findings in the water seal chamber should the nurse describe as normal? Select all that apply.", "options": ["At least 2 cm of sterile water in the chamber", "Intermittent bubbling in a client with a pneumothorax who still has air to evacuate", "The water column rises about 2 inches on inhalation and falls on exhalation", "Continuous bubbling throughout the respiratory cycle", "A water seal chamber that is completely dry with no fluid in it"], "correct": [0, 1, 2], "rationale": "The water seal acts as a one-way valve and must contain at least 2 cm of sterile water. Tidaling - the column rising roughly 2 inches on inhalation and falling on exhalation - reflects normal pressure changes. Some intermittent bubbling is normal in a client with a pneumothorax while pleural air is still being removed, and that bubbling appropriately stops once the air has been evacuated. Continuous bubbling throughout the respiratory cycle is the abnormal finding; it signals an air leak that must be located and reported. A dry water seal chamber is never normal - the chamber must hold at least 2 cm of sterile water to function as the one-way valve, and without it air can travel back into the pleural space."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q04", "exam": 2, "topic": "Drainage system", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse enters a client's room and finds the chest drainage unit lying on its side on the floor. The client is comfortable with no respiratory distress. What should the nurse do first?", "options": ["Replace the entire drainage system immediately", "Set the unit upright and check the connections and water seal level", "Clamp the chest tube and call the provider", "Lower the head of the bed and place the client on the left side"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The drainage system must always remain upright and below the level of the client's chest. When a unit tips over, the first action is to stand it upright and then verify the connections and the water seal level. The unit is changed only if fluid spilled between the chambers, which must be determined after it is set upright. Clamping the tube risks a tension pneumothorax and is not indicated for a stable client. Replacing the entire system before assessing whether it is still functioning is premature. Repositioning the client addresses nothing about the drainage system."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q05", "exam": 2, "topic": "Tube placement", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is to have a chest tube placed to drain a large pleural effusion. The nurse anticipates the tube will be inserted at which location, and why?", "options": ["Low, at the fifth to sixth intercostal space midaxillary line, because air collects at the base", "High, at the second intercostal space midclavicular line, because the apex has the most pleural space", "High, at the second intercostal space midclavicular line, because fluid rises in the chest", "Low, at the fifth to sixth intercostal space midaxillary line, because fluid settles by gravity"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Placement follows the physics of the collection: air rises, so tubes placed for a pneumothorax go high at the second intercostal space midclavicular line, while fluid settles, so tubes for an effusion, hemothorax or empyema go low at the fifth to sixth intercostal space midaxillary line. The correct site here is the low one, and the reason is gravity. Saying fluid rises inverts the principle. Choosing the low site but attributing it to air collecting at the base pairs the right action with the wrong physiology. The apex is where air accumulates, not where fluid drains."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q06", "exam": 2, "topic": "Drainage", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is 6 hours post-thoracotomy with a chest tube in place. Over the past hour the nurse measures 95 mL of sanguineous drainage. The previous three hours each produced about 30 mL. What is the nurse's priority action?", "options": ["Lower the drainage system further below the chest to improve flow", "Notify the health care provider about the drainage volume", "Milk the tubing to ensure a clot is not obstructing drainage", "Continue hourly measurement and reassess in another hour"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The provider must be notified of chest tube drainage greater than 70 mL per hour, and this client has produced 95 mL in one hour after a declining trend - both the volume and the sudden increase meet the criteria for reporting. Waiting another hour in the face of a threshold-crossing value risks missing active postoperative bleeding. Milking the tubing is generally avoided and would not be used here, since the problem is excessive rather than absent drainage. The unit should already be below the chest, and lowering it further does not address a bleeding concern."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q07", "exam": 2, "topic": "Drainage", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which chest tube drainage findings require the nurse to notify the health care provider? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Drainage exceeding 1,000 mL in 24 hours", "Drainage that has decreased steadily each day since surgery", "Drainage of 40 mL over the past hour", "Drainage greater than 70 mL per hour", "Drainage that changes from sanguineous to serosanguineous over two days"], "correct": [0, 3], "rationale": "Reportable drainage findings include output greater than 70 mL per hour or more than 1,000 mL in 24 hours, drainage that suddenly stops or suddenly increases, and purulent, cloudy or sediment-containing fluid or any sudden change in color. Progression from sanguineous to serosanguineous and then to serous, with a decreasing amount, is the expected course after surgery and is the one finding here that reflects normal healing rather than a complication. Forty milliliters in an hour is well under the 70 mL per hour threshold, and a steady daily decrease is the expected postoperative trend, so neither requires notification. Both are included because a nurse who reports every drainage observation is as unhelpful as one who reports none."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q08", "exam": 2, "topic": "Emergency management", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "While a client is being repositioned, the chest tube is accidentally pulled completely out of the chest. What should the nurse do first?", "options": ["Place the end of the tube in sterile water to maintain a water seal", "Obtain a stat chest x-ray before covering the site", "Cover the site immediately, using a gloved hand if nothing else is at reach", "Attempt to reinsert the tube into the same opening"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "When a chest tube is pulled out, the open tract allows air into the pleural space with every breath, so the site is covered immediately - with a gloved hand if nothing else is within reach - while the client exhales or performs a Valsalva maneuver, followed by sterile petroleum gauze and a dressing taped on three sides. A rapid response is called and the provider is notified. The tube is never pushed back in, because it is no longer sterile and reinsertion risks injuring the lung. Imaging follows, but covering the opening cannot wait for it. Placing the tube end in sterile water is the intervention for a disconnection from the drainage system, where the tube is still in the client."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q09", "exam": 2, "topic": "Emergency management", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client's chest tube becomes disconnected from the drainage system when the client gets up to the chair. What is the nurse's most appropriate immediate action?", "options": ["Clamp the chest tube and leave it clamped until a new system is obtained", "Place the open end of the chest tube 2 to 4 cm into a container of sterile water", "Tape the two ends back together without cleaning them to save time", "Have the client lie flat and hold their breath until help arrives"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "A disconnection breaks the closed system, so a temporary water seal is created by submerging the open end of the chest tube 2 to 4 cm into sterile water or saline; alternatively the tube may be clamped briefly near the chest. The drainage system is then replaced and the provider notified, which is why keeping sterile water at the bedside is standard. Reconnecting contaminated ends introduces organisms directly into the pleural space. Leaving the tube clamped for a prolonged period allows air to accumulate under pressure, risking a tension pneumothorax. Having the client hold their breath is not a sustainable or effective intervention."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q10", "exam": 2, "topic": "Tubing management", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is caring for a client with a chest tube. Which actions reflect correct tubing and drainage system management? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Keep the drainage system upright and below the level of the client's chest", "Tape the connections between the tubing and the drainage unit", "Clamp the chest tube whenever the client leaves the unit for a test", "Loosely coil excess tubing on the bed without dependent loops", "Strip the tubing every two hours to maintain patency"], "correct": [0, 1, 3], "rationale": "The system stays upright and below the chest so gravity drains and fluid cannot flow back; excess tubing is loosely coiled with no kinks or dependent loops that would trap drainage; connections are taped to prevent accidental disconnection; and during transport the tube is not clamped but kept on water seal below the chest. Stripping the tube is explicitly prohibited because it generates very high negative intrathoracic pressure that can damage lung tissue - even gentle hand-over-hand milking is generally avoided and performed only if ordered. Clamping for transport is specifically contraindicated: the tube travels on water seal, below the chest, because clamping traps air and can produce a tension pneumothorax."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q11", "exam": 2, "topic": "Dressing", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is changing the dressing at a chest tube insertion site. Which technique is correct?", "options": ["Apply petrolatum gauze and leave the dressing untaped so it can be lifted for inspection", "Apply petrolatum-impregnated gauze around the site, cover with split sponges, and tape securely", "Apply dry 4x4 gauze only and leave the edges open so the site can breathe", "Apply a transparent film dressing directly over the insertion site without gauze"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The chest tube dressing uses petrolatum-based gauze around the tube, which creates an airtight seal so air cannot be drawn into the pleural space and which also has bacteriostatic action and keeps the site moist, covered with split sponges and taped so the dressing is tight and intact. Dry gauze with open edges does not seal the site against air entry. A transparent film alone does not provide the occlusive petrolatum barrier at the tube-skin junction. An untaped dressing is not airtight and defeats the purpose of the occlusive layer."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q12", "exam": 2, "topic": "Suction control", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client has a dry suction chest drainage system ordered at -20 cm H2O. The nurse observes that the bellows indicator is not expanded. What does this finding indicate?", "options": ["The ordered suction level is too high and should be reduced", "An air leak is present in the water seal chamber", "The lung has re-expanded and suction can be discontinued", "Suction is not operating and the nurse should check the connection to wall suction"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "In a dry suction system the level is dialed on the device and the bellows serves as a visual indicator that suction is actually operating. A bellows that is not expanded means suction is not being delivered, so the nurse checks that the unit is connected and the wall suction is turned on. The dial setting itself is prescribed by the provider, so changing it is not a nursing judgment, and a too-high setting would not collapse the bellows. Lung re-expansion is assessed through tidaling, breath sounds and chest x-ray, not through the bellows, and suction is discontinued only on an order. An air leak shows as continuous bubbling in the water seal chamber, a different chamber entirely."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q13", "exam": 2, "topic": "Suction control", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client has a wet suction chest drainage system. The nurse notes gentle continuous bubbling in the suction control chamber. How should the nurse interpret this?", "options": ["This indicates excessive suction and the wall regulator should be turned down", "This indicates the water seal has been breached and the unit must be replaced", "This indicates an air leak and the tubing should be clamped to locate it", "This is the expected finding and indicates suction is working"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "In a wet suction system the suction level is governed by the height of the water column in the suction control chamber, and gentle continuous bubbling there is the expected sign that suction is operating. Bubbling that signals an air leak occurs in the water seal chamber, not the suction control chamber - confusing the two chambers is the central error in the other options. A breached water seal is also a water seal chamber problem. The amount of suction delivered in a wet system is set by the water column height rather than the wall regulator, so turning the regulator down would not change the suction level, only the vigor of the bubbling."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q14", "exam": 2, "topic": "Collection chamber", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse observes that the drainage in the collection chamber of a client's chest tube system is approaching the 2 L mark. What is the correct nursing action?", "options": ["Clamp the chest tube until the provider can remove the unit", "Empty the collection chamber using a large syringe and sterile technique", "Change the entire drainage unit, leaving the chest tube itself in place", "Disconnect the tube from the unit and reconnect it to a new unit the next morning"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The collection chamber fills from right to left and is not emptied - when drainage approaches the 2 L mark, the entire closed drainage system is changed while the chest tube itself remains in place. Aspirating drainage out of the chamber breaks the closed system and is not done. Clamping the tube to wait for the provider risks air accumulating under pressure in the pleural space. Delaying the change until morning allows fluid to back up into the tubing, which must never be permitted, and the tubing should not be left disconnected."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q15", "exam": 2, "topic": "Documentation", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which nursing actions are part of routine chest tube drainage documentation and monitoring? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Beginning the shift by visually inspecting the entire drainage system", "Recording the drainage as output on the intake and output record", "Marking the drainage level with a line, date and time at the end of the shift", "Recording only the total volume at discharge to avoid duplicate charting", "Measuring drainage hourly for the first 24 hours after surgery, then every 8 hours"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 4], "rationale": "The nurse starts the shift with eyes on the drainage system and ends it by marking the fluid level with a line and the date and time, so changes across shifts are visible. Drainage is counted as output on the intake and output record because it represents fluid the client has lost. After surgery it is measured every hour for 24 hours and then every 8 hours until the tube is removed. Recording only a discharge total would make it impossible to detect the hourly thresholds - more than 70 mL per hour or 1,000 mL in 24 hours - that trigger notification."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q16", "exam": 2, "topic": "Insertion", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is preparing a client for chest tube insertion at the bedside. Which actions are appropriate nursing responsibilities? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Position the client flat on the unaffected side with both arms at the sides", "Verify that the client's INR is above 2.5 before the procedure", "Gather supplies and verify that informed consent has been obtained", "Participate in a time-out before the procedure begins", "Position the client with the head of the bed at 45 to 60 degrees and the arm on the affected side raised above the head"], "correct": [2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The nurse gathers supplies, confirms consent, participates in the time-out, positions the client with the head of the bed elevated 45 to 60 degrees and the same-side arm above the head to open the intercostal spaces, monitors airway, vital signs and comfort during the procedure, and arranges the post-insertion chest x-ray that confirms placement. The coagulation goal is the reverse of requiring an INR above 2.5: an INR less than 1.5 and a platelet count above 50,000 are wanted, because bleeding is a listed risk of the procedure. Lying flat with the arms down closes the intercostal spaces the provider needs to access; the client is positioned with the head of the bed at 45 to 60 degrees and the same-side arm raised above the head."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q17", "exam": 2, "topic": "Removal", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse is evaluating whether a client is ready for chest tube removal. Which findings support readiness? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Continuous bubbling is present in the water seal chamber", "Chest x-ray shows the lung is re-expanded", "No air leak has been present for about 24 hours", "Breath sounds are symmetric", "Drainage has decreased to less than 150 to 400 mL in 24 hours"], "correct": [1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Removal criteria are a re-expanded lung on chest x-ray, absence of an air leak for roughly 24 hours, drainage below approximately 150 to 400 mL in 24 hours, and symmetric breath sounds. Continuous bubbling in the water seal is an active air leak, which is one of the specific findings that must be absent before the tube comes out - its presence means the pleural space is still communicating with air."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q18", "exam": 2, "topic": "Removal", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A chest tube is about to be removed. Which nursing action is most important immediately before the tube is pulled?", "options": ["Premedicate the client for pain and instruct the client to exhale or bear down as the tube is withdrawn", "Instruct the client to take a deep breath in and hold it while the tube is pulled out", "Disconnect the suction and place the client flat on the affected side", "Clamp the tube for 4 hours to confirm the client tolerates it"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Chest tube removal is painful, so the client is premedicated, and the tube is withdrawn during a Valsalva maneuver or on exhalation, when intrathoracic pressure is positive and air cannot be drawn back into the pleural space through the tract. An occlusive dressing is applied and a chest x-ray follows. Inhaling and holding generates negative intrathoracic pressure, which is exactly the condition that would suck air in. Prolonged clamping as a trial is not described as a removal step and carries tension pneumothorax risk. Lying flat on the affected side is not part of the removal technique."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q19", "exam": 2, "topic": "Assessment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse is assessing four clients with chest tubes. Which finding requires immediate rapid response activation?", "options": ["The trachea is deviated from midline", "Tidaling is present in the water seal chamber", "Crepitus is present at the insertion site and has not spread beyond the marked line", "Drainage has changed from bright red to pink over 48 hours"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Tracheal deviation is an emergency requiring a rapid response, because it reflects a mediastinal shift most commonly caused by a tension pneumothorax. Crepitus that has been marked and has not spread is described as usually not a concern and is monitored. Tidaling is a normal water seal finding showing the system is patent and responding to respiratory pressure changes. Drainage evolving from sanguineous toward serosanguineous over two days is the expected trajectory of healing."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q20", "exam": 2, "topic": "Clamping", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nursing student asks why chest tubes are generally not clamped. What is the nurse's best explanation?", "options": ["Clamping causes the water seal to evaporate, breaking the one-way valve", "Clamping traps air in the pleural space, which can produce a tension pneumothorax", "Clamping forces drainage back into the pleural space through the collection chamber", "Clamping always damages the tubing and requires the unit to be replaced"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The closed drainage system provides a one-way route for air and fluid to leave the pleural space. Clamping closes that route while air may still be entering from the lung, so pressure builds and a tension pneumothorax can develop - which is why clamping is limited to brief periods for locating an air leak, changing the unit, or when specifically ordered. Water seal evaporation is a separate, slow issue addressed by refilling the chamber, not by clamping. Drainage does not flow backward through the collection chamber when the tube is clamped; the system is designed to prevent that as long as the unit stays upright and below the chest. Clamping with padded clamps does not inherently damage the tubing."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q21", "exam": 2, "topic": "Interventions", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which interventions should the nurse include in the plan of care for a client with a chest tube after a lobectomy? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Turn and reposition the client and encourage ambulation", "Encourage coughing, deep breathing and maximal inhalation", "Limit oral fluid intake to reduce the volume of chest tube drainage", "Provide incentive spirometry", "Keep the client on bed rest until the chest tube is removed"], "correct": [0, 1, 3], "rationale": "Nursing interventions for a client with a chest tube include deep breathing, coughing and maximal inhalation, incentive spirometry, turning and repositioning, ambulation, pain management and chest physiotherapy - all of which promote lung re-expansion and prevent atelectasis. Strict bed rest until removal is contrary to that plan; immobility promotes atelectasis and venous thromboembolism, and the tube is managed during activity by keeping the unit upright and below the chest rather than by confining the client. Restricting fluids does not reduce pleural drainage and would dehydrate a postoperative client, thickening secretions at the very time deep breathing and coughing are needed."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q22", "exam": 2, "topic": "Oxygen", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nursing student states, \"Every client with a chest tube needs supplemental oxygen.\" How should the nurse respond?", "options": ["\"Not every client with a chest tube requires oxygen; it depends on their oxygenation status\"", "\"Oxygen is required only while the client has suction applied\"", "\"Oxygen must be given continuously to prevent the lung from collapsing again\"", "\"That is correct, because the chest tube itself causes hypoxemia\""], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The materials state directly that not every client with a chest tube is on supplemental oxygen; the need is determined by the client's oxygenation, assessed through pulse oximetry, respiratory rate and work of breathing. A chest tube does not itself cause hypoxemia - it treats the collection that does. Tying oxygen to the presence of suction confuses two unrelated therapies. Oxygen does not keep a lung inflated; the drainage system and the client's own ventilation do that."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q23", "exam": 2, "topic": "Emergency management", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse clamps a chest tube briefly near the chest wall to investigate continuous bubbling, and the bubbling stops. What does this finding tell the nurse?", "options": ["The tubing is kinked between the clamp and the drainage unit", "The leak is at the client or the insertion site, so the dressing should be reinforced and the provider notified", "The lung has re-expanded and the tube can be removed", "The leak is in the drainage unit, which should be replaced"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Clamping near the chest wall isolates the client from the rest of the system. If bubbling stops when the clamp is applied there, the air is entering between the clamp and the client - that is, at the insertion site or from the client - so the dressing is reinforced and the provider notified. If the bubbling had continued, the clamp would be moved progressively down the tubing to locate the leak, and a leak distal to all clamp points means the unit itself must be replaced. Cessation of bubbling with clamping says nothing about lung re-expansion, which is judged by tidaling, breath sounds and imaging. A kink distal to the clamp would not be identified by this maneuver."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q24", "exam": 2, "topic": "Tube types", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a malignant pleural effusion is being discharged home with a tunneled pleural catheter. Which teaching point is most important?", "options": ["To keep the catheter clamped at all times between clinic visits", "That the catheter will cure the underlying cause of the effusion", "That the catheter must be removed within 48 hours of discharge", "How to drain the catheter at home using vacuum bottles"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "A tunneled catheter is used for malignant effusions specifically so that the client can drain the fluid at home with vacuum bottles, which means the teaching priority is safe technique for home drainage. Keeping it clamped at all times defeats its purpose, since the problem is recurring fluid that needs intermittent removal. The catheter manages a symptom; it does not treat the malignancy causing the effusion, and telling the client otherwise would be false reassurance. It is a longer-term device, not one removed within 48 hours."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q25", "exam": 2, "topic": "Tube types", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a traumatic hemothorax and a client with a small spontaneous pneumothorax are both admitted. Which statement about the tubes they are likely to receive is accurate?", "options": ["Both clients will receive small-bore catheters because they are less painful and equally effective", "The hemothorax client will likely receive a large-bore tube, which is more painful, and the pneumothorax client a small-bore tube, which clogs more easily", "The pneumothorax client will receive a large-bore tube because air requires a wide lumen to escape", "The hemothorax client will receive a small-bore tube because blood drains easily by gravity"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Large-bore tubes greater than 20 French are used for trauma and blood because viscous drainage would obstruct a narrow lumen, and they are more painful. Small-bore tubes under 14 French, placed by Seldinger technique, are used for most pneumothoraces but clog more easily - a trade-off that matters little when draining air. Treating the two situations identically ignores the viscosity difference between blood and air. Blood does not drain easily through a narrow tube; clots obstruct it. Air requires the least lumen of anything drained from the pleural space, so a large bore is not needed for a simple pneumothorax."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q26", "exam": 2, "topic": "Evaluation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "The nurse is evaluating the effectiveness of chest tube therapy in a client treated for a tension pneumothorax. Which finding best indicates the therapy is working?", "options": ["A fall in the water seal column with inspiration", "Symmetrical breath sounds and symmetrical chest expansion", "Increased drainage in the collection chamber", "Continuous bubbling in the water seal chamber"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The goal of chest tube therapy is re-expansion of the lung, and the bedside evidence of that is symmetrical breath sounds and symmetrical chest expansion. Increasing drainage measures output but says nothing about whether the lung has re-expanded, and a rising volume may in fact signal bleeding. Continuous bubbling in the water seal indicates an air leak, which means the problem is ongoing rather than resolving. The water seal column normally rises on inspiration and falls on exhalation, so a fall with inspiration is the reverse of normal tidaling and would not be used as evidence of success."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q27", "exam": 2, "topic": "Expectations", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client asks the nurse how soon the lung will be fully inflated after chest tube insertion and the start of suction. Which response is most accurate?", "options": ["\"The lung re-expands immediately once suction is applied\"", "\"The lung will not re-expand until the chest tube is removed\"", "\"It can take hours to days for the lung to fully re-expand\"", "\"Re-expansion usually takes about three to four weeks\""], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Lung re-expansion after chest tube insertion is not immediate; it can take hours to days, and progress is followed with breath sounds, chest expansion and chest x-ray. Promising immediate re-expansion sets up false expectations and may cause alarm when the first follow-up film still shows a residual pneumothorax. The lung must re-expand before the tube comes out, not after - absence of an air leak and a re-expanded lung on x-ray are removal criteria. Three to four weeks overstates the usual course substantially."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q28", "exam": 2, "topic": "Milking and stripping", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse observes a colleague vigorously stripping a client's chest tube to dislodge a clot. What is the most appropriate action and rationale?", "options": ["Stop the colleague, because stripping generates high negative pressure that can damage lung tissue", "Allow the practice, because stripping is the recommended method for clearing clots", "Allow the practice but tell the colleague to clamp the tube first to protect the client", "Suggest the colleague strip only the distal half of the tubing to reduce the pressure generated"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Stripping a chest tube is absolutely prohibited: it creates extremely high negative intrathoracic pressure that can injure lung tissue. If any manipulation is needed at all, gentle hand-over-hand milking may be used, and even that is generally avoided and done only when ordered. Calling stripping recommended reverses the instruction. Limiting stripping to part of the tubing still generates the dangerous pressure. Clamping first would additionally risk a tension pneumothorax while doing nothing to make stripping safe."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q29", "exam": 2, "topic": "Bedside supplies", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which supplies should the nurse keep at the bedside of a client with a chest tube for emergency use? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A spare chest tube for reinsertion by the nurse", "A bottle of sterile water", "Sterile petrolatum-impregnated gauze", "Extra split gauze and 4x4 gauze with tape", "Padded clamps"], "correct": [1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Emergency bedside supplies are petroleum gauze and extra split and 4x4 gauze with tape, so an occlusive three-sided dressing can be applied instantly if the tube comes out; sterile water, so a disconnected tube can be submerged to re-create a water seal; and padded clamps, for the brief, specific situations in which clamping is appropriate. A spare chest tube for the nurse to reinsert is not among them - a dislodged chest tube is never pushed back in, and insertion is not within nursing scope."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q30", "exam": 2, "topic": "Indications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "For which conditions would the nurse anticipate that a chest tube may be required? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Status following a thoracotomy or cardiac surgery", "Empyema", "Chylothorax", "Hemothorax", "Pneumothorax"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "A chest tube drains air or fluid from the pleural space so the lung can re-expand, and it is used for pneumothorax, pleural effusion, hemothorax, chylothorax and empyema, as well as routinely after thoracotomy such as a lobectomy and after cardiac surgery. Uncomplicated pneumonia without a pleural collection is a parenchymal infection treated with antibiotics and pulmonary hygiene; there is nothing in the pleural space to drain, so a chest tube has no role unless an effusion or empyema develops. Chylothorax, a collection of lymph in the pleural space from thoracic duct injury or cancer, is also drained with a chest tube, so every condition listed here is a genuine indication."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q31", "exam": 2, "topic": "Positioning", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a chest tube is being transferred to the radiology department. A transport aide places the drainage unit on the bed beside the client's legs. What should the nurse do?", "options": ["Allow it, as long as the unit is kept upright on the bed", "Clamp the chest tube for the duration of the transport", "Move the unit so it hangs below the level of the client's chest and remains upright", "Disconnect the unit and cap the chest tube during transport"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The drainage system must stay upright and below the level of the client's chest - below the hip is the practical landmark - so drainage flows away by gravity and cannot siphon back into the pleural space. It must never be placed on the bed with the client or laid flat. Keeping it upright is necessary but not sufficient if it is sitting at chest level on the bed. Clamping during transport is specifically contraindicated; the tube travels on water seal. Disconnecting and capping breaks the closed system and risks both infection and tension pneumothorax."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q32", "exam": 2, "topic": "Assessment", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse performs a focused assessment on a client with a chest tube. Which components should be included? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Auscultation of breath sounds bilaterally", "Work of breathing, respiratory rate, heart rate and pulse oximetry", "Routine palpation of the tube tip within the pleural space", "Inspection of the chambers for tidaling and bubbling, and of the drainage amount and color", "Palpation for crepitus and assessment of tracheal alignment"], "correct": [0, 1, 3, 4], "rationale": "Chest tube assessment covers effectiveness of breathing through work of breathing, rate, heart rate and saturation; auscultation for diminished sounds suggesting re-accumulation; palpation for crepitus and checking tracheal alignment, since deviation is an emergency; and inspection of the chambers for tidaling and bubbling along with the amount, color and character of drainage, plus the water level, suction setting, connections, pain and signs of infection. Palpating the tube tip inside the pleural space is neither possible nor safe; placement is confirmed by chest x-ray."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q33", "exam": 2, "topic": "Notification criteria", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which findings in a client with a chest tube require the nurse to notify the health care provider? Select all that apply.", "options": ["An oxygen saturation that has fallen below 95%", "Sudden onset or worsening of dyspnea", "Drainage of 35 mL of serous fluid over the past 8 hours", "Tidaling of 2 inches in the water seal chamber with respiration", "Increasing chest pain"], "correct": [0, 1, 4], "rationale": "The provider is notified for tracheal deviation, sudden onset or increasing dyspnea, increasing chest pain, an oxygen saturation under 95%, drainage over 1,000 mL in 24 hours, visible chest tube eyelets or a tube that has fallen out, drainage that stops, increases or changes color, and spreading crepitus. Visible eyelets mean the tube has migrated outward and is no longer entirely in the pleural space. Tidaling of about 2 inches with respiration is the normal water seal finding and is reassuring rather than reportable. Thirty-five milliliters of serous drainage over 8 hours reflects both the expected decreasing volume and the expected progression toward serous fluid, so it is a reassuring finding rather than a reportable one."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q34", "exam": 2, "topic": "Chamber function", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nursing student is identifying the three chambers of a chest drainage unit. Which description is correct?", "options": ["The first chamber controls suction, the second collects drainage, and the third provides the water seal", "All three chambers collect drainage, and suction is controlled externally at the wall regulator", "The first chamber collects drainage, the second provides the water seal, and the third controls suction", "The first chamber provides the water seal, the second collects drainage, and the third controls suction"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The chambers are arranged in sequence: chamber one collects the fluid draining from the client and fills from right to left; chamber two is the water seal, which holds at least 2 cm of sterile water and acts as the one-way valve; chamber three is the suction control, either wet with a water column or dry with a dial and bellows. The other orders misplace the water seal or the suction control. Suction level in a wet system is governed by the water column height and in a dry system by the dial on the unit, not simply by the wall regulator, and only one chamber collects drainage."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q35", "exam": 2, "topic": "Water seal maintenance", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse notes that the water level in the suction control chamber of a wet suction system has dropped below the prescribed level over several days. What is the correct action?", "options": ["Document the change as expected and take no further action", "Increase the wall suction to compensate for the lower water level", "Replace the entire drainage unit", "Add sterile water to return the column to the prescribed level"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "In a wet suction system the water column evaporates over time, and the nurse refills it to the prescribed level so that the suction delivered matches what was ordered. Replacing the whole unit is unnecessary for a problem that is corrected by adding water. Increasing wall suction does not raise the amount of suction transmitted to the client in a wet system, because the water column height determines that - it only makes the chamber bubble more vigorously. Doing nothing leaves the client receiving less suction than prescribed."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q36", "exam": 2, "topic": "Delegation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is caring for a client with a chest tube and is working with an unlicensed assistive personnel. Which task is appropriate to delegate?", "options": ["Assisting the client to ambulate while the nurse manages the drainage unit", "Evaluating breath sounds for evidence of lung re-expansion", "Determining whether the drainage color change requires provider notification", "Assessing the water seal chamber for tidaling and bubbling"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Ambulation is within the scope of unlicensed assistive personnel, and ambulating is encouraged for clients with chest tubes, with the nurse keeping the drainage unit upright and below the chest. Assessing the chambers for tidaling and bubbling requires interpretation of whether findings are normal or indicate an air leak, which is nursing assessment. Deciding whether a drainage change meets notification criteria is nursing judgment. Auscultating and evaluating breath sounds for re-expansion is an assessment that cannot be delegated."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q37", "exam": 2, "topic": "Psychosocial care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client who had a thoracotomy with lobectomy for lung cancer has a chest tube and is withdrawn and tearful. Which nursing consideration is most relevant?", "options": ["Clients undergoing thoracotomy often have cancer and may need emotional and palliative support", "Emotional concerns should be deferred until the chest tube is removed", "Withdrawal indicates hypoxemia and the nurse should increase the oxygen flow rate", "The emotional response is a known side effect of the analgesia and will resolve as it wears off"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The materials specifically note that clients having thoracotomies generally do so because of cancer and may need emotional support, including palliative care involvement for chronic conditions and malignancy. Attributing the response to analgesia dismisses a legitimate reaction to a cancer diagnosis and major surgery. Deferring emotional care until the tube is out postpones a need that exists now and is part of holistic care. While a change in mental status can signal hypoxemia and should always be assessed, withdrawal and tearfulness in a client newly diagnosed with cancer is not by itself evidence of hypoxemia, and raising oxygen without assessment is not a substitute for addressing the distress."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q38", "exam": 2, "topic": "Troubleshooting", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse finds that a client's chest tube has stopped draining and there is no tidaling. The client is sitting upright, has mild dyspnea, and breath sounds are diminished at the base. What should the nurse assess first?", "options": ["The suction control dial setting on the unit", "The tubing for kinks, dependent loops or visible clots", "Whether the client has had a bowel movement", "The client's temperature and white blood cell count"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "When drainage stops and tidaling is absent, the possibilities are that the lung has re-expanded or that the tube is obstructed by a kink, a dependent loop or a clot. Diminished breath sounds with dyspnea argue against re-expansion and point to obstruction, so inspecting the tubing is the first and most directly corrective assessment. Temperature and white count screen for infection, which is not what this picture suggests. The suction setting can be verified but would not explain absent tidaling, which reflects pressure transmission from the pleural space rather than applied suction. Bowel function is unrelated to chest tube drainage."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q39", "exam": 2, "topic": "Insertion site care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is labeling a chest tube dressing after a dressing change. Which information should be included?", "options": ["The suction level prescribed by the provider", "The client's oxygen saturation at the time of the change", "The amount of drainage in the collection chamber", "The date, time and the nurse's initials"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The chest tube dressing is labeled with the date, time and initials so the next clinician knows when the airtight dressing was last changed and by whom. Drainage volume belongs in the drainage record and is marked on the collection chamber itself with a line, date and time. The suction level is documented in the medical record and set on the unit, not written on the dressing. The oxygen saturation is part of the assessment documentation rather than a dressing label."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q40", "exam": 2, "topic": "Tube selection", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Two clients need chest tubes: one with a traumatic hemothorax draining frank blood, and one with a small spontaneous pneumothorax. The provider asks the nurse to set up a 28 French tube and a 12 French tube. How should the nurse allocate them?", "options": ["Either tube for either client, since lumen size does not affect drainage", "The 28 French tube for the hemothorax and the 12 French tube for the pneumothorax", "Both clients should receive the 12 French tube because it causes less pain", "The 12 French tube for the hemothorax and the 28 French tube for the pneumothorax"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Tube size in millimeters is the French size divided by three, so a 28 French tube is a large-bore tube of roughly 9 mm and a 12 French tube is a small-bore tube of about 4 mm. Large-bore tubes above roughly 20 French are used for trauma and blood, because viscous drainage and clots obstruct a narrow lumen, and they are more painful. Small-bore tubes under 14 French, placed by Seldinger technique, are used for most pneumothoraces, since air needs the least lumen of anything drained from the pleural space. Reversing the two would leave the bleeding client with a tube that clots off while subjecting the pneumothorax client to unnecessary pain. Lumen size very much affects what will drain. Choosing the small tube for both on the basis of comfort would prioritize pain over the drainage the hemothorax client's survival depends on."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q41", "exam": 2, "topic": "Assessment technique", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse wants to determine whether a client's chest tube has an air leak. Which technique best elicits bubbling if a leak is present?", "options": ["Briefly disconnect the tubing from the unit while observing the collection chamber", "Ask the client to exhale forcefully or cough while the nurse observes the water seal chamber", "Ask the client to hold their breath for 10 seconds while observing the suction control chamber", "Raise the drainage unit to the level of the client's chest while observing all three chambers"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Bubbling and tidaling are evaluated during exhalation, forceful cough and position changes, because these maneuvers raise intrathoracic pressure and push any leaking air through the water seal, where a leak appears as bubbling. Breath-holding does not generate the pressure change, and the suction control chamber is the wrong chamber to watch for a leak. Disconnecting the tubing deliberately creates the very break in the closed system that the nurse is trying to rule out. Raising the unit to chest level risks drainage flowing back toward the client and is never done."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q42", "exam": 2, "topic": "Emergency management", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client's chest tube is accidentally pulled out. After covering the site, which actions should the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Call a rapid response and stay with the client", "Apply a dry gauze dressing taped securely on all four sides", "Reinsert the chest tube through the existing tract to restore the seal", "Apply sterile petroleum gauze with a dressing taped on three sides", "Obtain vital signs and perform a focused respiratory assessment"], "correct": [0, 3, 4], "rationale": "A dislodged chest tube requires immediate rapid response notification and the nurse's continuous presence. The site is covered with sterile petroleum gauze taped on three sides so air can escape but not enter, which prevents a tension pneumothorax. Vital signs and a focused respiratory assessment detect deterioration, and the provider is notified with follow-up imaging to assess tube location and any residual pneumothorax. The tube is never pushed back in: it has been contaminated and reinsertion risks injuring the lung. Taping a dressing on all four sides seals the tract completely, so air entering from the lung cannot escape - this is the mechanism that converts the situation into a tension pneumothorax, which is why the dressing is taped on three sides."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q43", "exam": 2, "topic": "Drainage characteristics", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is 36 hours post-cardiac surgery with mediastinal and pleural chest tubes. The drainage, which had become pink and was decreasing, is now bright red and has increased to 110 mL in the last hour. How should the nurse interpret this?", "options": ["This indicates the tubes are functioning well and should be left alone", "This suggests an infection is developing in the mediastinum", "This is the expected progression of drainage after cardiac surgery", "This represents a change requiring immediate provider notification for possible bleeding"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Expected drainage moves from sanguineous to serosanguineous to serous with a decreasing volume. A reversal of that trend - drainage returning to bright red and increasing above the 70 mL per hour threshold - meets two separate notification criteria, a sudden color change and excessive hourly output, and raises concern for postoperative bleeding. Calling this the expected progression inverts the normal trajectory. Treating increasing bloody output as reassuring confuses patency with safety. Infection produces purulent, cloudy drainage with sediment, often with fever, rather than a sudden return of frank blood."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q44", "exam": 2, "topic": "Safety", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse is precepting a student who says, \"The drainage unit has a kickstand, so it is fine to leave it flat on the floor.\" What is the nurse's best response?", "options": ["\"It is fine as long as it stays below the client's chest, regardless of position\"", "\"Flat on the floor is preferred because it keeps the unit furthest below the chest\"", "\"That is correct, since the kickstand prevents the chambers from mixing\"", "\"The unit must always remain upright; the kickstand holds it up but it should never lie flat\""], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The drainage system must never be flat; it is always kept upright, either standing on its kickstand or hung from a chest drainage hanger at the side of the bed. If a unit tips and fluid spills between the chambers, the water seal is compromised and the unit must be replaced. The kickstand exists to hold the unit upright, not to make a flat position acceptable. Being below the chest is necessary but does not substitute for remaining upright - both conditions must be met. Maximizing the distance below the chest by laying the unit flat sacrifices the function of the water seal."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q45", "exam": 2, "topic": "Placement confirmation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A chest tube has just been inserted at the bedside. Which action should the nurse take to confirm correct placement?", "options": ["Auscultate for bilateral breath sounds only", "Verify that the client reports relief of chest pain", "Arrange for a chest x-ray", "Confirm that drainage is present in the collection chamber"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Chest tube placement is confirmed by chest x-ray, and the tube can also be seen on the film. Auscultation is part of the post-insertion assessment and improving breath sounds are reassuring, but they do not establish where the tube tip lies. Drainage appearing in the collection chamber shows the tube is draining something but not that it is correctly positioned - a tube with eyelets partly outside the pleural space may still yield drainage. Symptom relief is a desirable outcome, not a confirmation of placement."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q46", "exam": 2, "topic": "Risks", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is reviewing the risks of chest tube insertion with a client before the procedure. Which risks should be included? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Bleeding", "A permanent need for home oxygen therapy", "Recurrent pneumothorax", "Permanent loss of the affected lung", "Injury to nearby organs"], "correct": [0, 2, 4], "rationale": "The listed risks of chest tube insertion are bleeding, injury to organs, and recurrent pneumothorax, and infection is a recognized concern at any invasive site, which is why the nurse assesses for signs of infection as part of routine chest tube care. Permanent loss of the lung is not a risk of the procedure - the entire purpose of the tube is to allow the lung to re-expand, and re-expansion occurs over hours to days. A permanent oxygen requirement is not a risk of the procedure; not every client with a chest tube even needs supplemental oxygen, and the tube is placed so the lung can re-expand."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q47", "exam": 2, "topic": "Prioritization", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "The nurse receives report on four clients with chest tubes. Which client should the nurse assess first?", "options": ["A client with 45 mL of serosanguineous drainage in the last hour", "A client whose chest tube drainage abruptly stopped two hours ago and who now reports increasing dyspnea", "A client with a small area of crepitus marked with a skin pen four hours ago", "A client whose water seal chamber shows tidaling with respiration"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Drainage that suddenly stops is itself a reportable finding, and when it is accompanied by increasing dyspnea the combination suggests the pleural collection is re-accumulating behind an obstructed tube - a situation that can progress to tension physiology. That client needs assessment first. Forty-five milliliters of serosanguineous drainage in an hour is below the 70 mL per hour threshold and reflects the expected color progression. Tidaling is a normal water seal finding. Crepitus that was marked and has not been described as spreading is the benign finding that is monitored rather than escalated."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q48", "exam": 2, "topic": "Client teaching", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is teaching a client who has a chest tube in place. Which instructions should be included? Select all that apply.", "options": ["\"Tell me right away if your breathing suddenly gets worse or your chest pain increases\"", "\"Keep the tubing clamped whenever you are out of bed\"", "\"Let me know before you get up so I can manage the drainage container\"", "\"Use your incentive spirometer and take deep breaths regularly\"", "\"Ask for pain medicine before activity so you can breathe deeply and move\""], "correct": [0, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Client teaching supports lung re-expansion and early recognition of complications: incentive spirometry and deep breathing prevent atelectasis; sudden worsening dyspnea and increasing chest pain are findings the provider must know about; involving the nurse before getting up keeps the unit upright and below the chest; and premedicating for pain makes deep breathing and ambulation possible. Teaching the client to clamp the tubing when out of bed is unsafe - the tube stays on water seal during activity and transport, because clamping can trap air and produce a tension pneumothorax."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q49", "exam": 2, "topic": "Troubleshooting", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse clamps the tubing near the chest wall of a client with continuous bubbling, and the bubbling continues. The nurse then moves the clamp progressively down the tubing toward the unit, and the bubbling persists at every point. What does this indicate?", "options": ["The leak is in the drainage unit itself, which should be replaced", "The client has developed a tension pneumothorax", "The water seal chamber needs additional sterile water", "The leak is at the insertion site and the dressing should be reinforced"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Moving the clamp down the tubing isolates progressively more of the system from the client. If bubbling never stops no matter where the clamp is placed, the source of air lies beyond all of those points - in the drainage unit - and the unit is replaced. Bubbling that stopped with the clamp at the chest wall would have localized the leak to the client or insertion site, which is not what happened here. Tension pneumothorax presents with hemodynamic and respiratory collapse, not simply with persistent bubbling. Adding water to the water seal corrects evaporation and does not resolve an air leak."},
  {"id": "n504e2s2q50", "exam": 2, "topic": "Crepitus", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse marked the border of a small area of crepitus on a client's chest wall at 0800. At 1600 the crackling area extends well beyond the marked line and the client reports increasing chest tightness. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": ["Apply a pressure dressing over the area to stop further spread", "Re-mark the new border and continue routine monitoring", "Reassure the client that subcutaneous emphysema always resolves on its own", "Notify the provider, because spreading subcutaneous emphysema indicates an ongoing air leak"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "A small amount of crepitus after chest tube insertion is usually not a concern and is marked and monitored, but spread beyond the marked border is the specific trigger for notifying the provider, because it means air continues to escape into the tissues. The marking exists precisely so that spread can be detected and acted on, not simply re-documented. A pressure dressing does not stop air from tracking through subcutaneous tissue and may impair chest expansion. Reassuring the client that it always resolves ignores the underlying leak and the new symptom of chest tightness."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q01", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client who received repeated doses of an opioid for postoperative pain has these arterial blood gases: pH 7.22, PaCO2 55 mm Hg, HCO3 25 mEq/L, PaO2 55 mm Hg. How should the nurse interpret these results?", "options": ["Partially compensated respiratory acidosis with mild hypoxemia", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis with moderate hypoxemia", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis with moderate hypoxemia", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis with severe hypoxemia"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The pH of 7.22 is below 7.35, so the client is acidotic. The PaCO2 of 55 is above 45 and matches the acidosis, making the problem respiratory; the HCO3 of 25 is within the normal range, so no compensation has begun - that is uncompensated. The PaO2 of 55 falls in the 40 to 59 range, which is moderate hypoxemia. Partial compensation would require the HCO3 to be abnormal, and it is not. The problem cannot be metabolic because the bicarbonate is normal while the carbon dioxide is not. Full compensation would require the pH to have returned to the normal range, which it has not."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q02", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with persistent vomiting for four days has these arterial blood gases: pH 7.50, PaCO2 42 mm Hg, HCO3 33 mEq/L, PaO2 75 mm Hg. How should the nurse interpret these results?", "options": ["Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis with mild hypoxemia", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis with moderate hypoxemia", "Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis without hypoxemia", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis with mild hypoxemia"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The pH of 7.50 is above 7.45, so the client is alkalotic. The HCO3 of 33 is above the normal range and matches the alkalosis, making this metabolic - consistent with the loss of stomach acid from prolonged vomiting. The PaCO2 of 42 is normal, so the lungs have not begun compensating, which makes it uncompensated. The PaO2 of 75 falls in the 60 to 79 range, which is mild hypoxemia. Partial compensation requires an abnormal PaCO2, which is absent. The problem is not respiratory because the carbon dioxide is normal. Full compensation would require a pH back within 7.35 to 7.45."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q03", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with COPD has these arterial blood gases: pH 7.36, PaCO2 55 mm Hg, HCO3 30 mEq/L, PaO2 87 mm Hg. How should the nurse interpret these results?", "options": ["Normal acid-base balance requiring no further analysis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis without hypoxemia", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis with mild hypoxemia", "Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis without hypoxemia"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The pH of 7.36 is within the normal range but sits on the acidic side of the 7.40 midpoint, which is the clue used to identify the primary problem in full compensation. Both the PaCO2 of 55 and the HCO3 of 30 are abnormal, which is the signature of full compensation. Because the pH leans acidic and the carbon dioxide is elevated, the primary disorder is respiratory acidosis and the kidneys have retained bicarbonate to normalize the pH. The PaO2 of 87 is normal, so there is no hypoxemia. Partial compensation would leave the pH outside the normal range. Calling it normal ignores two abnormal values. If this were metabolic alkalosis, the pH would lean toward the alkalotic side of 7.40."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q04", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted with severe diarrhea for three days has these arterial blood gases: pH 7.30, PaCO2 40 mm Hg, HCO3 18 mEq/L, PaO2 55 mm Hg. How should the nurse interpret these results?", "options": ["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis with moderate hypoxemia", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis with mild hypoxemia", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis with moderate hypoxemia", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis with severe hypoxemia"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The pH of 7.30 is acidotic. The HCO3 of 18 is below 22 and matches the acidosis, so the problem is metabolic - consistent with base lost through severe diarrhea. The PaCO2 of 40 is normal, meaning the lungs have not yet begun to blow off carbon dioxide, so the disorder is uncompensated. The PaO2 of 55 is in the 40 to 59 range, which is moderate hypoxemia. Partial compensation would require an abnormal PaCO2. The disorder is not respiratory because the carbon dioxide is normal while the bicarbonate is not. Full compensation would require a pH that had returned to normal."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q05", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client brought in after a prolonged panic episode has these arterial blood gases: pH 7.48, PaCO2 30 mm Hg, HCO3 24 mEq/L, PaO2 55 mm Hg. Which interpretation and intervention are most appropriate?", "options": ["Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis with moderate hypoxemia; reduce anxiety and address the low PaO2", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis without hypoxemia; have the client breathe into a paper bag only", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis with moderate hypoxemia; stop any diuretics", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis; adjust the ventilator settings"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The pH of 7.48 is alkalotic and the PaCO2 of 30 is low and matches it, so this is respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation. The HCO3 of 24 is normal, so it is uncompensated. Critically, the PaO2 of 55 indicates moderate hypoxemia, and the materials emphasize always checking the PaO2 because a low value is a rescue priority. Treating this as a simple anxiety problem and reaching only for a paper bag overlooks real hypoxemia, which rebreathing would worsen. The disorder is not metabolic, since the bicarbonate is normal. Partial compensation would require an abnormal bicarbonate, and this client is not described as ventilated."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q06", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A mechanically ventilated client with a set tidal volume of 500 mL and a rate of 12 breaths per minute has these arterial blood gases: pH 7.32, PaO2 84 mm Hg, PaCO2 56 mm Hg, HCO3 25 mEq/L. What is the nurse's interpretation?", "options": ["Ventilation is inadequate and respiratory acidosis is present", "The results show signs of severe hypoxemia", "Ventilation is excessive and respiratory alkalosis is present", "Ventilation is adequate to maintain oxygenation"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "A pH of 7.32 with a PaCO2 of 56 means carbon dioxide is being retained, which by definition is inadequate ventilation producing respiratory acidosis; the bicarbonate of 25 is normal, so no compensation has occurred. The PaO2 of 84 is normal, so although oxygenation is adequate the ventilation is not - treating these gases as acceptable would miss the acidosis entirely. Excessive ventilation would blow off carbon dioxide and produce a low PaCO2 with alkalosis, the opposite pattern. Severe hypoxemia means a PaO2 below 40, and this client's PaO2 of 84 is within the normal range of 80 to 100."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q07", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A trauma client has these arterial blood gases on 100% oxygen: pH 7.25, PaCO2 92 mm Hg, PaO2 32 mm Hg, HCO3 26 mEq/L. How should the nurse interpret this?", "options": ["Metabolic acidosis from hypoperfusion with mild hypoxemia", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis with moderate hypoxemia", "Combined respiratory and metabolic acidosis with adequate oxygenation", "Severe respiratory acidosis with profound hypoxemia requiring immediate resuscitation"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The pH of 7.25 is markedly acidotic, the PaCO2 of 92 is profoundly elevated and matches the acidosis, so this is severe respiratory acidosis, and the HCO3 of 26 is normal, meaning no compensation. A PaO2 of 32 is below 40, which is severe hypoxemia, and it is occurring despite 100% oxygen - this client is near death without immediate airway control and ventilation. Partial compensation would require an abnormal bicarbonate, and a PaO2 of 32 is far worse than moderate. The disorder is not metabolic because the bicarbonate is normal. There is no second metabolic acidosis evident, and a PaO2 of 32 is the opposite of adequate oxygenation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q08", "exam": 3, "topic": "Compensation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse compares two sets of results from the same client: at 0600, pH 7.28, PaCO2 59, HCO3 24; at 1800, pH 7.28, PaCO2 59, HCO3 32. What has changed between the two sets?", "options": ["The kidneys have begun compensating, so the disorder is now partially compensated", "The client's respiratory status has improved", "The disorder has become fully compensated", "The client has developed a second, metabolic alkalosis"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "In the first set the bicarbonate is normal, so the respiratory acidosis is uncompensated. In the second set the bicarbonate has risen to 32 while the pH remains abnormal, which is the definition of partial compensation - the kidneys have started retaining bicarbonate to offset the retained carbon dioxide, a process that takes hours to days. The rising bicarbonate is a compensatory response to the primary problem, not a separate disorder. Full compensation would require the pH to have returned into the 7.35 to 7.45 range, and it is unchanged at 7.28. The respiratory status has not improved; the PaCO2 is identical at 59."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q09", "exam": 3, "topic": "Compensation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is explaining compensation to a student. Which statement best reflects how the two systems compensate for each other?", "options": ["Only the system that caused the problem can correct it", "The lungs can compensate within minutes by changing the respiratory rate, while the kidneys take hours to days", "The kidneys compensate within minutes, while the lungs take hours to days", "Both systems compensate at the same rate, within about one hour"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The lungs regulate carbon dioxide by increasing or decreasing the rate and depth of breathing and respond within minutes, which is why respiratory compensation is described as occurring quickly. The kidneys manipulate bicarbonate and hydrogen ions through secretion, absorption and reabsorption, and respond over hours to days. Reversing the two inverts the physiology. The rates are not equivalent. The premise that only the causative system can correct the problem contradicts the entire concept of compensation, in which the opposite system moves in the other direction to normalize pH."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q10", "exam": 3, "topic": "Metabolic acidosis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with diabetic ketoacidosis is breathing deeply and rapidly at a rate of 32. The nurse understands this breathing pattern is:", "options": ["An indication that the client requires immediate sedation to slow the rate", "Evidence of an anxiety reaction that should be treated with reassurance", "Kussmaul respirations, a compensatory attempt to blow off carbon dioxide", "A sign of developing respiratory alkalosis as the primary problem"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Kussmaul respirations are deep, rapid, often labored breaths that occur as a compensatory mechanism in metabolic acidosis: the lungs blow off carbon dioxide to reduce acid load. They are a sign of a medical emergency and reflect the body's attempt to address the underlying problem, so the treatment is directed at the cause - in diabetic ketoacidosis, managing the hyperglycemia with insulin. Calling it a primary respiratory alkalosis mistakes the compensation for the disorder. Attributing it to anxiety would delay treatment of a life-threatening metabolic emergency. Sedating the client would suppress the very compensation keeping the pH from falling further, which could be fatal."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q11", "exam": 3, "topic": "Respiratory acidosis", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which conditions would the nurse expect to produce respiratory acidosis? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Aspiration of a foreign body obstructing the airway", "Opioid overdose causing respiratory depression", "Tension pneumothorax", "Pulmonary edema", "Pneumonia impairing diffusion"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Respiratory acidosis results from hypoventilation - any condition where carbon dioxide cannot be blown off. Opioid overdose and other causes of respiratory depression such as anesthesia and increased intracranial pressure suppress the drive to breathe. Airway obstruction from aspiration, sputum or choking blocks air movement. Tension pneumothorax, pleural effusion and chest trauma restrict ventilation mechanically. Pneumonia, COPD, ARDS, pulmonary embolism and pulmonary edema impair diffusion. Prolonged nasogastric suction removes gastric acid and causes metabolic alkalosis, which is the opposite disturbance. Pulmonary edema also impairs diffusion and is listed alongside pneumonia, COPD, ARDS and pulmonary embolism, so every condition here produces respiratory acidosis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q12", "exam": 3, "topic": "Metabolic alkalosis", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client who has had continuous nasogastric suction for several days now has a pH of 7.52 and HCO3 of 31 mEq/L. Which nursing interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Administer sodium bicarbonate to stabilize the pH", "Increase the nasogastric suction to remove more gastric contents", "Monitor the serum potassium level", "Obtain an order to reduce or discontinue the nasogastric suction", "Monitor the cardiac rhythm for dysrhythmias"], "correct": [2, 3, 4], "rationale": "This is metabolic alkalosis from loss of gastric acid, and the stated goal is to stop the acid loss - turn down or turn off the suction and treat the underlying cause. Hypokalemia accompanies metabolic alkalosis and produces dysrhythmias with tachycardia, so potassium and cardiac rhythm are monitored and fluids and electrolytes, particularly potassium, are replaced. Giving sodium bicarbonate would add more base to a client who already has too much; excess sodium bicarbonate and antacids are themselves listed causes of metabolic alkalosis. Increasing the suction would accelerate the loss of gastric acid that caused the alkalosis in the first place, which is the opposite of the stated goal of stopping the acid loss."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q13", "exam": 3, "topic": "Respiratory acidosis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with COPD has a pH of 7.31, PaCO2 of 58 and SpO2 of 86% on room air. The nurse applies oxygen. Which target should guide titration for this client?", "options": ["An SpO2 of 95% to 100%, the same as for any adult", "An SpO2 above 98% to fully correct the acidosis", "Oxygen should be withheld entirely because it will suppress the respiratory drive", "An SpO2 of 88% to 92%, avoiding levels above 92%"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "For the client with COPD the oxygen target is an SpO2 of 88% to 92%, and the materials specifically caution against exceeding 92% because higher levels reduce the respiratory drive in a client who chronically retains carbon dioxide - which would worsen the respiratory acidosis already present. Applying the general adult target of 95% to 100% ignores that risk, and aiming above 98% magnifies it. Withholding oxygen altogether is the opposite error: this client is hypoxemic at 86%, and oxygen is given as prescribed with careful titration rather than withheld."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q14", "exam": 3, "topic": "Interventions", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client's arterial blood gases show pH 7.26, PaCO2 57, HCO3 24 after abdominal surgery. The client is somnolent with a respiratory rate of 8 and has received several doses of hydromorphone. Which intervention addresses the cause?", "options": ["Encourage the client to breathe into a paper bag", "Increase the intravenous fluid rate", "Administer a dose of a loop diuretic", "Hold further opioid doses and prepare to support ventilation"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "These gases show uncompensated respiratory acidosis, and the respiratory rate of 8 with somnolence after repeated opioid doses identifies opioid-induced respiratory depression as the cause. Because acidosis is a condition rather than a disease, the intervention is to treat the cause: hold the opioid and be prepared to ventilate the client if the muscles fatigue or the drive does not recover. A diuretic treats volume overload and can itself cause metabolic alkalosis. Increasing fluids does nothing for carbon dioxide retention. Rebreathing into a paper bag raises carbon dioxide, which is precisely the wrong direction for a client already retaining it."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q15", "exam": 3, "topic": "Electrolytes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with respiratory alkalosis reports numbness and tingling in the fingers and around the mouth, along with muscle cramps. Which explanation should the nurse give?", "options": ["Alkalosis increases calcium binding to albumin, lowering the free calcium available to tissues", "The symptoms are unrelated to the acid-base disturbance and indicate a stroke", "Alkalosis directly damages peripheral nerves, producing permanent numbness", "Alkalosis causes potassium to move out of the cells, raising the serum potassium"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "When the blood is alkalotic, calcium binds more readily to albumin, which lowers the ionized calcium available to nerve and muscle tissue and produces numbness and tingling of the extremities, hyperreflexia, cramps and in severe cases seizures. Potassium behavior in alkalosis is the reverse of moving out of the cells - it shifts into them, producing hypokalemia, which is why both potassium and calcium are monitored. The nerve symptoms are functional and reverse when the alkalosis is corrected, not from permanent nerve damage. Dismissing these as unrelated and suggesting stroke ignores a well-described and reversible mechanism."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q16", "exam": 3, "topic": "Electrolytes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client in metabolic acidosis from acute kidney failure has a potassium of 6.1 mEq/L and new peaked T waves on the monitor. Which understanding guides the nurse's care?", "options": ["The elevated potassium is unrelated to the acid-base status and reflects laboratory error", "The acidosis will drive potassium into the cells, so the level will fall without intervention", "Peaked T waves are an expected finding in any client with kidney disease", "In acidosis potassium shifts out of the cells, so the rhythm must be monitored closely while the cause is treated"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "In acidosis potassium shifts out of the cells into the serum, and hyperkalemia with resulting dysrhythmias is a listed finding in both respiratory and metabolic acidosis - which is why monitoring potassium, cardiac rhythm and level of consciousness is part of care while the underlying cause is treated, in this case with dialysis for kidney failure. Calling the relationship coincidental or a laboratory error ignores a predictable physiologic shift in a client whose kidneys cannot excrete potassium. Potassium moves into the cells in alkalosis, not acidosis, so the level will not self-correct. Peaked T waves are an ominous conduction finding, never a routine expectation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q17", "exam": 3, "topic": "Respiratory alkalosis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A mechanically ventilated client has these results: pH 7.49, PaCO2 29 mm Hg, HCO3 23 mEq/L. Which intervention should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Administering sodium bicarbonate intravenously", "Collaborating with the team to adjust the ventilator settings", "Increasing the set respiratory rate on the ventilator", "Suctioning the client to clear retained secretions"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "These gases show uncompensated respiratory alkalosis, and improper or wrong mechanical ventilator settings are a listed cause - the client is being ventilated too much and blowing off carbon dioxide. The intervention that addresses the cause is adjusting the ventilator settings. Sodium bicarbonate adds base to a client who is already alkalotic. Increasing the rate would remove even more carbon dioxide and deepen the alkalosis. Suctioning clears secretions, which is an intervention for retained carbon dioxide in respiratory acidosis, not for over-ventilation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q18", "exam": 3, "topic": "Metabolic acidosis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client in shock from sepsis has a pH of 7.19, HCO3 of 14 mEq/L, PaCO2 of 28 mm Hg and an elevated lactate. Which intervention should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Withholding oxygen until the pH normalizes", "Reducing the intravenous fluid rate to limit further acid delivery", "Administration of sodium bicarbonate, since the HCO3 is low and the pH is below 7.2", "Administration of a loop diuretic to clear the lactic acid"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "This is metabolic acidosis from tissue hypoperfusion producing lactic acid, and the stated parameter is that bicarbonate may be used when the HCO3 is low and the pH is below 7.2 - both conditions are met here. The low PaCO2 reflects respiratory compensation. A loop diuretic does not remove lactic acid and would worsen hypoperfusion by reducing volume in a client in shock. Withholding oxygen in a hypoperfused client worsens the tissue hypoxia generating the lactate. Reducing fluids in shock is the opposite of what is needed, since restoring perfusion is what ultimately corrects the acidosis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q19", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG procedure", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is preparing to assist with an arterial blood gas draw. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Transport the sample to the laboratory promptly", "Warm the extremity in hot water for 10 minutes to dilate the artery", "Apply firm pressure to the site after the sample is drawn", "Note the oxygen delivery device and flow rate at the time of the draw", "Perform Allen's test before a radial puncture"], "correct": [0, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Allen's test assesses collateral ulnar flow and must be done before a radial puncture so the hand remains perfused if the radial artery is compromised. Arterial punctures bleed, so firm pressure afterward is essential. Documenting the oxygen device and flow rate is necessary because the PaO2 cannot be interpreted without knowing how much oxygen the client was receiving - the difference between a PaO2 of 55 on room air and on 100% oxygen is enormous. Prompt transport preserves sample accuracy. Immersing the extremity in hot water is not a described step and risks thermal injury."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q20", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse reviews these results: pH 7.44, PaCO2 47 mm Hg, HCO3 41 mEq/L. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": ["Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Normal acid-base balance", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The pH of 7.44 is within the normal range but leans toward the alkalotic side of the 7.40 midpoint, which identifies the primary disorder when compensation is complete. Both the PaCO2 of 47 and the HCO3 of 41 are abnormal, the hallmark of full compensation. Because the pH leans alkalotic and the bicarbonate is markedly elevated, the primary problem is metabolic alkalosis, and the lungs have retained carbon dioxide to bring the pH back into range. Uncompensated would require a normal PaCO2 and an abnormal pH. Respiratory acidosis would make the pH lean acidic. Calling it normal overlooks two substantially abnormal values."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q21", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client has these arterial blood gases: pH 7.26, PaCO2 57 mm Hg, HCO3 34 mEq/L. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": ["Combined respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The pH of 7.26 is acidotic. The PaCO2 of 57 is elevated and matches the acidosis, identifying a respiratory primary problem. The HCO3 of 34 is also abnormal, elevated in the opposite direction, which indicates the kidneys are compensating - but because the pH remains outside the normal range, the compensation is only partial. Full compensation would require the pH to be back within 7.35 to 7.45. Metabolic alkalosis would produce an alkalotic pH, not an acidotic one. A combined acidosis would show a low bicarbonate alongside the high carbon dioxide, whereas here the bicarbonate is elevated, which is compensation rather than a second acid-producing disorder."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q22", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse reviews these results: pH 7.37, PaCO2 39 mm Hg, HCO3 24 mEq/L, PaO2 95 mm Hg. What is the nurse's correct conclusion?", "options": ["Normal acid-base balance with normal oxygenation", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Early metabolic acidosis that will worsen", "Respiratory alkalosis with mild hypoxemia"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Every value falls within its reference range: pH 7.35 to 7.45, PaCO2 35 to 45, HCO3 within the normal range, and PaO2 80 to 100. Acid-base balance is defined as the state when pH, carbon dioxide and bicarbonate are all within range, so this is a normal result. Full compensation would require both the PaCO2 and the HCO3 to be abnormal with a pH that had returned to normal, and neither gas is abnormal here. Nothing in a fully normal panel supports a developing metabolic acidosis. There is no alkalosis, and a PaO2 of 95 is normal rather than hypoxemic."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q23", "exam": 3, "topic": "Respiratory acidosis", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with pneumonia has developed respiratory acidosis. Which nursing interventions address the goal of reducing carbon dioxide and improving oxygenation? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Administer prescribed bronchodilators and mucolytics", "Administer an additional dose of the ordered opioid to reduce the work of breathing", "Encourage incentive spirometry, coughing and deep breathing", "Encourage the client to breathe into a paper bag", "Suction secretions and promote hydration to thin them"], "correct": [0, 2, 4], "rationale": "The goal in respiratory acidosis is to decrease carbon dioxide, increase oxygen and relieve any obstruction. For pneumonia the interventions are incentive spirometry with coughing and deep breathing, suctioning and hydration to mobilize sputum, and bronchodilators, steroids and mucolytics; mechanical ventilation is anticipated if the respiratory muscles fatigue. Opioids are specifically held in respiratory acidosis because they depress ventilation further, which would raise the carbon dioxide the nurse is trying to lower. Rebreathing into a paper bag raises carbon dioxide, which is the intervention for respiratory alkalosis; in a client already retaining carbon dioxide it would deepen the acidosis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q24", "exam": 3, "topic": "Respiratory alkalosis", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which clinical findings would the nurse expect in a client with respiratory alkalosis? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Numbness and tingling of the extremities", "Respiratory rate greater than 20 with increased depth", "Hyperkalemia with peaked T waves on the monitor", "Warm, flushed skin with Kussmaul respirations", "Hypokalemia"], "correct": [0, 1, 4], "rationale": "Respiratory alkalosis results from hyperventilation, so the rate and depth of breathing are increased, typically above 20. Tachycardia with a normal or lowered blood pressure, hypokalemia from potassium shifting into the cells, and numbness and tingling of the extremities from reduced ionized calcium are all expected. Warm flushed skin with Kussmaul respirations belongs to metabolic acidosis - Kussmaul breathing is the compensatory deep, rapid pattern seen when the body is trying to blow off acid, a different disorder entirely. Hyperkalemia belongs to the acidotic disorders, where potassium shifts out of the cells; in alkalosis potassium moves into the cells, which is why hypokalemia is the expected finding."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q25", "exam": 3, "topic": "Prioritization", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "The nurse reviews arterial blood gases for four clients. Which client should the nurse assess first?", "options": ["pH 7.19, PaCO2 60, HCO3 23, PaO2 48", "pH 7.48, PaCO2 32, HCO3 24, PaO2 88", "pH 7.36, PaCO2 50, HCO3 28, PaO2 85", "pH 7.33, PaCO2 47, HCO3 25, PaO2 76"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The first client has a severely acidotic pH with an elevated carbon dioxide and a PaO2 of 48, which is moderate hypoxemia bordering on severe - the combination of profound uncompensated respiratory acidosis and significant hypoxemia makes this the most unstable client, and a low PaO2 is specifically identified as a critical rescue priority. The second client has a mild uncompensated respiratory alkalosis with normal oxygenation. The third shows full compensation with a normal pH and normal PaO2. The fourth has a mildly acidotic pH with only mild hypoxemia. All four warrant attention, but only one has both a critically low pH and threatened oxygenation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q26", "exam": 3, "topic": "ROME method", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A student uses the ROME method to interpret an ABG showing a pH of 7.50 and a PaCO2 of 28. Which reasoning is correct?", "options": ["The pH is up and the PaCO2 is down; opposite arrows indicate a metabolic problem", "The pH and PaCO2 both represent alkalosis, so the arrows are equal and the problem is metabolic", "ROME applies only to bicarbonate, so the PaCO2 cannot be used to classify this result", "The pH is up and the PaCO2 is down; opposite arrows indicate a respiratory problem"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "ROME stands for Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal: when the pH and the PaCO2 move in opposite directions the disorder is respiratory, and when the pH and the HCO3 move in the same direction it is metabolic. Here the pH is elevated while the PaCO2 is reduced - opposite arrows - so this is respiratory alkalosis. Assigning opposite arrows to a metabolic cause reverses the rule. Describing the arrows as equal misreads the data, since one value is above its range and the other below. ROME uses both components, applying the opposite rule to carbon dioxide and the equal rule to bicarbonate."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q27", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a history of heart failure taking a daily loop diuretic has these results: pH 7.53, PaCO2 40 mm Hg, HCO3 32 mEq/L. Which interpretation and likely cause should the nurse identify?", "options": ["Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis, likely related to diuretic therapy", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis, likely related to vomiting", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis, likely related to anxiety", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis, likely related to kidney disease"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The pH of 7.53 is alkalotic and the HCO3 of 32 is elevated and matches it, so this is metabolic alkalosis; the PaCO2 of 40 is normal, so there is no compensation. Diuretics are a listed cause of metabolic alkalosis, and the intervention is to adjust or stop them. It is not respiratory, since the carbon dioxide is normal. Vomiting is also a legitimate cause of metabolic alkalosis, but the interpretation of partial compensation is wrong because that would require an abnormal PaCO2. Metabolic acidosis would produce a low pH and a low bicarbonate, the opposite of these values."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q28", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse reviews these results for a client with worsening COPD: pH 7.30, PaCO2 49 mm Hg, HCO3 25 mEq/L. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": ["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The pH of 7.30 is acidotic and the PaCO2 of 49 is elevated, matching the acidosis, so the problem is respiratory. The HCO3 of 25 falls within the normal range, meaning the kidneys have not yet begun to retain bicarbonate, so the disorder is uncompensated. Partial compensation would require an abnormal bicarbonate. The disorder cannot be metabolic because the bicarbonate is normal while the carbon dioxide is not. Full compensation would require the pH to have returned into the normal range."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q29", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client found unresponsive has these results: pH 7.15, PaCO2 55 mm Hg, HCO3 25 mEq/L, PaO2 82 mm Hg. Which statement about this client is most accurate?", "options": ["The client has severe uncompensated respiratory acidosis without hypoxemia and needs ventilatory support", "The client has a partially compensated disorder and can be monitored", "The client has metabolic acidosis and needs sodium bicarbonate", "The client has adequate acid-base status because the PaO2 is normal"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "A pH of 7.15 with a PaCO2 of 55 and a normal bicarbonate is severe uncompensated respiratory acidosis, meaning the client is not ventilating adequately and needs support of ventilation. The PaO2 of 82 is within the normal 80 to 100 range, so oxygenation is currently preserved - a useful reminder that a normal PaO2 does not mean the client is safe, because the problem here is carbon dioxide removal rather than oxygen uptake. The disorder is not metabolic, since the bicarbonate is normal. Nothing about a pH of 7.15 permits watchful monitoring, and compensation has not begun."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q30", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a chest wall injury has these results: pH 7.27, PaCO2 53 mm Hg, HCO3 25 mEq/L, PaO2 70 mm Hg. Which conclusion should the nurse draw?", "options": ["Uncompensated respiratory acidosis with mild hypoxemia from inadequate chest expansion", "Normal acid-base status with moderate hypoxemia", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis with moderate hypoxemia", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis with mild hypoxemia"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The pH of 7.27 is acidotic with an elevated PaCO2 of 53 that matches it, identifying respiratory acidosis; the normal bicarbonate of 25 means it is uncompensated. The PaO2 of 70 falls in the 60 to 79 range, which is mild hypoxemia. Inadequate chest expansion from chest trauma is a listed cause of respiratory acidosis. Partial compensation would require an abnormal bicarbonate, and a PaO2 of 70 is mild rather than moderate, since moderate begins below 60. The disorder is not metabolic with a normal bicarbonate, and a pH of 7.27 is not normal."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q31", "exam": 3, "topic": "ARDS case", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with frequent ventricular ectopy and worsening pulmonary status has these results on 6 L by nasal cannula: pH 7.30, PaCO2 59 mm Hg, PaO2 82 mm Hg, HCO3 36 mEq/L. How should the nurse interpret these results?", "options": ["Metabolic alkalosis from the elevated bicarbonate as the primary problem", "Respiratory acidosis with a compensating bicarbonate and acceptable oxygenation on supplemental oxygen", "Respiratory alkalosis caused by the client's tachypnea", "Normal gases that require no intervention"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The pH of 7.30 is acidotic and the PaCO2 of 59 is elevated and moves with the acidosis, so the primary disorder is respiratory acidosis; the elevated bicarbonate of 36 is the metabolic system compensating rather than a separate disorder, and because the pH remains abnormal the compensation is incomplete. The PaO2 of 82 is within the normal range on 6 L of oxygen. Treating the high bicarbonate as the primary problem would make the pH alkalotic, which it is not. Respiratory alkalosis would show a low PaCO2. A pH of 7.30 with a PaCO2 of 59 is not normal and requires attention to ventilation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q32", "exam": 3, "topic": "Metabolic acidosis", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which conditions would the nurse expect to cause metabolic acidosis? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Excessive use of bicarbonate-containing antacids", "Prolonged vomiting", "Diabetic ketoacidosis", "Severe prolonged diarrhea", "Acute kidney failure"], "correct": [2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Metabolic acidosis occurs when too much acid is produced or retained or when base is lost. Diabetic ketoacidosis and kidney failure cause acid retention and production; severe diarrhea causes loss of bicarbonate-rich intestinal contents; and shock produces lactic acid through tissue hypoperfusion. Starvation, hypoxia, seizures and salicylate intoxication belong on the same list. Prolonged vomiting removes gastric acid and causes metabolic alkalosis - the opposite disorder - which is why the direction of gastrointestinal loss matters so much in interpreting these clients. Excess sodium bicarbonate and antacids add base rather than acid, which causes metabolic alkalosis - the mirror image of the disorder in question."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q33", "exam": 3, "topic": "Metabolic acidosis", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which assessment findings would the nurse expect in a client with metabolic acidosis? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Tingling of the fingers with hyperreflexia and cramps", "Decreased blood pressure", "Hypokalemia with muscle cramping", "Hyperkalemia", "Kussmaul respirations"], "correct": [1, 3, 4], "rationale": "Metabolic acidosis produces Kussmaul respirations as the lungs attempt to blow off carbon dioxide, hyperkalemia as potassium shifts out of the cells, a falling blood pressure, warm flushed skin, headache with changes in level of consciousness, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and muscle twitching. Tingling of the fingers with hyperreflexia and cramps belongs to the alkalotic disorders, where reduced ionized calcium produces neuromuscular irritability - the opposite acid-base state. Hypokalemia accompanies metabolic alkalosis, not acidosis; in acidosis potassium shifts out of the cells and the client becomes hyperkalemic."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q34", "exam": 3, "topic": "Metabolic alkalosis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client who takes large quantities of antacids daily for reflux is admitted with confusion, dizziness and tremors. Laboratory results show pH 7.51 and HCO3 30 mEq/L. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": ["Review the client's antacid use, because excess bicarbonate-containing antacids cause metabolic alkalosis", "Administer an additional antacid dose to settle the client's stomach", "Prepare the client for emergency intubation", "Encourage the client to continue the antacids, since the symptoms are unrelated"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Excess sodium bicarbonate and antacids are listed causes of metabolic alkalosis, which produces restlessness progressing to lethargy, confusion, dizziness, tremors, cramps and tingling. Reviewing and addressing the source of the excess base is the intervention aimed at the cause. Telling the client the symptoms are unrelated misses the connection the laboratory values support. Giving more antacid would worsen the alkalosis. Intubation treats a ventilatory problem; this client's disturbance is metabolic, and nothing described indicates airway compromise."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q35", "exam": 3, "topic": "Respiratory acidosis", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which assessment findings would the nurse expect in a client with respiratory acidosis? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Numbness and tingling around the mouth and in the fingers", "Respiratory rate greater than 20 with increased depth", "Muscle weakness and hyperreflexia", "Drowsiness, dizziness and disorientation", "Headache with pallor progressing to cyanosis"], "correct": [2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Respiratory acidosis reflects retained carbon dioxide with inadequate oxygen, producing drowsiness, dizziness and disorientation, headache, pallor progressing to cyanosis, muscle weakness and hyperreflexia, and hyperkalemia with dysrhythmias as potassium shifts out of the cells. Blood pressure falls and the client becomes tachycardic. The breathing pattern is typically rapid and shallow, and may be slower than 12 per minute when the client is depressed by opioids or anesthesia - a rate above 20 with increased depth describes hyperventilation, which produces respiratory alkalosis instead. Perioral and digital numbness reflects the reduced ionized calcium of an alkalotic state, so it belongs to respiratory alkalosis rather than acidosis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q36", "exam": 3, "topic": "Causes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client who ingested a large quantity of aspirin is brought to the emergency department breathing rapidly and deeply. Which acid-base disturbance should the nurse anticipate first?", "options": ["Metabolic alkalosis from the salicylate acting as a base", "Respiratory acidosis from salicylate-induced respiratory depression", "No acid-base disturbance, since salicylates are not absorbed systemically", "Respiratory alkalosis from salicylate-induced hyperventilation"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Aspirin and salicylate toxicity is specifically listed as a cause of respiratory alkalosis, because salicylates stimulate the respiratory center and the client hyperventilates, blowing off carbon dioxide - which fits the rapid, deep breathing described. Salicylate intoxication is also listed as a cause of metabolic acidosis, so the client may develop a mixed picture, but respiratory alkalosis is what the hyperventilation produces first. Salicylates do not depress respirations, and they are acids rather than bases. They are absorbed readily and cause systemic toxicity."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q37", "exam": 3, "topic": "Causes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A hiker who ascended rapidly to a high altitude reports light-headedness and tingling in the hands. Which acid-base change explains these findings?", "options": ["Respiratory acidosis from reduced atmospheric pressure trapping carbon dioxide", "Metabolic alkalosis from dehydration at altitude", "Metabolic acidosis from increased exercise-related lactic acid only", "Respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation in response to lower oxygen availability"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "High altitude is a listed cause of respiratory alkalosis: lower available oxygen drives an increase in rate and depth of breathing, which blows off carbon dioxide and raises the pH, producing light-headedness and the tingling that comes from reduced ionized calcium. Reduced atmospheric pressure does not trap carbon dioxide; the client is ventilating more, not less. Lactic acid from exertion can contribute to a metabolic acidosis but does not explain the tingling and would not be the primary change in a hiker who has just ascended. Dehydration is a listed cause of metabolic alkalosis but does not account for the hyperventilation pattern described."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q38", "exam": 3, "topic": "Interventions", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with end-stage kidney disease has a pH of 7.24 and HCO3 of 15 mEq/L. Which intervention most directly addresses the cause of this acid-base disturbance?", "options": ["Administering an antiemetic", "Arranging for dialysis", "Increasing the oxygen flow rate", "Teaching pursed-lip breathing"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Because acidosis is a condition rather than a disease, treatment targets the cause. In kidney failure the kidneys cannot excrete hydrogen ions or regenerate bicarbonate, so dialysis is the intervention identified for metabolic acidosis in this population. An antiemetic treats nausea, which is a symptom of uremia but does not correct the acid load. Oxygen addresses hypoxemia rather than metabolic acid retention. Pursed-lip breathing is a technique for obstructive lung disease and does nothing for a kidney-driven metabolic acidosis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q39", "exam": 3, "topic": "Oxygenation priority", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "A nurse reviews an ABG and notes an unremarkable pH and PaCO2 but a PaO2 of 52 mm Hg. The nurse recognizes that the most important implication of this result is that:", "options": ["The value reflects peripheral rather than arterial oxygenation and may be inaccurate", "The result can be considered normal because the acid-base balance is intact", "The PaO2 should be rechecked in four hours before any action is taken", "The client may need oxygen now, because a low PaO2 is a critical rescue priority"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "PaO2 reflects whether the blood is actually carrying enough dissolved oxygen, and the materials stress checking it first and recognizing that a low value signals the client may need oxygen immediately - it is described as a critical rescue priority. A PaO2 of 52 is moderate hypoxemia regardless of a normal pH, so the gas cannot be dismissed as normal. Waiting four hours leaves a hypoxemic client untreated. PaO2 is specifically the arterial, or true, oxygenation value; SaO2 is the measure that reflects peripheral oxygenation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q40", "exam": 3, "topic": "Physiology", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse explains to a student why carbon dioxide is considered an acid. Which explanation is correct?", "options": ["Carbon dioxide combines with water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates into hydrogen ions and bicarbonate", "Carbon dioxide is converted to lactic acid in the alveoli", "Carbon dioxide lowers the pH only when the kidneys have already failed", "Carbon dioxide binds directly to bicarbonate and neutralizes it in the kidneys"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Carbon dioxide combines with water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates into hydrogen ions and bicarbonate - the reversible reaction that makes carbon dioxide behave as an acid and bicarbonate as the base. This is why retaining carbon dioxide lowers the pH and blowing it off raises the pH. Carbon dioxide does not neutralize bicarbonate in the kidneys; the kidneys control bicarbonate independently by secretion, absorption and reabsorption. Lactic acid is produced by anaerobic tissue metabolism, not by conversion of carbon dioxide in the alveoli. Carbon dioxide lowers pH whenever it accumulates, regardless of kidney function."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q41", "exam": 3, "topic": "Monitoring", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which parameters should the nurse monitor in any client with an acid-base imbalance? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Serum electrolytes", "Daily pulmonary function testing", "Cardiac rhythm", "Serial arterial blood gases", "Fluid volume status"], "correct": [0, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Monitoring for all acid-base conditions includes arterial blood gases, electrolytes, fluid volume status, cardiac rhythm, vital signs, respiratory rate and effort, and response to treatment. Electrolytes and rhythm matter because potassium and calcium shift predictably with pH and produce dysrhythmias. Daily pulmonary function testing is not part of acid-base monitoring; those studies evaluate lung volumes and airflow in chronic respiratory disease rather than tracking an acute acid-base disturbance."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q42", "exam": 3, "topic": "Interventions", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with metabolic acidosis caused by severe diarrhea is being treated. Which combination of interventions is most appropriate?", "options": ["Begin continuous nasogastric suction to rest the bowel", "Place the client on a non-rebreather mask and restrict potassium", "Administer a loop diuretic and restrict oral fluids", "Treat the diarrhea, rehydrate the client, and give antimicrobial therapy if indicated"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "When metabolic acidosis is caused by diarrhea, the plan is to treat the diarrhea, replace the fluid and electrolytes lost, and give antimicrobial therapy when an infectious cause is present. A diuretic with fluid restriction would deepen the volume deficit in a client already losing fluid, and diuretics are themselves a cause of metabolic alkalosis. A non-rebreather addresses oxygenation, which is not the identified problem, and potassium restriction is risky given the large gastrointestinal losses. Nasogastric suction removes gastric acid and would push the client toward metabolic alkalosis while adding another route of fluid loss."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q43", "exam": 3, "topic": "Compensation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse is determining whether a client's acid-base disorder is fully compensated. Which finding confirms full compensation?", "options": ["All three values have returned to within their normal ranges", "The pH remains abnormal while both the PaCO2 and HCO3 are abnormal", "The pH has returned within 7.35 to 7.45 while both the PaCO2 and HCO3 remain abnormal", "The pH is abnormal and only one of the two components is abnormal"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Full compensation means the opposing system has succeeded in bringing the pH back into the normal range, even though both the respiratory and metabolic components remain abnormal because the underlying problem persists. A pH that is still abnormal with both components abnormal is partial compensation. An abnormal pH with only one abnormal component is uncompensated. If all three values were normal, there would be no disorder to compensate for - that is simply acid-base balance."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q44", "exam": 3, "topic": "Interpretation strategy", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse is working through the four-step approach to ABG interpretation. After determining that the pH is acidotic, what is the next step?", "options": ["Identify the underlying disease process causing the abnormality", "Determine whether hypoxemia is present by checking the PaO2", "Examine the PaCO2 and HCO3 to determine whether the problem is respiratory or metabolic", "Decide whether compensation has occurred"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The four steps are: determine whether the client is acidotic or alkalotic from the pH; identify whether the problem is respiratory or metabolic by examining the PaCO2 and HCO3, with the value that matches the pH indicating the primary problem; determine whether compensation is occurring by seeing whether the other value is abnormal; and finally assess for hypoxemia using the PaO2. Checking the PaO2 is the fourth step rather than the second. Compensation is assessed in step three, after the primary problem has been classified. Identifying the disease process follows interpretation and guides treatment, but it is not one of the four interpretive steps."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q45", "exam": 3, "topic": "Nursing judgment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "A client with respiratory alkalosis from acute anxiety has a PaO2 of 92 mm Hg and SpO2 of 98%. The nurse coaches the client to slow their breathing and offers a rebreathing technique. Which assessment finding would indicate the intervention should be stopped?", "options": ["The client reports the tingling in their fingers is improving", "The SpO2 falls to 88% and the client becomes more dyspneic", "The respiratory rate decreases from 34 to 24", "The client is able to speak in full sentences"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Rebreathing techniques raise carbon dioxide to correct hyperventilation, and they are appropriate only when the client is not hypoxemic - which is why this client's normal PaO2 and SpO2 justified the intervention. A falling saturation with increasing dyspnea means oxygenation is now compromised, so the technique must be stopped and oxygenation addressed. A respiratory rate falling from 34 to 24, improving paresthesias, and the return of full-sentence speech are all signs that the intervention is working and should continue."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q46", "exam": 3, "topic": "ABG interpretation", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client has these results: pH 7.30, PaCO2 34 mm Hg, HCO3 16 mEq/L, PaO2 55 mm Hg. Which statements about this ABG are accurate? Select all that apply.", "options": ["The lungs are compensating", "The primary disorder is metabolic", "The disorder is uncompensated", "Moderate hypoxemia is present", "The client is acidotic"], "correct": [0, 1, 3, 4], "rationale": "The pH of 7.30 is below 7.35, so the client is acidotic. The HCO3 of 16 is low and matches the acidosis, identifying a metabolic primary problem. The PaCO2 of 34 is below the normal range, moving in the opposite direction, which is the lungs compensating by blowing off carbon dioxide - and because a component other than the primary one is abnormal, the disorder is partially compensated rather than uncompensated. The PaO2 of 55 falls in the 40 to 59 range, which is moderate hypoxemia."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q47", "exam": 3, "topic": "Causes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with Guillain-Barre syndrome has progressive weakness and a declining vital capacity. Which acid-base disturbance should the nurse monitor for most closely?", "options": ["Metabolic alkalosis from immobility", "Metabolic acidosis from reduced activity", "Respiratory alkalosis from compensatory hyperventilation", "Respiratory acidosis from respiratory muscle fatigue"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Neuromuscular disease weakens the respiratory muscles, so ventilation becomes inadequate and carbon dioxide is retained, producing respiratory acidosis - muscle fatigue is specifically listed among its causes, and mechanical ventilation is the intervention when the muscles can no longer do the work. Compensatory hyperventilation is not possible in a client whose muscles are failing. Immobility does not produce metabolic alkalosis, which comes from acid loss or excess base. Reduced activity does not generate the acid load required for metabolic acidosis; the threat in this client is ventilatory."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q48", "exam": 3, "topic": "Interventions", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a nasogastric tube to continuous suction develops metabolic alkalosis. Besides addressing the suction, which electrolyte abnormality should the nurse anticipate and monitor?", "options": ["Hypermagnesemia", "Hypernatremia", "Hypokalemia", "Hyperkalemia"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Metabolic alkalosis is accompanied by hypokalemia, which produces dysrhythmias and tachycardia, so potassium is monitored and replaced along with fluids while the source of acid loss is stopped. Hyperkalemia accompanies acidosis, where potassium shifts out of the cells, not alkalosis, where it shifts in. Sodium and magnesium disturbances can occur with prolonged gastrointestinal losses, but potassium is the electrolyte specifically paired with metabolic alkalosis in both the laboratory findings and the replacement plan."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q49", "exam": 3, "topic": "Clinical judgment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "A client with pneumonia had a pH of 7.28 and PaCO2 of 58 this morning. After four hours of bronchodilators, incentive spirometry and suctioning, repeat gases show pH 7.36 and PaCO2 of 44. How should the nurse evaluate the plan of care?", "options": ["The interventions are effective and should be continued", "The interventions have failed and mechanical ventilation should be prepared", "The improvement reflects renal compensation rather than the interventions", "The results indicate a new respiratory alkalosis has developed"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The pH has moved from acidotic back into the normal range and the PaCO2 has returned to normal, which means the client is now ventilating adequately and clearing carbon dioxide - the goal of reducing carbon dioxide and improving oxygenation has been met, so the interventions are working and continue. Preparing for mechanical ventilation would be indicated if the client were fatiguing and the carbon dioxide rising, which is the opposite of this trend. Renal compensation occurs over hours to days through bicarbonate retention and would not normalize the PaCO2, which is a respiratory value. A pH of 7.36 with a PaCO2 of 44 is normal, not alkalotic."},
  {"id": "n504e2s3q50", "exam": 3, "topic": "Assessment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A postoperative client has a respiratory rate of 10 and shallow respirations after receiving anesthesia and opioids. The nurse recognizes that if this pattern continues, the client is at greatest risk for which problem?", "options": ["Blowing off carbon dioxide and developing respiratory alkalosis", "Retaining bicarbonate and developing metabolic alkalosis", "Retaining carbon dioxide and developing respiratory acidosis", "Losing bicarbonate and developing metabolic acidosis"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Hypoventilation is the mechanism of respiratory acidosis, and respiratory depression from anesthesia and opioids is among the listed causes; a rate of 10 with shallow breaths means carbon dioxide is not being adequately exhaled. Blowing off carbon dioxide requires an increased rate and depth, which is the opposite of this client's pattern. Bicarbonate losses come from the gastrointestinal tract or the kidneys and are not produced by shallow breathing. Bicarbonate retention occurs as compensation over hours to days, and would be a response to the acidosis rather than the primary risk."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q01", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS recognition", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse is caring for a client being treated for aspiration pneumonia who is receiving oxygen by 100% non-rebreather mask. Which finding is the hallmark indicating the client is developing acute respiratory distress syndrome?", "options": ["The blood pressure is 180/96", "The client is experiencing bradypnea", "The client is tired and confused", "The PaO2 remains at 45 mm Hg"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The hallmark of ARDS is refractory hypoxemia - a PaO2 that does not improve despite oxygen administration and increasing oxygen concentration. A PaO2 stuck at 45 on a 100% non-rebreather is exactly that picture, and it is the finding that distinguishes ARDS from other causes of hypoxemia. Fatigue and confusion reflect a change in level of consciousness, which occurs with hypoxemia generally and is an early sign of acute respiratory failure but is not specific to ARDS. Bradypnea is the wrong direction entirely - ARDS produces tachypnea with accessory muscle use. An elevated blood pressure is nonspecific and is not among the listed signs."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q02", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF classification", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client has these arterial blood gases: PaO2 54 mm Hg, PaCO2 38 mm Hg, pH 7.37. How should the nurse classify this client's respiratory failure?", "options": ["Combined respiratory failure involving both oxygenation and ventilation", "The client does not meet criteria for respiratory failure", "Type II, hypercapnic respiratory failure - a ventilation problem", "Type I, hypoxemic respiratory failure - an oxygenation problem"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Type I, hypoxemic respiratory failure is defined by a PaO2 below 60 with a normal or low PaCO2, and reflects impaired gas exchange at the alveolar-capillary membrane - an oxygenation problem. This client's PaO2 of 54 is below 60 while the PaCO2 of 38 and pH of 7.37 are both normal. Type II requires a PaCO2 above 50 occurring with acidemia, a pH below 7.35, neither of which is present. Combined failure requires both a PaO2 under 60 and a PaCO2 over 50 with acidemia. The PaO2 of 54 clearly meets the threshold, so saying the criteria are unmet is incorrect."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q03", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF recognition", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client admitted with pneumonia has been alert and oriented. The nurse now finds the client restless and agitated, picking at the bedding, with a respiratory rate of 30 and SpO2 of 90% on 4 L nasal cannula. What is the nurse's priority interpretation?", "options": ["The agitation is most likely a medication side effect that should be reported in the morning", "The change in level of consciousness is the first sign of acute respiratory failure", "The client is experiencing situational anxiety from the hospital admission", "The findings are expected with pneumonia and warrant continued observation"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Confusion and change in level of consciousness are identified as the first sign of acute respiratory failure, and the materials direct the nurse to always assess for restlessness and agitation first. Combined with a respiratory rate of 30 and a falling saturation on supplemental oxygen, this client is decompensating now. Deferring the finding to morning as a medication effect delays recognition of failing gas exchange. Attributing it to situational anxiety ignores the objective respiratory abnormalities. Calling tachypnea with a saturation of 90% on oxygen an expected finding misses that compensatory mechanisms are failing, which is the definition of respiratory failure."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q04", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS pathophysiology", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse explains why clients with ARDS have stiff, non-compliant lungs. Which sequence is correct?", "options": ["Bronchial smooth muscle constricts, airways narrow, and air becomes trapped behind the obstruction", "Chronic inflammation thickens the bronchial walls and increases mucus production over years", "Inflammation makes capillaries leaky, protein-rich fluid floods the alveoli, surfactant is destroyed, and a hyaline membrane forms", "Blood clots lodge in the pulmonary arteries, so alveoli are ventilated but not perfused"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "In ARDS, inflammation increases capillary permeability so protein-rich fluid leaks into the alveoli, producing pulmonary edema; surfactant is destroyed so alveoli collapse into atelectasis; and a hyaline membrane forms, leaving the lungs stiff and non-compliant with refractory hypoxemia. Bronchoconstriction with air trapping describes obstructive disease such as asthma, where the problem is airflow rather than alveolar flooding. Clot obstruction with ventilated but unperfused alveoli describes pulmonary embolism, a perfusion problem. Thickened bronchial walls with excess mucus over years describes chronic bronchitis, not the acute diffuse alveolar damage of ARDS."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q05", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS risk factors", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client involved in a severe motor vehicle crash received 36 units of packed red blood cells, 20 units of platelets, 12 units of fresh frozen plasma and 18 L of lactated Ringer's in the operating room, and sustained bilateral flail chest, a right hemothorax and pneumothorax, a fractured spleen and liver lacerations. Which factors in this history increase the client's risk for ARDS? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Shock from the injuries", "Scheduled postoperative pain control", "Multiple blood transfusions", "Extensive chest trauma", "The client's age of 56"], "correct": [0, 2, 3], "rationale": "Massive transfusion, chest trauma and shock states are all listed triggers of the inflammatory cascade that causes ARDS, and this client has all three. Scheduled pain control is appropriate care, not a risk factor; adequate analgesia actually supports deep breathing and helps prevent atelectasis. Although age is named among ARDS risk factors in general, being 56 is not in itself the feature that places this particular client at risk - the transfusions, trauma and shock are the overwhelming contributors here, and selecting age would mean treating an unremarkable adult age as equivalent to 36 units of blood."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q06", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS diagnosis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which combination of findings would support a diagnosis of ARDS in a client following major trauma?", "options": ["Unilateral consolidation on chest x-ray with purulent sputum and a PAWP of 24 mm Hg", "Clear chest x-ray with a PaO2 of 90 on room air and a normal echocardiogram", "Bilateral infiltrates with a PAWP of 26 mm Hg and an ejection fraction of 25%", "Bilateral infiltrates on chest x-ray without fluid overload, a PaO2 to FiO2 ratio below 200, and a PAWP of 16 mm Hg"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "ARDS is diagnosed by bilateral infiltrates on chest x-ray in the absence of fluid overload, a PaO2 to FiO2 ratio less than 200 regardless of PEEP, and a pulmonary artery wedge pressure of 18 mm Hg or less with no evidence of heart failure - the low wedge pressure is what distinguishes it from cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Unilateral consolidation with purulent sputum describes pneumonia. A clear film with normal oxygenation excludes ARDS entirely. Bilateral infiltrates with a wedge pressure of 26 and an ejection fraction of 25% point to cardiogenic pulmonary edema, since both the elevated wedge pressure and the poor ejection fraction indicate heart failure as the cause."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q07", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS phases", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is three weeks out from the lung injury that caused ARDS. The lung is remodeled by collagenous and fibrous tissue, compliance is decreased, and hypoxemia persists. Which phase does this describe?", "options": ["Proliferative phase", "Fibrotic phase", "Exudative phase", "Resolution phase"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The fibrotic phase occurs after about two to three weeks, when diffuse scarring and fibrosis reduce lung compliance and the surface area available for gas exchange, so hypoxemia continues and pulmonary hypertension may develop from vascular destruction - the prognosis in this phase is poor. The exudative phase occurs roughly within the first 24 hours to day 7 and is the leaky, edematous stage with refractory hypoxemia and bilateral infiltrates. The proliferative phase spans about days 7 to 21, when the lung attempts repair and may improve or progress to fibrosis. A resolution phase is not among the three phases described."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q08", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS management", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with ARDS has a PaO2 of 54 on an FiO2 of 0.80. The provider wants to improve oxygenation without raising the FiO2 further. Which intervention should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Increasing the tidal volume to 12 mL/kg", "Decreasing the PEEP to reduce intrathoracic pressure", "Administering a bronchodilator by nebulizer", "Placing the client in the prone position"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Prone positioning is specifically identified as a way to increase oxygenation without increasing the FiO2, which is valuable because high oxygen concentrations are themselves injurious. Increasing the tidal volume raises airway pressures and the risk of barotrauma in lungs that are already stiff and non-compliant. A bronchodilator treats bronchoconstriction, which is not the problem in ARDS - the alveoli are flooded and collapsed, not the airways narrowed. Decreasing PEEP would be counterproductive, since PEEP is what holds the alveoli open during expiration; in ARDS it is typically run at 10 to 20 cm H2O for that reason."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q09", "exam": 4, "topic": "PEEP", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with ARDS has the PEEP increased from 10 to 18 cm H2O. Thirty minutes later the blood pressure falls from 118/70 to 84/50 and the heart rate rises to 118. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": ["The PEEP change has caused an immediate drop in the PaO2", "The client is developing sepsis unrelated to the ventilator change", "The higher PEEP has increased intrathoracic pressure, reducing venous return and cardiac output", "The client is having an allergic reaction to the sedation"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "PEEP keeps alveoli open during expiration and improves oxygenation, but it also raises intrathoracic pressure; too much PEEP causes barotrauma and lowers the blood pressure by impeding venous return and therefore cardiac output. A hypotensive episode appearing within half an hour of a substantial PEEP increase points directly to that mechanism. Sepsis is possible in any critically ill client but would not be expected to coincide so precisely with the ventilator change. Raising PEEP generally improves rather than lowers the PaO2 in ARDS. An allergic reaction to sedation would typically involve other features and is not suggested by an isolated fall in pressure after a known hemodynamic intervention."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q10", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS nursing care", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which nursing interventions are appropriate for a client with ARDS on mechanical ventilation? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Perform chest percussion every hour to clear the alveolar fluid", "Use prone positioning as prescribed", "Provide pulmonary toilet", "Wean the client to room air as soon as the PaO2 reaches 60", "Perform a focused respiratory assessment and provide ventilator care"], "correct": [1, 2, 4], "rationale": "Nursing care in ARDS centers on focused respiratory assessment with mechanical ventilation care, pulmonary toilet, clustering care so the client gets uninterrupted rest, and prone positioning to improve oxygenation without raising the FiO2. Weaning to room air once the PaO2 reaches 60 would be unsafe and premature: a PaO2 of 60 sits at the bottom of the mild hypoxemia range, the lungs remain non-compliant, and weaning proceeds only when the established parameters - adequate oxygenation and ventilation, breathing over the tube, ability to maintain an airway and a stable neurologic exam - are met. Hourly chest percussion is not the answer either: the fluid in ARDS is protein-rich edema inside flooded alveoli from leaky capillaries, which percussion cannot mobilize, and the constant handling works against the clustered care these clients need."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q11", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF management", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with acute respiratory failure is deteriorating despite a Venturi mask. Which sequence of escalating oxygen and ventilatory support should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Immediate tracheostomy, then mechanical ventilation, then CPAP", "CPAP, then nasal cannula, then non-rebreather mask", "Non-rebreather mask, then bag-mask ventilation, then BiPAP or CPAP, then mechanical ventilation", "Nasal cannula, then room air trial, then tracheostomy"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The escalation described for acute respiratory failure moves from the Venturi mask to a non-rebreather, then bag-mask ventilation, then BiPAP or CPAP, and finally mechanical ventilation as the client's needs increase. Moving a deteriorating client down to a nasal cannula or to room air withdraws support when more is needed, and tracheostomy is not the next step - it is considered for long-term airway management after roughly 10 to 14 days of intubation. Starting with tracheostomy inverts the entire progression. Listing CPAP ahead of a nasal cannula and a non-rebreather puts the therapies in descending rather than ascending order of support."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q12", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF nursing care", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client in acute respiratory failure is dyspneic and anxious. Which nursing interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Allow the client to lean forward over pillows in a tripod position", "Encourage the client to lie flat to conserve energy", "Place the client in high Fowler's position", "Restrict all oral intake and withhold nutritional support", "Cluster care and provide frequent rest periods"], "correct": [0, 2, 4], "rationale": "High Fowler's position maximizes chest expansion, tripod positioning over pillows helps open the alveoli and is actively encouraged, and clustering care with frequent rest periods conserves the energy of a client whose work of breathing is high. Lying flat reduces chest expansion and worsens dyspnea, which is why the upright positions are used. Withholding nutrition is also wrong: nutritional support is a listed intervention, because respiratory muscles require energy and malnutrition contributes to ventilatory failure and is a risk factor for ventilator-associated pneumonia."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q13", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF causes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with a heroin overdose has a respiratory rate of 6, PaCO2 of 68 and PaO2 of 55. The nurse identifies the cause of this client's respiratory failure as:", "options": ["An extra-pulmonary cause - central nervous system depression reducing respiratory drive", "A pulmonary cause - upper airway obstruction", "A pulmonary cause - alveolar abnormality impairing diffusion", "An extra-pulmonary cause - a chest wall disorder restricting expansion"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Extra-pulmonary causes of respiratory failure include central nervous system depression, which reduces the drive to breathe; drug overdose is specifically listed, and a respiratory rate of 6 with carbon dioxide retention is the signature. The lungs themselves are structurally normal, so this is not an alveolar or diffusion problem such as pneumonia, pulmonary edema or ARDS. There is no obstruction described, and obstruction typically produces stridor or absent air movement rather than a slow regular rate. Chest wall disorders such as rib fractures and kyphoscoliosis restrict expansion mechanically, which is not the mechanism in an opioid overdose."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q14", "exam": 4, "topic": "COVID-19", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with COVID-19 pneumonia has a decreasing SpO2 despite increasing oxygen, and arterial blood gases confirm hypoxemia with respiratory acidosis. Which order should the nurse identify as most appropriate?", "options": ["Assess respiratory status every 4 hours", "Initiate high-flow nasal cannula oxygen at 60 L/min", "Begin broad-spectrum antibiotics for the viral infection", "Prepare immediately for intubation before trying noninvasive support"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "High-flow nasal cannula is a noninvasive, preferred method of providing high levels of oxygen without placing the client on mechanical ventilation, delivering 60 or more liters of warmed, humidified oxygen, and it is appropriate for this client. Assessing every 4 hours is too infrequent; respiratory status should be checked every 1 to 2 hours because changes in the lung can occur quickly. Antibiotics treat bacterial infections and would be ineffective against a viral lung infection. Intubation should not occur at this point because noninvasive methods such as high-flow nasal cannula have not yet been tried."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q15", "exam": 4, "topic": "COVID-19", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is planning care for a client with COVID-19 pneumonia and worsening oxygenation. Which intervention should be included to improve oxygenation?", "options": ["Maintain strict supine positioning to prevent dislodging the oxygen device", "Encourage vigorous chest percussion every 2 hours", "Schedule awake pronation periods", "Limit position changes to once per shift"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Research supports that awake pronation can improve oxygenation in clients with COVID-19, and scheduling pronation periods is identified as an appropriate nursing action. Strict supine positioning works against that benefit and promotes atelectasis in dependent lung regions. Limiting position changes to once per shift contradicts both pronation and the general practice of turning clients every 2 hours. Vigorous chest percussion every 2 hours is not among the appropriate actions for this client, whose problem is diffuse inflammatory lung injury rather than retained secretions requiring percussion."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q16", "exam": 4, "topic": "COVID-19", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with COVID-19 has a fever, frequent cough, crackles, worsening shortness of breath, chest infiltrates and an SpO2 of 92% on room air. Which conclusion is best supported by these findings?", "options": ["The client has asthma triggered by the infection", "The client has viral pneumonia resulting from the COVID-19 infection", "The client has a pulmonary embolism", "The client has acute respiratory distress syndrome"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Chest infiltrates, fever, crackles, shortness of breath and a low SpO2 together support pneumonia caused by the virus - note that the normal saturation range is 95% or greater, so 92% is low. Pulmonary embolism is not supported by this cluster of findings, which point to a parenchymal infectious process rather than an acute perfusion defect. ARDS requires refractory hypoxemia that does not improve with oxygen; this client's saturation of 92% on room air has not yet demonstrated that, although the priority is to prevent progression to acute lung injury. Nothing in the findings indicates asthma, which would produce wheezing and airflow obstruction."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q17", "exam": 4, "topic": "COVID-19", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "The nurse compares a client's current findings to those on admission: crackles now in both bases rather than throughout all lung fields, respiratory rate 20 rather than 32, SpO2 90% on room air rather than 96%, and chest x-ray showing 20% of lung fields with few infiltrates rather than 50% with diffuse infiltrates. How should the nurse evaluate the plan of care?", "options": ["The interventions have failed because the oxygen saturation has fallen", "The client has returned to baseline and can be discharged", "No conclusion can be drawn without a repeat arterial blood gas", "The interventions have been largely effective, although the oxygen saturation warrants continued attention"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Crackles have receded from all lung fields to the bases, the respiratory rate has normalized from 32 to 20, and the chest x-ray has improved from 50% involvement with diffuse infiltrates to 20% with few infiltrates - these show the nursing and collaborative actions were effective in meeting optimal clinical outcomes. The falling saturation from 96% to 90% is the one parameter that has not improved and requires follow-up, so it tempers the evaluation without negating the overall improvement. Concluding the plan failed ignores four parameters that improved. The client is not at baseline with a saturation of 90% and residual infiltrates. Waiting for an ABG before drawing any conclusion disregards the substantial clinical and radiographic evidence already available."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q18", "exam": 4, "topic": "PAH", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client reports syncope, dyspnea at rest, substernal chest pain and fatigue. Assessment reveals jugular vein distention, peripheral edema, ascites and liver engorgement. Which condition do these findings most support?", "options": ["Pulmonary artery hypertension with right heart failure", "Left-sided heart failure with pulmonary edema", "Acute respiratory distress syndrome", "A large pleural effusion"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "In pulmonary artery hypertension the pulmonary arteries tighten and stiffen, so the right side of the heart must work harder, enlarges, and eventually fails. The listed signs and symptoms are syncope, weakness, dyspnea at rest, fatigue, liver engorgement, substernal chest pain, right heart failure, jugular vein distention, peripheral edema and ascites - which is precisely this cluster. Left-sided failure produces pulmonary congestion with crackles and orthopnea rather than this systemic venous congestion. ARDS presents with refractory hypoxemia and bilateral infiltrates, not ascites and liver engorgement. A pleural effusion causes dyspnea with dullness and diminished sounds but does not produce right heart failure findings."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q19", "exam": 4, "topic": "PAH", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client is being evaluated for pulmonary artery hypertension. Which diagnostic test is identified as the definitive study for this condition?", "options": ["Brain natriuretic peptide level", "Swan-Ganz right heart catheterization", "Pulmonary function testing", "Chest computed tomography"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Pulmonary artery hypertension is defined by a mean pulmonary artery pressure greater than 25 mm Hg at rest, and the Swan-Ganz or right heart catheter is the study that directly measures that pressure - it is a quadruple-lumen catheter with a thermodilution sensor attached to a pressure transducer that determines central venous, right atrial, right ventricular and pulmonary artery pressures. Pulmonary function testing, chest CT, echocardiogram, arterial blood gases and BNP are all part of the diagnostic workup, but none of them measures the pulmonary artery pressure that defines the diagnosis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q20", "exam": 4, "topic": "PAH", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client newly diagnosed with pulmonary artery hypertension asks the nurse, \"Will the medication cure this?\" Which response is most accurate?", "options": ["\"Yes, as long as you also take a diuretic and digoxin daily\"", "\"Yes, the prostanoids reverse the narrowing permanently\"", "\"These medications manage the disease; lung transplant is the only cure\"", "\"There is no treatment available for this condition\""], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Pulmonary artery hypertension is described as a chronic, incurable disease, and lung transplant is identified as the only cure. Medical management with prostanoids such as epoprostenol, treprostinil and iloprost, along with diuretics, nitric oxide, oxygen, anticoagulation and digoxin, controls symptoms and slows progression but does not reverse the disease. Telling the client the prostanoids permanently reverse the narrowing or that the combination of a diuretic and digoxin constitutes a cure would be false reassurance. Saying there is no treatment is equally wrong and would leave the client hopeless when effective management exists."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q21", "exam": 4, "topic": "PAH", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with pulmonary artery hypertension is prescribed epoprostenol. The nurse teaching the client explains that this medication works by:", "options": ["Increasing the force of myocardial contraction to overcome the resistance", "Removing excess fluid to decrease the workload on the right ventricle", "Relaxing vascular smooth muscle and inhibiting smooth muscle cell growth", "Preventing clot formation in the pulmonary arteries"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Prostanoids such as epoprostenol, treprostinil and iloprost relax vascular smooth muscle by stimulating production of adenosine monophosphate and inhibit the growth of smooth muscle cells, which directly targets the tightening and stiffening of the pulmonary arteries. Increasing the force of contraction describes digoxin, which is used in management but by a different mechanism. Removing excess fluid describes the diuretics also used in this condition. Preventing clot formation describes the anticoagulation that is part of the regimen. All four drug classes appear in the management of pulmonary artery hypertension, but only one is the prostanoid mechanism."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q22", "exam": 4, "topic": "PAH", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which conditions are identified as causes of pulmonary artery hypertension? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Heart failure", "Well-controlled seasonal allergic rhinitis", "HIV infection", "Connective tissue disorders such as systemic lupus erythematosus", "Liver disease with portal hypertension"], "correct": [0, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The etiology of pulmonary artery hypertension includes idiopathic disease, drugs and toxins, connective tissue disorders such as systemic lupus erythematosus, HIV, liver disease with portal hypertension, heart failure and lung disease. Well-controlled seasonal allergic rhinitis is an upper airway allergic condition with no role in remodeling the pulmonary vasculature, so it is the one option that does not belong on the list."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q23", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS case", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client recovering from ARDS has fine crackles throughout the posterior lung fields and coarse crackles over the large airways. The previous nurse charted \"fine and coarse crackles that clear with vigorous coughing.\" How should the nurse evaluate the accuracy of that documentation?", "options": ["It is inaccurate, because coarse crackles cannot be cleared by coughing", "It is accurate, because the client's cough was described as vigorous", "It is accurate, because all crackles represent secretions that coughing mobilizes", "It is inaccurate, because fine crackles cannot be cleared by coughing"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Fine crackles indicate fluid accumulation in the pulmonary or parenchymal tissue, which is why they signal impaired gas exchange and why a diuretic rather than a cough is what reduces them. Coarse crackles are produced by air passing around or through secretions in the larger airways, and those can be cleared by coughing. The documentation is therefore inaccurate specifically as to the fine crackles - it is physiologically impossible to clear them with a cough. Treating all crackles as secretions ignores the distinction. Saying the coarse crackles cannot be cleared reverses which type responds to coughing."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q24", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS case", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with pulmonary edema after ARDS is prescribed furosemide 60 mg IV push. Laboratory results show sodium 129 mEq/L, potassium 3.0 mEq/L, calcium 7.1 mg/dL, BUN 37 mg/dL and creatinine 2 mg/dL. What should the nurse do?", "options": ["Hold the dose and notify the provider about the abnormal laboratory values", "Give the dose and add oral potassium supplementation without an order", "Give the dose as ordered, since the diuretic will improve the pulmonary edema", "Give half the dose and recheck the electrolytes in 6 hours"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Furosemide causes electrolyte depletion, and this client's sodium, potassium and calcium are all already low and would be expected to fall further; the elevated BUN and creatinine additionally raise the question of renal impairment affecting the diuretic response. Because several values are outside normal range and a life-threatening situation could result, the nurse must call the provider to report them so the client can be treated appropriately - which is what happened in this case, with potassium, calcium and a magnesium level ordered before the diuretic. Giving the drug as ordered ignores a dangerous electrolyte profile. Altering the dose or adding a supplement without an order is outside nursing scope."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q25", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS case", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "After receiving furosemide, a client with low potassium, calcium and magnesium reports, \"A couple of times I felt like my heart flipped over, and now it feels like a bird is flopping around in there.\" The monitor shows frequent premature ventricular contractions and a four-beat run of ventricular tachycardia. What is the most likely cause?", "options": ["The combination of low oxygen, potassium, calcium and magnesium levels", "Anxiety related to the intensive care environment", "An allergic reaction to the furosemide", "Normal ectopy that requires no further evaluation"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The dysrhythmia in this client is attributed to the combination of low oxygen, potassium, calcium and magnesium - each of these destabilizes myocardial membranes, and the diuretic deepened already low levels. The appropriate actions are to give oxygen, infuse the ordered electrolytes promptly, maintain continuous monitoring with a stat 12-lead ECG, page the provider and establish additional IV access. An allergic reaction would present with other features rather than isolated ventricular ectopy. Anxiety does not generate runs of ventricular tachycardia. Nonsustained ventricular tachycardia in an electrolyte-depleted, hypoxemic client is never dismissed as normal."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q26", "exam": 4, "topic": "Prioritization", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "The nurse has received the morning shift report. Which client should the nurse assess first?", "options": ["A client with COPD on 2 L/min nasal cannula reporting mild fatigue", "A client with pneumonia whose temperature increased from 100.2°F (37.9°C) to 102.4°F (39.1°C) overnight", "A client with a pulmonary embolism who is now reporting worsening shortness of breath", "A client with tuberculosis receiving scheduled IV antibiotics requesting pain medication for pleuritic discomfort rated 4 out of 10"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Worsening shortness of breath in a client with a known pulmonary embolism signals a deteriorating perfusion problem that can progress rapidly to respiratory failure and hemodynamic collapse, so this client is assessed first. Mild fatigue in a client with COPD on a low flow rate is a chronic, stable finding. A rising temperature in pneumonia is important and must be addressed, but fever alone is not an immediate threat to airway, breathing or circulation. A request for analgesia for pain rated 4 out of 10 deserves prompt attention but does not outrank an acute change in respiratory status."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q27", "exam": 4, "topic": "KCl safety", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with a potassium of 3.0 mEq/L is prescribed potassium chloride 40 mEq IVPB through a PICC line. Which interventions are required for safe administration? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Place the client on continuous electrocardiogram monitoring", "Administer the infusion using an IV pump", "Invert the potassium-containing IV bag several times before and during the infusion", "Assess the patency of the PICC line before initiating the infusion", "Administer the potassium infusion over a period of at least 2 hours"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3], "rationale": "Intravenous potassium must be given on a pump to control the rate, with continuous ECG monitoring because potassium shifts alter cardiac conduction, after verifying line patency so the drug does not extravasate, and with the bag inverted several times before and during the infusion so the potassium is evenly distributed rather than pooling. The two-hour limit is the unsafe option: no more than 10 mEq is typically given per hour, which makes the minimum infusion time for 40 mEq four hours, not two."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q28", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS complications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is recovering from a prolonged ICU stay for ARDS. Which long-term complications should the nurse monitor for and discuss with the client? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Permanent dependence on hemodialysis", "Permanent loss of the ability to speak", "Chronic hypoxemia from scarring and thickening of lung tissue", "Depression", "Memory loss and cognitive problems"], "correct": [2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Scarring and thickening of lung tissue can produce chronic hypoxemia; immobility raises the risk of venous thromboembolism; sedative use and hypoxemia can lead to memory loss and cognitive problems that may lessen over time or be permanent; and most ARDS survivors report a period of depression, which is treatable and should be discussed openly. Permanent loss of speech is not a described complication - clients who were intubated may have temporary hoarseness or difficulty communicating, and communication boards are used during intubation, but permanent aphonia is not an expected outcome. Permanent dialysis dependence is not a described consequence of ARDS, which is a lung injury; kidney failure may occur during critical illness but is not among the long-term complications identified for ARDS survivors."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q29", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS etiology", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which client has the single most common cause of ARDS as an indirect trigger?", "options": ["A client who aspirated gastric contents during a seizure", "A client with septic shock from a urinary source", "A client who nearly drowned in a swimming pool", "A client with COVID-19 pneumonia"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Sepsis is identified as the most common cause of ARDS and is an indirect trigger, meaning the lung injury results from a systemic inflammatory cascade rather than from something entering the lung. Aspiration, COVID-19 pneumonia and near drowning are all genuine ARDS triggers, but they are direct injuries in which the insult reaches the alveoli through the airway. The question asks for the most common indirect cause, which makes sepsis the answer even though all four clients are at risk."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q30", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS assessment", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which assessment findings would the nurse expect in a client developing ARDS? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A low PaO2 that does not respond to oxygen supplementation", "Excessive sweating", "Tachypnea with accessory muscle use", "Diminished lung sounds", "Bradycardia with a widening pulse pressure"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3], "rationale": "The signs of ARDS include tachypnea, accessory muscle use, a low PaO2 that does not respond to supplemental oxygen, diminished lung sounds, excessive sweating and ventilator alarms indicating the client is not oxygenating as expected. Bradycardia with a widening pulse pressure is not among them; a client struggling with refractory hypoxemia is typically tachycardic, and a widening pulse pressure is associated with other processes entirely."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q31", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS management", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with severe ARDS is fighting the ventilator despite adequate sedation, and oxygenation is worsening. Which intervention should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Extubation and a trial of high-flow nasal cannula", "Discontinuing sedation so the client can breathe spontaneously", "Administration of a neuromuscular blocking agent along with continued sedation", "Decreasing the FiO2 to reduce oxygen toxicity"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Medical management of ARDS includes mechanical ventilation, paralytics and sedation, maintaining the airway and supporting hemodynamics. When asynchrony persists and oxygenation worsens, paralytics eliminate the client's work against the ventilator - and because a paralyzed client can feel but not move or breathe, sedation must always continue alongside. Extubating a client with worsening refractory hypoxemia removes the support they depend on. Decreasing the FiO2 while oxygenation is deteriorating is the wrong direction. Stopping sedation in a client already fighting the ventilator would worsen asynchrony and distress."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q32", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF assessment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which finding in a client with worsening respiratory status is most concerning and suggests imminent decompensation?", "options": ["The client reports mild shortness of breath with ambulation", "The client states, \"Something is terribly wrong, I think I'm going to die\"", "The client's respiratory rate increased from 16 to 20", "The client prefers the head of the bed elevated"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "A sense of impending doom is a listed sign of acute respiratory failure and is a recognized marker of severe physiologic distress that should never be dismissed as anxiety. Mild exertional dyspnea is a symptom to evaluate but is not an acute warning. A respiratory rate rising from 16 to 20 remains within or just at the edge of normal and is a far weaker signal than the others would suggest. Preferring the head of the bed elevated reflects a client positioning themselves for easier breathing, which is a reasonable self-management behavior rather than an alarming finding."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q33", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF diagnostics", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client is admitted with acute respiratory failure of unknown cause. Which diagnostic studies should the nurse anticipate to evaluate the underlying cause? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Lumbar puncture and cerebrospinal fluid analysis", "Arterial blood gas and chest x-ray", "Electrocardiogram and echocardiogram", "Electrolytes and thyroid function studies", "Complete blood count and pan-culture"], "correct": [1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Diagnostics for acute respiratory failure begin with the arterial blood gas, which classifies the failure, and the chest x-ray; the workup to find the cause includes a complete blood count, pan-culture, electrolytes, pulmonary function tests, thyroid function, electrocardiogram, echocardiogram and bronchoscopy - thyroid dysfunction and electrolyte problems are listed among the metabolic causes. Lumbar puncture is not part of this workup; although central nervous system depression is an extra-pulmonary cause, it is evaluated through the history, level of consciousness and drug effects rather than by routine cerebrospinal fluid analysis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q34", "exam": 4, "topic": "Oxygen delivery", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client in acute respiratory failure needs a precise, controlled concentration of oxygen because of a history of carbon dioxide retention. Which delivery device should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Venturi mask", "Non-rebreather mask", "Simple face mask", "Bag-mask ventilation"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The Venturi mask is the first device listed in the oxygen escalation for acute respiratory failure and delivers a precise, controlled oxygen concentration, which is what a carbon dioxide retainer needs so the oxygen can be titrated without blunting the respiratory drive. A non-rebreather delivers a high, less precisely controlled concentration and sits further up the escalation. A simple face mask provides variable concentrations that change with the client's breathing pattern. Bag-mask ventilation is used to ventilate a client who is not breathing adequately, not to deliver a controlled concentration to a spontaneously breathing client."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q35", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF medications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which medication classes are used in the medical management of acute respiratory failure? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Antibiotics", "Corticosteroids", "Neuromuscular stimulants to increase respiratory drive", "Bronchodilators", "Diuretics"], "correct": [0, 1, 3, 4], "rationale": "Medical management of acute respiratory failure includes treating the underlying cause with bronchodilators for airway narrowing, steroids for inflammation, diuretics for pulmonary edema, antibiotics for infection and anticoagulants when embolism is the cause, alongside oxygen and ventilatory support. Neuromuscular stimulants to increase respiratory drive are not among the listed therapies; when the drive or the muscles fail, the answer is mechanical ventilation rather than pharmacologic stimulation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q36", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS vs ARF", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nursing student asks how ARDS differs from acute respiratory failure. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": ["ARDS occurs only in clients who are already mechanically ventilated", "ARDS is acute respiratory failure that resolves within 24 hours", "ARDS is acute respiratory failure plus hypoxemia that persists despite 100% oxygen", "ARDS involves only carbon dioxide retention, while respiratory failure involves only low oxygen"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "ARDS is described as acute respiratory failure plus hypoxemia that persists despite 100% oxygen, and acute respiratory failure can progress to ARDS - the refractory nature of the hypoxemia is the defining difference, captured in the principle that if oxygen does not work, think ARDS. ARDS does not resolve in 24 hours; it evolves through exudative, proliferative and fibrotic phases over weeks. ARDS is fundamentally an oxygenation failure rather than a pure carbon dioxide problem. It is triggered by events such as sepsis, trauma, aspiration and pneumonia and can develop in clients who were never previously ventilated."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q37", "exam": 4, "topic": "PAH nursing", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which assessments should the nurse prioritize for a client with pulmonary artery hypertension and right heart failure? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Assessment of jugular venous distention and peripheral edema", "Daily weights and abdominal girth", "Serial measurement of peak expiratory flow to grade airway obstruction", "Assessment for tracheal deviation at the start of each shift", "Activity tolerance and episodes of dizziness or syncope"], "correct": [0, 1, 4], "rationale": "Right heart failure from pulmonary artery hypertension produces systemic venous congestion, so daily weights and abdominal girth track fluid retention and ascites, and jugular venous distention and peripheral edema are the direct physical markers. Syncope, weakness and fatigue are listed symptoms, so activity tolerance and syncopal episodes guide both safety and the response to therapy. Peak expiratory flow grades airflow obstruction in asthma and is not relevant here, because the pathology lies in the pulmonary vasculature rather than the airways."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q38", "exam": 4, "topic": "PAH", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client asks why pulmonary artery hypertension causes the right side of the heart to enlarge. Which explanation is correct?", "options": ["The left ventricle fails first and the right side enlarges to take over its work", "Blood backs up from the lungs into the left atrium and stretches the right side", "The pulmonary veins dilate, allowing excessive blood to pool in the right ventricle", "The right side must work harder to push blood through narrowed, stiffened pulmonary arteries"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "In pulmonary artery hypertension the arteries of the lungs tighten and stiffen, so the right side of the heart has to work harder to push blood through them; that extra stress causes it to enlarge and become less flexible, eventually producing right heart failure. The right ventricle does not take over the left ventricle's work, and this disease begins on the right. Blood backing into the left atrium describes left-sided congestion, which is a different problem. Dilation of the pulmonary veins is not the mechanism; the pathology is narrowing of the pulmonary arteries, which raises resistance rather than allowing pooling."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q39", "exam": 4, "topic": "COVID-19", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with COVID-19 pneumonia has worsening infiltrates. The nurse identifies that the priority of care is to implement interventions that prevent which complication?", "options": ["Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "Spontaneous pneumothorax", "Acute lung injury, which can progress to acute respiratory distress syndrome", "Pulmonary artery hypertension"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "COVID-19 and other pneumonias cause acute lung injury because inflammation occurs as a pathologic response; if it is not responsive to treatment, acute lung injury can lead to acute respiratory distress syndrome, which is life-threatening. Preventing that progression is therefore the priority. COPD is a chronic disease that develops over years from exposures such as smoking, not an acute complication of this admission. Spontaneous pneumothorax is not the complication the infiltrates predict. Pulmonary artery hypertension can follow chronic lung disease and the fibrotic phase of ARDS, but it is a later consequence rather than the immediate complication to prevent."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q40", "exam": 4, "topic": "COVID-19", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client has tested positive for COVID-19 and has fever, a frequent cough, crackles, worsening shortness of breath, chest infiltrates and an SpO2 of 92%. Which findings in this client's record are risk factors for COVID-19 infection rather than current evidence of lung involvement? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A history of hypertension", "A body mass index over 24.9", "Chest infiltrates on x-ray", "Crackles on auscultation", "Worsening shortness of breath"], "correct": [0, 1], "rationale": "A history of hypertension and a body mass index over 24.9 are risk factors for acquiring COVID-19, but this client has already tested positive, so these historical findings are not of concern to the nurse at this point in care. Crackles, worsening shortness of breath and chest infiltrates are current findings that evidence active lung involvement, together with the fever, the frequent cough and an SpO2 of 92% against a normal range of 95% or greater - these are what should concern the nurse now, because they signal the acute lung injury that can progress to ARDS."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q41", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS ventilation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with ARDS on mechanical ventilation triggers frequent high pressure alarms. The nurse suctions without obtaining secretions, finds no kinks, and notes the client is not biting the tube. Which explanation should the nurse consider next?", "options": ["The endotracheal tube cuff has deflated", "The ventilator circuit has become disconnected", "The stiff, non-compliant lungs of ARDS are generating higher airway pressures", "The client has been over-sedated"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A high pressure alarm means the machine is meeting resistance, and while secretions, kinks, biting and coughing against the vent are the common causes, ARDS itself produces stiff, non-compliant lungs that require higher pressures to inflate - which is why ventilator alarms are listed among the signs of ARDS. A deflated cuff and a circuit disconnection both cause low pressure or low volume alarms, since the problem is failure to reach the lung rather than resistance. Over-sedation would produce apnea alarms rather than high pressure alarms."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q42", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF pathophysiology", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse explains that adequate gas exchange requires a near match between ventilation and perfusion. Which client demonstrates a ventilation-perfusion mismatch in which alveoli are perfused but not ventilated?", "options": ["A client with a normal chest x-ray and normal ABGs", "A client who is hyperventilating from anxiety", "A client with a saddle pulmonary embolism", "A client whose right mainstem bronchus is obstructed by a mucus plug"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Gas exchange depends on diffusion, ventilation and perfusion, and a near match between ventilation and perfusion is needed. A mucus plug obstructing a bronchus stops air from reaching alveoli that still receive blood flow - perfused but not ventilated. A saddle embolism is the reverse: the alveoli are ventilated but blood cannot reach them. Hyperventilation from anxiety increases ventilation without a primary perfusion defect. A client with normal imaging and normal gases demonstrates matched ventilation and perfusion, which is the standard against which mismatch is judged."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q43", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS psychosocial", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with new ventricular ectopy and worsening respiratory status looks frightened and is lying rigidly still. Which nursing response is most appropriate?", "options": ["Explain what is happening and what is being done, and ask whether there is someone to call to be with her", "Dim the lights and leave the room so the client can rest", "Tell the client not to worry because the team is handling everything", "Explain that focusing on the fear will make the dysrhythmia worse"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The appropriate response is to explain to the client what is happening and the treatments being implemented and why, to say that she will be kept informed, to ask whether there is someone who could come and be with her, to invite questions, and to give her the call light with instructions to alert the nurse to any change. Telling a frightened client not to worry dismisses the fear without giving information. Leaving a client who is acutely deteriorating alone in a dark room removes both monitoring and support. Suggesting her fear worsens the dysrhythmia blames the client for a problem caused by hypoxemia and electrolyte depletion."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q44", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS interventions", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with respiratory acidosis and frequent ventricular ectopy after ARDS has nursing priorities of stabilizing cardiac status and improving respiratory status. Which interventions meet these priorities? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Elevate the head of the bed", "Maintain the prescribed electrolyte infusions", "Monitor vital signs frequently with continuous ECG and SaO2 monitoring", "Continue oxygen per protocol", "Auscultate lung sounds every 1 to 2 hours"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "All five interventions were identified for this client. Oxygen addresses the hypoxemia contributing to the ectopy; elevating the head of the bed improves chest expansion and ventilation; frequent vital signs with continuous ECG and oxygen saturation monitoring detect further deterioration; maintaining the electrolyte infusions corrects the low potassium, calcium and magnesium driving the dysrhythmia; and auscultating lung sounds every 1 to 2 hours tracks the pulmonary status. Encouraging coughing, deep breathing and incentive spirometry every 2 hours and giving an as-needed anxiolytic complete the plan, so there is no option here that should be excluded."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q45", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF nursing care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with acute respiratory failure is found sitting upright, leaning forward over the bedside table with arms braced. What is the nurse's most appropriate action?", "options": ["Support the position with pillows, because it helps open the alveoli and ease the work of breathing", "Encourage the client to lie on the unaffected side to improve perfusion", "Interpret the position as a sign of anxiety and administer an anxiolytic", "Reposition the client supine with the head of the bed flat to reduce energy expenditure"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Tripod positioning is a listed sign of increased work of breathing and is also an intervention the nurse encourages, with the client leaning over a few pillows to open the alveoli. Supporting the position makes the client's own compensation more effective and more comfortable. Placing a client in respiratory distress flat and supine removes the mechanical advantage they have found and worsens dyspnea. Side-lying is not described as the intervention for this situation. Treating the position as mere anxiety and sedating the client risks depressing an already compromised respiratory drive."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q46", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS timing", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client was admitted with severe pancreatitis four days ago and now has worsening hypoxemia and bilateral infiltrates. How should the nurse interpret the timing in relation to ARDS?", "options": ["This is too early for ARDS, which requires at least 2 weeks after the trigger", "This is consistent with ARDS, which usually develops within 7 days of the precipitating factor", "Pancreatitis is not a recognized trigger, so ARDS can be excluded", "This is too late for ARDS, which must occur within 2 hours of the trigger"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "ARDS results from an inflammatory cascade with onset within 7 days of the precipitating factor, so a presentation on day 4 fits the expected window. Requiring 2 weeks would describe the fibrotic phase, which is a stage of established ARDS rather than its onset. A 2-hour window is far too narrow and does not match the described time course. Pancreatitis is explicitly listed among the indirect triggers of ARDS, alongside sepsis, trauma, massive transfusion and drug overdose, so it cannot be used to exclude the diagnosis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q47", "exam": 4, "topic": "Refractory hypoxemia", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "A client with suspected ARDS has the FiO2 increased from 0.50 to 0.80 and then to 1.0 over two hours. The PaO2 moves from 48 to 51 to 53 mm Hg. How should the nurse evaluate this response?", "options": ["The hypoxemia is refractory, which supports ARDS and means ventilatory strategies beyond FiO2 are needed", "The response is expected and the FiO2 should be increased further", "The results indicate a laboratory error and should be repeated before acting", "The therapy is effective because the PaO2 has risen with each increase"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "A PaO2 that barely moves while the FiO2 is doubled is refractory hypoxemia, the hallmark of ARDS, and it means the problem is collapsed, fluid-filled alveoli that oxygen alone cannot reach - the answer is mechanical ventilation with PEEP to recruit alveoli, prone positioning, sedation and paralytics, and treatment of the root cause. Calling a 5-point rise across a doubling of FiO2 effective misreads a failure as a success. The FiO2 is already at 1.0 and cannot be increased further, and high concentrations are themselves harmful. Dismissing three consistent results as laboratory error would delay recognition of a life-threatening condition."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q48", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARF nutrition", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is planning care for a client with chronic acute respiratory failure who has lost 12 pounds over the past month. Why is nutritional support a priority for this client?", "options": ["Respiratory muscles require energy, and malnutrition contributes to ventilatory failure", "High-calorie intake decreases the amount of carbon dioxide produced", "Additional calories will directly increase the oxygen saturation", "Nutritional support prevents the development of pulmonary artery hypertension"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Nutritional support is a listed nursing intervention for acute respiratory failure because breathing is muscular work; a malnourished client has weakened respiratory muscles that fatigue sooner, and malnutrition is also named as a risk factor for ventilator-associated pneumonia. Calories do not directly raise the oxygen saturation, which depends on gas exchange. Pulmonary artery hypertension arises from vascular remodeling and chronic lung disease rather than from nutritional status. Metabolizing nutrients produces carbon dioxide rather than reducing it, so the claim that a high-calorie intake decreases carbon dioxide production states the physiology backwards."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q49", "exam": 4, "topic": "PAH", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with pulmonary artery hypertension is prescribed anticoagulation. Which outcome indicates the nurse should notify the provider?", "options": ["Improved exercise tolerance over several weeks", "Resolution of ankle edema after starting a diuretic", "New hemoptysis with a drop in hemoglobin", "A stable weight across three consecutive days"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Anticoagulation is part of the medical management of pulmonary artery hypertension, and bleeding is its major hazard; new hemoptysis with a falling hemoglobin indicates bleeding and must be reported. Resolution of ankle edema after a diuretic, improved exercise tolerance and a stable weight are all desirable outcomes showing the regimen is working - fluid status and activity tolerance are exactly what the nurse monitors to evaluate therapy in right heart failure, and improvement in them is reassuring rather than reportable."},
  {"id": "n504e2s4q50", "exam": 4, "topic": "ARDS collaborative care", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which elements are part of the collaborative ICU care that clients with ARDS generally receive? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Mechanical ventilation with PEEP", "Nutritional support with parenteral or enteral feeding", "Hemodynamic pressure monitoring to assess cardiac status", "Routine daily bronchoscopy regardless of findings", "Treatment of the underlying condition"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 4], "rationale": "Collaborative care for ARDS includes mechanical ventilation with PEEP to maintain oxygenation, with alternative strategies such as airway pressure-release or high-frequency oscillatory ventilation; hemodynamic pressure monitoring to assess cardiac status; nutritional support by the parenteral or enteral route; and treatment of the underlying conditions. Bronchoscopy has a role when it is indicated - for example to obtain samples or clear secretions and pus - but scheduling it daily regardless of findings is not part of the described plan and would expose the client to unnecessary risk."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q01", "exam": 5, "topic": "Tube placement verification", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client has just been intubated by the anesthesiologist. Which action should the nurse take first to check for proper tube placement?", "options": ["Instill air into the endotracheal tube and listen for airflow into the lungs", "Arrange for a CT scan", "Auscultate the lungs for the presence of bilateral breath sounds", "Assess for signs of desaturation"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Immediately after intubation, placement is verified at the bedside: the end-tidal carbon dioxide detector changes color, bilateral breath sounds are auscultated with symmetric chest rise, and a chest x-ray then confirms the tip is 2 to 3 cm above the carina as the gold standard. Of the options offered, auscultating for bilateral breath sounds is the immediate bedside check. A CT scan is not used to confirm tube placement and would require transporting a newly intubated client. Instilling air and listening is a technique associated with feeding tubes and is unreliable even there. Waiting for desaturation means waiting for harm; placement is confirmed before the client deteriorates."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q02", "exam": 5, "topic": "Tube placement", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A chest x-ray after intubation shows the endotracheal tube tip in the right mainstem bronchus. Which assessment finding would the nurse most likely have noted?", "options": ["Continuous bubbling in the water seal chamber", "Breath sounds present on the right but absent or diminished on the left", "Breath sounds equal bilaterally with symmetric chest rise", "Gurgling over the epigastrium with each delivered breath"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The tube tip should sit at least 2 cm above the carina so both lungs are ventilated. If it advances past the carina it enters a mainstem bronchus - most often the right - and only that lung is ventilated, producing unilateral breath sounds and asymmetric chest rise. Equal bilateral sounds with symmetric rise is the finding expected with correct placement. Epigastric gurgling suggests esophageal rather than tracheal intubation. Water seal bubbling is a chest tube finding and has nothing to do with endotracheal tube position."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q03", "exam": 5, "topic": "Cuff management", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse measures an endotracheal tube cuff pressure of 48 cm H2O. What is the most important consequence of leaving this uncorrected?", "options": ["Aspiration of oral secretions around the cuff", "Accidental extubation from inadequate cuff seal", "A low pressure ventilator alarm from a cuff leak", "Tracheal tissue ischemia and necrosis from excessive pressure on the mucosa"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Cuff pressure is maintained in the range of 20 to 40 cm H2O; it must be high enough to prevent aspiration of secretions but low enough not to compress the tracheal mucosa, and tracheal necrosis is a listed complication of mechanical ventilation. A pressure of 48 exceeds the range and threatens the tracheal blood supply. Aspiration around the cuff, accidental extubation and a low pressure alarm are all consequences of a cuff that is under-inflated or leaking, which is the opposite problem from the one described here."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q04", "exam": 5, "topic": "DOPE", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "An intubated client suddenly desaturates and becomes difficult to ventilate. Using the DOPE mnemonic, which assessment sequence should the nurse follow?", "options": ["Check for dehydration, overload, perfusion and edema", "Check for drug reaction, oliguria, pain and effusion", "Check for delirium, oxygen toxicity, pressure injury and electrolytes", "Check for tube displacement, obstruction, pneumothorax and equipment failure"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "DOPE directs the nurse through the four causes of acute deterioration in an intubated client: Displacement of the tube, Obstruction by secretions or a kink, Pneumothorax from barotrauma, and Equipment failure. Each has a distinct remedy - repositioning or reintubation, suctioning, decompression, or bagging the client while the ventilator is checked. The other expansions are invented and do not correspond to the emergency assessment framework used for a ventilated client who acutely worsens."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q05", "exam": 5, "topic": "Ventilator alarms", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A high pressure alarm sounds on a ventilated client's machine. Which assessment finding would explain this alarm?", "options": ["The ventilator circuit has come apart at a connection", "Thick secretions audible in the endotracheal tube", "The pilot balloon on the endotracheal tube is flat", "The client has become apneic after a sedation bolus"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "A high pressure alarm means the machine is meeting resistance on the way to the lung, and secretions are the most common cause, along with a kinked or bitten tube, the client coughing or fighting the ventilator, bronchospasm and pneumothorax. A flat pilot balloon indicates a deflated cuff, and a disconnected circuit means air is never reaching the lung - both produce low pressure or low volume alarms. Apnea after a sedation bolus triggers an apnea alarm, which signals the absence of breaths rather than resistance to them."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q06", "exam": 5, "topic": "Ventilator alarms", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A low pressure alarm is sounding on a ventilated client's machine. Which causes should the nurse investigate? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Equipment failure", "A disconnection in the ventilator circuit", "Displacement of the endotracheal tube", "A leaking or deflated endotracheal tube cuff", "A mucus plug obstructing the endotracheal tube"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3], "rationale": "A low pressure or low volume alarm means the delivered breath is not reaching the lung wall, so the nurse looks for a disconnection or loose tubing, a cuff leak or deflated cuff, extubation or tube displacement, and equipment failure. A mucus plug is the opposite situation: it creates resistance and triggers a high pressure alarm. Distinguishing the two alarm families matters because one calls for restoring the circuit and the other for clearing the airway."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q07", "exam": 5, "topic": "Ventilator emergency", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A ventilator alarm is sounding and the nurse cannot identify or correct the cause while the client's saturation falls. What is the nurse's priority action?", "options": ["Disconnect the client from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve mask and 100% oxygen", "Increase the FiO2 on the ventilator to 100% and continue troubleshooting", "Silence the alarm and call respiratory therapy to assess the machine", "Administer a sedative bolus to reduce the client's work of breathing"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The guiding principle is to be concerned with the patient first and the ventilator second: when an alarm cannot be fixed, the client is disconnected and bagged with 100% oxygen while the problem is sorted out. That guarantees ventilation regardless of what is wrong with the machine. Silencing the alarm and waiting for respiratory therapy leaves a desaturating client unventilated. Raising the FiO2 does nothing if breaths are not reaching the lung. Sedating a client whose oxygenation is falling for an unknown reason may remove their remaining respiratory effort and worsen the emergency."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q08", "exam": 5, "topic": "Accidental extubation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A confused client self-extubates. The client is breathing spontaneously but is stridorous with a saturation of 86%. Which sequence of actions is correct?", "options": ["Apply soft wrist restraints first, then notify the provider", "Reinsert the endotracheal tube immediately before calling for help", "Apply a nasal cannula at 2 L and continue to observe the client", "Call a rapid response, assess airway, breathing and circulation, support ventilation with a bag-valve mask and 100% oxygen, and prepare for reintubation"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "For accidental extubation the sequence is to call a rapid response, assess airway, breathing and circulation, provide bag-valve-mask ventilation with 100% oxygen if the client is not breathing adequately, and prepare for emergency reintubation. A stridorous client with a saturation of 86% is not breathing adequately. Reinserting the tube is not within nursing scope and would delay calling for the team. A 2 L nasal cannula is far too little support for this degree of compromise. Applying restraints before addressing airway and oxygenation inverts the priorities, and restraints are a last resort rather than a first response."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q09", "exam": 5, "topic": "Ventilator modes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client who has been on assist control is being transitioned toward extubation, and the provider changes the mode so the ventilator delivers breaths in synchrony with the client's own efforts and provides breaths if the client becomes apneic. Which mode is being used?", "options": ["Continuous positive airway pressure", "Assist control", "Synchronized intermittent mandatory ventilation", "Bi-level positive airway pressure"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Synchronized intermittent mandatory ventilation is the weaning mode: it breathes with the client and supplies breaths when the client is apneic, which is exactly the description given. Assist control is the full support mode in which the ventilator completely breathes for the client, so it is what the client is being moved away from. CPAP delivers continuous pressure to keep the alveoli open but does not deliver set breaths. BiPAP provides two pressure levels and is typically a noninvasive mode rather than one used to wean an intubated client through mandatory breath delivery."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q10", "exam": 5, "topic": "PEEP", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse is explaining PEEP to a student. Which statement is accurate?", "options": ["PEEP keeps the alveoli open at the end of expiration, typically starting at 5 and running 10 to 20 in ARDS", "PEEP is the number of breaths the ventilator delivers each minute", "PEEP determines the amount of air pushed in with each breath", "PEEP sets the percentage of oxygen the client receives"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "PEEP is positive end-expiratory pressure, which holds the alveoli open during expiration so they do not collapse; it usually starts around 5 and may run 10 to 20 cm H2O in ARDS, with too much causing barotrauma and a falling blood pressure. The amount of air delivered per breath is the tidal volume. The percentage of oxygen delivered is the FiO2. The number of breaths per minute is the rate. Each is a separate ventilator setting, and confusing them leads to the wrong corrective action when gas exchange is inadequate."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q11", "exam": 5, "topic": "Weaning", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse is evaluating whether a ventilated client is ready for weaning. Which findings support readiness? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Adequate oxygenation with normal arterial blood gases", "The client is breathing over the set ventilator rate", "The client has a stable neurologic examination", "The client is coughing and gagging on the tube", "The client requires a continuous paralytic infusion for ventilator synchrony"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3], "rationale": "Weaning parameters are adequate oxygenation with normal gases, breathing over the tube, coughing or gagging on the tube, adequate ventilation, the ability to maintain an airway, and a stable neurologic exam - all signs the client is beginning to breathe on their own. A client requiring a continuous paralytic cannot breathe spontaneously at all, cannot protect an airway and cannot demonstrate any of the other parameters, so that finding excludes rather than supports weaning readiness."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q12", "exam": 5, "topic": "Weaning", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which pair of daily interventions most directly supports earlier extubation in a ventilated client?", "options": ["Daily prophylactic antibiotics and routine suctioning every 2 hours", "Daily increases in PEEP and tidal volume", "A daily sedation vacation and a spontaneous breathing trial", "Daily chest physiotherapy and a fluid bolus"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A daily sedation vacation lets the team see whether the client can wake and follow commands, and the spontaneous breathing trial tests whether they can do the work of breathing - together these are the core practices that move a client toward extubation, and planning for extubation begins at intubation. Chest physiotherapy helps clear secretions and fluid may be needed, but neither tests readiness to breathe independently. Increasing PEEP and tidal volume escalates support rather than reducing it. Prophylactic antibiotics are not indicated, and routine scheduled suctioning is specifically discouraged because suctioning should occur only when needed."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q13", "exam": 5, "topic": "Suctioning", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse plans to suction an intubated client every 2 hours as a routine. What is the most appropriate response from the charge nurse?", "options": ["\"Increase it to hourly if the client has any secretions at all\"", "\"Suction only when it is truly needed, because routine suctioning increases the risk of VAP and tracheal injury\"", "\"Suction only once per shift regardless of the client's secretions\"", "\"Every 2 hours is appropriate, since it keeps the airway clear between assessments\""], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Suctioning is performed only when truly needed - for excess secretions causing airway compromise, audible secretions, or an inability to clear them. A client is never routinely suctioned, because suctioning raises the risk of ventilator-associated pneumonia and can cause tracheal injury, and it stimulates the vagus nerve, which can produce sinus bradycardia. A fixed 2-hour or hourly schedule makes suctioning routine, which is exactly what should be avoided. Limiting suctioning to once per shift regardless of secretions is the opposite error, withholding a needed intervention from a client who cannot clear their own airway."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q14", "exam": 5, "topic": "Suctioning", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "During endotracheal suctioning the client's heart rate drops from 88 to 44. What is the most likely cause and the nurse's best immediate action?", "options": ["Vagal stimulation from the suction catheter; stop suctioning and oxygenate the client", "An opioid overdose; administer naloxone", "A failing pacemaker; prepare for transcutaneous pacing", "Hypovolemia; administer a fluid bolus"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Suctioning stimulates the vagus nerve and can cause the onset of sinus bradycardia, so an abrupt fall in heart rate during the procedure points to vagal stimulation; the nurse stops suctioning and oxygenates the client, which removes the stimulus and treats any associated hypoxemia. Naloxone reverses opioids, but nothing suggests an opioid is responsible for a bradycardia that began precisely during suctioning. Pacing is not the first response to a bradycardia with an identifiable and removable cause. Hypovolemia typically produces tachycardia rather than bradycardia."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q15", "exam": 5, "topic": "VAP prevention", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which interventions are part of the ventilator-associated pneumonia prevention bundle? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Provide ulcer prophylaxis with an H2 blocker or proton pump inhibitor", "Maintain cuff pressure within the prescribed range to prevent aspiration", "Maintain the head of the bed at 30 degrees or higher", "Turn the client every 2 hours and perform spontaneous breathing trials", "Perform oral care frequently"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The VAP prevention bundle includes keeping the head of the bed at 30 degrees or higher, frequent oral care because poor oral care is the leading contributor, maintaining cuff pressure so oral secretions are not aspirated past the cuff, ulcer prophylaxis, pulmonary hygiene, suctioning only as needed, turning every 2 hours, sedation vacations and spontaneous breathing trials. Changing the circuit every 8 hours is not part of the bundle - the ventilator circuit is itself a route by which pathogens enter, and unnecessary manipulation of it increases rather than decreases risk. Turning every 2 hours and performing spontaneous breathing trials along with sedation vacations are also bundle elements, because the faster a client comes off the ventilator the shorter the window for infection."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q16", "exam": 5, "topic": "VAP", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client has been ventilated for 9 days and was reintubated yesterday after a failed extubation. The nurse recognizes this client is at particularly high risk for ventilator-associated pneumonia because of which factors?", "options": ["Use of inline suctioning and oral care", "A single intubation attempt and early extubation", "Prolonged intubation and reintubation", "Young age and good nutritional status"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Reintubation and prolonged intubation are both emphasized as major risk factors for ventilator-associated pneumonia, and this client has both. Advanced age, chronic lung disease, aspiration, previous antibiotics, malnutrition and ARDS complete the risk list, so young age and good nutrition would be protective rather than risk-conferring. A single intubation with early extubation describes the low-risk course. Inline suctioning and oral care are preventive interventions, not risks."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q17", "exam": 5, "topic": "VAP", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A ventilated client develops a temperature of 101.8°F (38.8°C), rhonchi, increased thick secretions and a rise in oxygen requirements from 40% to 65%. Which action should the nurse anticipate first?", "options": ["Obtaining a sputum culture, white blood cell count and chest x-ray", "Immediate extubation to remove the source of infection", "Discontinuing oral care to avoid disturbing the airway", "Decreasing the PEEP to reduce barotrauma"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "These findings - fever, rhonchi, increased secretions and rising oxygen requirements - are the signs of ventilator-associated pneumonia, which is diagnosed with a sputum sample, white blood cell count and chest x-ray, then treated with antibiotics and sometimes bronchoscopy. Extubating a client whose oxygen requirement is climbing removes needed support and does not treat the infection. Oral care is one of the main preventive measures and should be intensified rather than stopped. Reducing PEEP addresses barotrauma risk but would worsen oxygenation in a client who is already requiring more oxygen."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q18", "exam": 5, "topic": "VAP transmission", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "By which routes do pathogens enter the lung to cause ventilator-associated pneumonia? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Oral bacteria", "The ventilator circuit", "Biofilm on the endotracheal tube", "Hematogenous spread from a distant wound", "The hands of health care workers"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 4], "rationale": "Pathogens reach the lung through the endotracheal tube itself, carried on the hands of health care workers, from the ventilator circuit, from biofilm that forms on the tube, and from oral bacteria - which is why hand hygiene and frequent oral care are so central to prevention. Hematogenous spread from a distant site is a mechanism of other infections but is not among the routes described for ventilator-associated pneumonia, which is defined by organisms entering through the artificial airway."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q19", "exam": 5, "topic": "Agitation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "An intubated client who was calm becomes suddenly restless and agitated, pulling at the ventilator tubing. What should the nurse consider first?", "options": ["Hypoxia, and assess oxygenation and tube position before increasing sedation", "That the client is experiencing ICU psychosis and needs an antipsychotic", "That the client needs restraints applied for safety", "That the sedation should be immediately doubled"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Agitation and restlessness in an intubated client should make the nurse think hypoxia first, so oxygenation and tube position are assessed before anything is given - the DOPE causes of displacement, obstruction, pneumothorax and equipment failure all present this way. Applying restraints without assessment treats a symptom and restraints are a last resort in any case. Doubling sedation may mask a developing emergency and remove the client's respiratory effort. ICU delirium and psychosis are real complications, but they are considered after a physiologic cause for acute agitation has been ruled out."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q20", "exam": 5, "topic": "RASS", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "An intubated client is assessed at +3 on the Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, pulling at tubes and fighting the ventilator. What should the nurse do?", "options": ["Target a RASS of -5 so the client is completely unarousable", "Maintain the current sedation and document the finding", "Increase the sedation, targeting a RASS of about 0 to -2", "Decrease the sedation so the client can participate in care"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The RASS runs from +4, combative, down to -5, unarousable. A score of +3 indicates a very agitated client pulling at tubes, and the guidance is that +2 or +3 calls for increasing sedation. The target for an intubated client is approximately 0 to -2, around -1, which is a drowsy but rousable state that tolerates the tube. Decreasing sedation in a client at +3 risks self-extubation. Documenting without acting leaves the client unsafe. A target of -5 is over-sedation, which prolongs ventilation, prevents sedation vacations and spontaneous breathing trials, and causes apnea alarms."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q21", "exam": 5, "topic": "RASS", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse assesses a sedated client who opens their eyes to voice and makes eye contact for less than 10 seconds. Which RASS score does this represent?", "options": ["-1, drowsy", "-4, deep sedation", "-2, light sedation", "-3, moderate sedation"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "On the RASS, -1 describes a drowsy client who sustains eye contact for more than 10 seconds, -2 is light sedation with eye contact lasting less than 10 seconds, -3 is moderate sedation where the client moves to voice but makes no eye contact, and -4 is deep sedation with a response only to physical stimulation. Eye opening with eye contact under 10 seconds matches -2 precisely. The distinction between -1 and -2 rests entirely on the duration of eye contact, which is why the 10-second threshold is the key to scoring accurately."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q22", "exam": 5, "topic": "Sedation levels", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client receiving moderate procedural sedation responds purposefully to verbal commands accompanied by light touch, maintains their own airway, and has stable spontaneous ventilation. How should the nurse classify this level of sedation?", "options": ["Deep sedation, which requires immediate airway support", "Minimal sedation, which means more medication is required", "Moderate sedation and analgesia, which is the intended level", "General anesthesia, which requires an anesthesia provider"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Moderate sedation is defined as a drug-induced depression of consciousness in which the client responds purposefully to verbal commands, alone or with light tactile stimulation, maintains a patent airway and adequate spontaneous ventilation without intervention, and usually maintains cardiovascular function - which describes this client exactly. Minimal sedation means responding normally to verbal commands, a lighter state than described. Deep sedation means the client cannot be easily aroused and responds only to repeated or painful stimulation, with possible airway compromise. General anesthesia is deeper still."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q23", "exam": 5, "topic": "RSI", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is preparing medications for a rapid sequence intubation. Which sequence reflects the correct order of administration?", "options": ["Analgesic, then paralytic, then induction agent", "Induction agent, then anti-anxiety medication, then paralytic", "Paralytic, then induction agent, then anti-anxiety medication", "Anti-anxiety medication, then induction agent, then paralytic"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Rapid sequence intubation proceeds in three steps: first an anti-anxiety medication such as midazolam, then an induction or sedation agent such as etomidate, propofol or ketamine, then a skeletal muscle paralytic such as succinylcholine, rocuronium or vecuronium, with analgesia such as fentanyl, morphine or hydromorphone also given. The order matters enormously because a paralytic given first would leave a fully awake client unable to breathe or move - an outcome that makes any sequence beginning with the paralytic dangerous."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q24", "exam": 5, "topic": "Propofol", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client is receiving propofol for sedation. Which nursing considerations apply? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Monitor closely for loss of respiratory rate", "Have an Ambu bag, suction, oxygen and a code cart available", "Check state regulatory guidelines regarding registered nurse bolus or IV push administration", "Expect the client to recall the procedure in detail afterward", "Be prepared for rapid intubation"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 4], "rationale": "Propofol causes respiratory depression along with bradycardia and hypotension, so the nurse monitors closely for loss of respiratory rate, keeps an Ambu bag, suction, oxygen and code cart at hand, and prepares for rapid intubation. Because some states do not permit registered nurses to give propofol by bolus or IV push, state regulatory guidelines must be checked. Propofol has a short half-life and clients typically do not remember the procedure, so expecting detailed recall misrepresents the drug and would lead to inaccurate client teaching."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q25", "exam": 5, "topic": "Reversal agents", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client who received midazolam and fentanyl for a procedure becomes unresponsive with a respiratory rate of 5. Which medications should the nurse have immediately available for reversal?", "options": ["Protamine sulfate and vitamin K", "Atropine and adenosine", "Flumazenil and naloxone", "Dantrolene and epinephrine"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Midazolam is a benzodiazepine, reversed by flumazenil, and fentanyl is an opioid, reversed by naloxone; both reversal agents are kept on hand during sedation, along with an Ambu bag, airway tray and access to vasopressors. Protamine sulfate reverses heparin and vitamin K reverses warfarin, which are anticoagulant reversals. Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia from the fluranes, and epinephrine is a resuscitation drug rather than a sedation reversal. Atropine and adenosine are used for bradycardia and supraventricular tachycardia."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q26", "exam": 5, "topic": "Intubation indications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "For which clients would the nurse anticipate endotracheal intubation? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A client who will receive paralytics and cannot breathe independently", "A client with ARDS and impaired gas exchange", "A client with neurologic impairment who cannot protect their airway", "A client with mild exertional dyspnea and an SpO2 of 95% on room air", "A client with copious secretions and ineffective ventilation"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 4], "rationale": "Intubation is indicated for neurologic impairment, increased secretions, impaired gas exchange and ineffective ventilation, as well as infection, the use of paralytics, and severe hypothyroid conditions such as myxedema coma. A client with mild exertional dyspnea and a normal saturation on room air is protecting their own airway and ventilating adequately, so intubation would expose them to the complications of an artificial airway without any benefit."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q27", "exam": 5, "topic": "Intubation contraindications", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with an unstable cervical spine fracture requires long-term airway management. Which approach should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Noninvasive BiPAP indefinitely regardless of the client's gas exchange", "Nasal cannula oxygen only, since no airway can be established", "Tracheostomy, because an unstable spine is a contraindication to endotracheal intubation", "Immediate oral endotracheal intubation with the neck fully extended"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "An unstable spine and an inability to open the mouth are listed contraindications to endotracheal intubation, and the alternative in those situations is tracheostomy. Oral intubation requires extending the head to visualize the cords, which is precisely the movement that could injure the cord in an unstable cervical fracture. Limiting the client to a nasal cannula abandons airway management in someone who needs it. Committing to indefinite BiPAP ignores the client's actual gas exchange and the fact that a definitive airway is required."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q28", "exam": 5, "topic": "Long-term intubation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client has been intubated for 12 days and remains ventilator dependent. What should the nurse anticipate discussing with the care team?", "options": ["Replacing the endotracheal tube with a new one of the same size every 2 weeks", "Immediate extubation regardless of weaning parameters", "Tracheostomy, since the maximum time for an endotracheal tube is about 10 to 14 days", "Permanent endotracheal intubation, since tracheostomy is reserved for home ventilation"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "The maximum time for an endotracheal tube is about 10 to 14 days; beyond that, longer-term airway management requires a tracheostomy, because prolonged translaryngeal intubation risks tracheal necrosis and injury. Routinely exchanging the endotracheal tube every 2 weeks would subject the client to repeated intubation, and reintubation is itself a major risk factor for ventilator-associated pneumonia. Extubating a ventilator-dependent client who does not meet weaning parameters would likely fail and require emergency reintubation. Endotracheal tubes are not a permanent solution."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q29", "exam": 5, "topic": "Ventilator complications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which complications should the nurse monitor for in a client receiving mechanical ventilation? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Hypercalcemia caused by positive pressure ventilation", "Barotrauma and auto-PEEP", "Improved chest wall compliance from the positive pressure", "Respiratory muscle atrophy leading to ventilator dependence", "Stress ulcer and gastritis"], "correct": [1, 3, 4], "rationale": "Mechanical ventilation complications include asynchrony or bucking, increased intrathoracic pressure that can produce a tension pneumothorax, barotrauma and auto-PEEP, muscle atrophy leading to ventilator dependence, gastric ulcer and gastritis, nosocomial infection and ventilator-associated pneumonia, tracheal necrosis and ICU delirium. Improved chest wall compliance is not a complication at all; positive pressure ventilation does not improve the mechanical properties of the chest wall, and in ARDS the lungs remain stiff despite ventilation. Positive pressure ventilation does not raise serum calcium; the pressure-related complications are barotrauma, auto-PEEP and the increased intrathoracic pressure that can produce a tension pneumothorax."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q30", "exam": 5, "topic": "Ulcer prophylaxis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A ventilated client is prescribed famotidine. When a family member asks why, which explanation by the nurse is correct?", "options": ["\"It reduces secretions so less suctioning is needed\"", "\"It helps the client wake up faster during the sedation vacation\"", "\"Ventilated clients are at risk for stress ulcers, and this medication helps prevent them\"", "\"It treats the pneumonia that caused the respiratory failure\""], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Gastric ulcer and gastritis are listed complications of mechanical ventilation, and ulcer prophylaxis with an H2 blocker or proton pump inhibitor is part of the prevention bundle precisely to prevent stress ulcer and mucosal necrosis. Famotidine is an acid-reducing agent, not an antibiotic, so it does not treat pneumonia. It does not reduce pulmonary secretions. It has no sedative-reversal properties and plays no role in how quickly a client awakens during a sedation vacation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q31", "exam": 5, "topic": "Oral care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse is prioritizing care for an intubated client. Which rationale best explains why frequent oral care is emphasized?", "options": ["Oral bacteria travel down the endotracheal tube, and poor oral care is a leading contributor to VAP", "Oral care is performed to assess for oral candidiasis from the antibiotics", "Oral care prevents the endotracheal tube cuff from deflating", "Oral care keeps the client comfortable but has no effect on infection rates"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Pathogens reach the lung through the endotracheal tube, and oral bacteria are one of the listed routes, with poor oral care identified as the leading contributor to ventilator-associated pneumonia - which is why frequent oral care, roughly every 1 to 2 hours, is a bundle element. Treating oral care as comfort only understates an intervention with a direct effect on infection rates. Assessing for candidiasis may happen during oral care but is not why it is prioritized. Oral care has no relationship to cuff inflation, which is maintained by monitoring cuff pressure."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q32", "exam": 5, "topic": "Restraints", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "An intubated client is intermittently reaching toward the endotracheal tube. Which approach should the nurse take first?", "options": ["Obtain an order for a continuous paralytic infusion", "Move the client to a room farther from the nurses' station to reduce stimulation", "Assess for hypoxia and pain, titrate sedation appropriately, and reorient the client", "Apply bilateral soft wrist restraints immediately"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Restraints are explicitly a last resort. The first approach is to assess for physiologic causes - agitation and restlessness in an intubated client should make the nurse think hypoxia - and to address pain, titrate sedation toward the target RASS and reorient and talk to the client, which also helps prevent delirium and ICU psychosis. Applying restraints first skips assessment of a potentially life-threatening cause. A continuous paralytic is a far more drastic intervention than this situation calls for and would require deep sedation alongside it. Moving the client away from observation reduces the monitoring a confused, intubated client needs."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q33", "exam": 5, "topic": "Delirium prevention", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which nursing actions help reduce the risk of delirium and ICU psychosis in a ventilated client? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Keep the room dark and silent at all hours so the client is never disturbed", "Maintain deep sedation continuously to prevent any awareness", "Talk to the client even when they cannot respond", "Perform daily sedation vacations", "Provide a communication board for a client with long-term intubation"], "correct": [2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Talking to the client, playing music, and providing a communication board for long-term intubation address the isolation and loss of control that contribute to delirium and ICU psychosis, and daily sedation vacations limit the cumulative sedative exposure that drives delirium while allowing assessment of neurologic status. Maintaining continuous deep sedation is the opposite of the recommended practice - it prevents sedation vacations and spontaneous breathing trials, prolongs ventilation, and sedative use is itself associated with the memory loss and cognitive problems seen after critical illness."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q34", "exam": 5, "topic": "Vent nursing care", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which interventions belong in the daily plan of care for a mechanically ventilated client? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Obtain arterial blood gases as ordered and troubleshoot alarms", "Provide chest physiotherapy", "Keep the client supine and flat to optimize tidal volume delivery", "Turn the client every 2 hours", "Perform inline suctioning as needed"], "correct": [0, 1, 3, 4], "rationale": "Ventilator care includes oral care, turning every 2 hours to prevent pressure injuries and promote rest, chest physiotherapy, obtaining arterial blood gases, troubleshooting alarms, and inline or Ballard suctioning as needed. Keeping the client supine and flat directly conflicts with the ventilator-associated pneumonia bundle, which requires the head of the bed at 30 degrees or higher to prevent aspiration of oral and gastric contents."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q35", "exam": 5, "topic": "Asynchrony", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A ventilated client is bucking the ventilator, with frequent high pressure alarms and visible distress. Vital signs show HR 124, BP 158/92, SpO2 90%. After confirming tube position and suctioning the airway, which question should guide the nurse's next step?", "options": ["Should the client be extubated immediately?", "Should the tidal volume be reduced to 4 mL/kg?", "Should the ventilator alarm limits be widened so the alarms stop?", "Is the sedation adequate for this client?"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Asynchrony, or bucking, is a listed complication, and the question the nurse asks is whether sedation is adequate - the client is fighting the machine, which raises airway pressures and worsens oxygenation. Having already ruled out displacement and obstruction, inadequate sedation is the logical next consideration. Dropping the tidal volume to 4 mL/kg is not the described response to asynchrony. Extubating a distressed, hypoxemic client who is fighting the ventilator removes the support they need. Widening alarm limits silences the warning without addressing the problem, which is unsafe."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q36", "exam": 5, "topic": "Pre-sedation assessment", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "During a pre-sedation assessment, the nurse identifies that the client has obstructive sleep apnea and uses CPAP nightly. Why is this finding significant?", "options": ["Sleep apnea has no bearing on sedation because the client will be monitored", "The greatest concern during sedation is losing the airway, so the team may opt for full intubation instead", "Sleep apnea means the client cannot receive any analgesia during the procedure", "The client will require a higher sedative dose to achieve the same effect"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The biggest worry during sedation is a client with a history of sleep apnea and the risk of losing the airway, which is why the team may decide on full intubation rather than procedural sedation. Dismissing sleep apnea because monitoring is in place ignores that monitoring detects a lost airway but does not prevent it. Sleep apnea does not create sedative tolerance requiring higher doses; if anything these clients are more sensitive to respiratory depression. Analgesia is not withheld from clients with sleep apnea - it is given with careful monitoring and preparation for airway support."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q37", "exam": 5, "topic": "STOP-BANG", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse screens a client with the STOP-BANG tool and calculates a score of 6. How should the nurse interpret and act on this result?", "options": ["High risk for obstructive sleep apnea; communicate the result and prepare for possible airway compromise", "The score is not interpretable without a sleep study", "Moderate risk; no change to the sedation plan is needed", "Low risk; proceed with standard sedation monitoring"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "STOP-BANG screens for obstructive sleep apnea using snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure meaning hypertension, body mass index over 35, age over 50, neck circumference over 40 cm and male gender; a score of 0 to 2 is low risk, 3 to 4 is moderate and 5 to 8 is high. A score of 6 is high risk and must be communicated, with preparation for airway compromise during sedation. Calling 6 low or moderate misreads the scale. The tool is designed as a bedside screen precisely so that risk can be identified without waiting for a sleep study."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q38", "exam": 5, "topic": "ASA score", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client scheduled for a procedure has severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life. Which American Society of Anesthesiology physical status classification applies?", "options": ["ASA 2", "ASA 4", "ASA 3", "ASA 5"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The ASA physical status scale runs from 1, a healthy client, to 2, mild systemic disease, to 3, severe systemic disease that is not a constant threat to life, to 4, severe disease that is a constant threat to life, to 5, a moribund client. Severe disease constituting a constant threat to life is ASA 4. ASA 2 describes mild disease. ASA 3 is severe disease that is not a constant threat - the distinction between 3 and 4 rests entirely on whether the disease constantly threatens life. ASA 5 describes a moribund client who is not expected to survive without the procedure."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q39", "exam": 5, "topic": "Anesthetic selection", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A hemodynamically unstable client in septic shock requires emergent intubation. Which induction agent consideration is most relevant?", "options": ["Nitrous oxide should be used because it is highly potent", "Etomidate provides cardiovascular stability but should not be used in septic shock", "Ketamine must be avoided because it causes respiratory depression", "Propofol is preferred because it raises the blood pressure"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Etomidate is valued for cardiovascular stability, but it carries the specific caution that it cannot be used in septic shock. Propofol causes hypotension along with respiratory depression and bradycardia, so it lowers rather than raises blood pressure and is a poor choice in an unstable client. Ketamine is actually the agent noted as potent without respiratory depression and good for the hemodynamically unstable client, though it raises heart rate and cardiac output and can cause hallucinations and nightmares. Nitrous oxide is described as low potency and is a minimal sedation agent, not an induction agent for emergent intubation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q40", "exam": 5, "topic": "Anesthetic complications", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client receiving a volatile inhaled anesthetic develops a rapidly rising temperature, muscle rigidity and tachycardia. Which medication should the nurse prepare?", "options": ["Flumazenil", "Dantrolene", "Naloxone", "Protamine sulfate"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The fluranes - desflurane, isoflurane and sevoflurane - are potent agents whose characteristic adverse effect is malignant hyperthermia, treated with dantrolene. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and naloxone reverses opioids; neither addresses a hypermetabolic crisis triggered by an inhaled anesthetic. Protamine sulfate reverses heparin and has no role here. Recognizing the drug class is what points to the antidote, since the presentation of rising temperature with rigidity is specific to this reaction."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q41", "exam": 5, "topic": "Conscious sedation medications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which statements about medications used for conscious sedation are accurate? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Propofol has a short half-life and clients generally do not remember the procedure", "Midazolam is a benzodiazepine used as an anxiolytic and sedative", "Succinylcholine and rocuronium produce skeletal muscle paralysis", "Fentanyl, morphine and hydromorphone are opioids used for analgesia", "Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and naloxone reverses opioids"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Midazolam is a benzodiazepine anxiolytic and sedative reversed by flumazenil; fentanyl, morphine and hydromorphone are opioid analgesics reversed by naloxone; propofol is a sedative with a short half-life after which clients typically do not recall the procedure; and succinylcholine, rocuronium and vecuronium are the paralytics that produce skeletal muscle relaxation. The claim that benzodiazepines provide analgesia is false and clinically dangerous - they sedate without relieving pain, which is why opioids are given alongside them. The reversal pairings are also accurate and are the reason both agents are kept on hand during sedation, so every statement listed here is correct."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q42", "exam": 5, "topic": "Sedation monitoring", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which items must be in place before the nurse begins administering moderate procedural sedation? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Cardiac monitor with heart rate, blood pressure and SpO2 monitoring", "Post-procedure orders prepared in advance", "Naloxone, an Ambu bag and an airway tray immediately available", "Access to vasopressors if needed", "Reliable IV access with IV fluids"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Before sedation the nurse ensures a cardiac monitor with heart rate, blood pressure and oxygen saturation monitoring; IV fluids and reliable IV access; naloxone on hand with an Ambu bag and airway tray; access to vasopressors; all IV lines labeled and multiple pumps programmed correctly; and intake and output, airway and sedation level monitored throughout with recovery and documentation per policy. Having post-procedure orders prepared in advance is emphasized so that pain and other needs can be met without delay once the client wakes. Every option listed is a required element of safe preparation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q43", "exam": 5, "topic": "Pain assessment", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which tools and parameters are appropriate for assessing adequacy of pain control after sedation? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Wong-Baker faces pain rating scale", "The amount of sedative the client received during the procedure", "Vital signs and change in the client's condition", "Numerical rating scale or visual analog scale", "Level of activity and ability to ambulate"], "correct": [0, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Tools for assessing acute pain control include the visual analog scale, verbal rating scale, numerical rating scale, faces pain scale and Wong-Baker faces scale, supplemented by vital signs, any change in the client's condition, and the level of activity and ambulation the client can tolerate. The dose of sedative given during the procedure is not a measure of current pain - sedation and analgesia are distinct, and a client can be deeply sedated and still in pain, which is why pain is assessed directly."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q44", "exam": 5, "topic": "Scope of practice", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A newly hired nurse will be administering moderate procedural sedation in an endoscopy unit. Which action is most important before the nurse assumes this role?", "options": ["Demonstrate clinical competency and confirm the requirements of the state board of nursing", "Review the manufacturer's package insert for each medication only", "Complete a course in advanced cardiac life support as the sole requirement", "Obtain a prescription from the provider allowing sedation administration"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Registered nurses providing moderate procedural sedation and analgesia must demonstrate clinical competency and have a working knowledge of their state board of nursing requirements - an important point because some states do not allow registered nurses to give propofol by bolus or IV push. Reading package inserts is useful but does not establish competency or legal authority. A provider's prescription authorizes a medication but cannot extend a nurse's scope of practice beyond what the state board permits. Advanced cardiac life support certification is valuable but is not described as the sole requirement."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q45", "exam": 5, "topic": "Minimal sedation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client is to receive nitrous oxide during a dental procedure. Which effects should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Euphoria, relaxation, calmness and possible fits of laughter, with the client responding normally to verbal commands", "Profound analgesia with no alteration in mental status", "Skeletal muscle paralysis requiring mechanical ventilation", "Complete loss of consciousness requiring airway support"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Nitrous oxide is a minimal sedation agent commonly used in dental procedures; it slows the brain and the body's responses and produces euphoria, relaxation, calmness and fits of giggles and laughter, while minimal sedation by definition means the client responds normally to verbal commands. Loss of consciousness with airway compromise describes deep sedation or general anesthesia, a different level entirely. Paralysis requires a neuromuscular blocking agent, which nitrous oxide is not. Nitrous oxide does alter mental status, which is the basis of its sedative effect, and it is described as low potency."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q46", "exam": 5, "topic": "Tidal volume", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The provider asks the nurse to report the setting that determines how much air is pushed into the lungs with each breath. Which setting should the nurse report?", "options": ["Pressure support", "Tidal volume", "FiO2", "Rate"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Tidal volume is the amount of air delivered with each breath - the volume pushed in per breath. The rate sets how many breaths per minute the ventilator delivers. FiO2 is the fraction of inspired oxygen, the percentage of oxygen in the delivered gas. Pressure support augments the client's own spontaneous breaths. Each is a distinct setting, and reporting the wrong one could lead to a change that does not address the problem the provider is trying to correct."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q47", "exam": 5, "topic": "Suctioning technique", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A nurse notes that secretions are consistently harder to clear from a ventilated client's left lung. Which understanding explains this?", "options": ["The left lung is harder to suction because of the angle at which the left bronchus leaves the trachea", "Secretions never collect in the left lung in supine clients", "The left lung has fewer lobes, so secretions cannot be reached by any catheter", "The left lung is smaller and therefore produces no secretions"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "The left lung is noted to be harder to suction, which reflects the anatomy of the bronchial tree: the right mainstem bronchus leaves the trachea at a more vertical angle, so a suction catheter and aspirated material both tend to pass to the right. This is the same anatomy that makes right mainstem intubation the common malposition. The left lung has two lobes rather than three but that does not make it unreachable. Secretions do collect in the left lung, and asserting otherwise would lead the nurse to overlook them. A smaller lung still produces secretions."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q48", "exam": 5, "topic": "Intubation equipment", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is gathering equipment for an emergent intubation. Which items are required? Select all that apply.", "options": ["IV access with medications including propofol, paralytics and epinephrine", "Working suction equipment", "A chest drainage system with sterile water", "Endotracheal tubes in several sizes and an Ambu bag", "An end-tidal carbon dioxide detector and a stethoscope"], "correct": [0, 1, 3, 4], "rationale": "Intubation equipment includes endotracheal tubes in several sizes, an Ambu bag, suction, intravenous access for fluids and medications, a stethoscope, an end-tidal carbon dioxide detector, the ventilator, and medications such as propofol, paralytics and epinephrine, with an interdisciplinary team of a provider, respiratory therapist, rapid response team and at least two nurses. A chest drainage system is equipment for a chest tube; it belongs at the bedside of a client with a pleural collection, not on the intubation setup."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q49", "exam": 5, "topic": "Documentation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Immediately after a client is intubated and placement is confirmed, which documentation is essential?", "options": ["The client's preferred sleeping position", "The total time the provider required to intubate", "The brand of laryngoscope blade used", "The tube size and the depth at which it is marked at the teeth or lip"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The size and placement of the endotracheal tube are documented, with the tube marked where it touches the teeth or lip, so that any subsequent migration can be detected at a glance by comparing the current depth with the documented one. The laryngoscope blade brand does not affect ongoing care. Sleeping position is irrelevant for a client whose position is now dictated by the ventilator-associated pneumonia bundle and pressure injury prevention. The duration of the intubation attempt may be noted in the procedure record but is not the information that guides every subsequent shift assessment."},
  {"id": "n504e2s5q50", "exam": 5, "topic": "Communication", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client who has been intubated for 8 days is alert and repeatedly mouthing words and gesturing with evident frustration. Which nursing action is most appropriate?", "options": ["Explain that communication is not possible until the tube is removed", "Provide a communication board and ask yes-or-no questions", "Tell the family to speak for the client during all care discussions", "Increase the sedation so the client is less distressed by the inability to speak"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "A communication board is specifically identified for clients with long-term intubation, and pairing it with yes-or-no questions gives an alert client a workable way to express needs, which also reduces the frustration that contributes to delirium. Telling the client communication is impossible abandons an alert person to isolation. Sedating a client to manage the nurse's communication difficulty removes autonomy, prolongs ventilation and works against sedation vacations and weaning. Having family speak for an alert, competent client substitutes someone else's voice for their own."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q01", "exam": 6, "topic": "CKD staging", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client's glomerular filtration rate has declined from 38 to 12 mL/min over the past year. How should the nurse interpret this change?", "options": ["The client has progressed to stage 5, end-stage renal disease, and kidney replacement therapy should be discussed", "The client's kidney function has improved and monitoring can be reduced", "The client has progressed to stage 4 and should begin peritoneal dialysis today", "The client remains in stage 3 and needs only dietary modification"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Normal glomerular filtration rate is 90 to 120 mL/min. Staging runs from stage 1 above 90, stage 2 at 60 to 89, stage 3A at 45 to 59, stage 3B at 30 to 44, stage 4 at 15 to 29, and stage 5, end-stage renal disease, below 15. A rate of 12 is below 15, which is stage 5 - the kidneys are no longer able to function and kidney replacement therapy must be discussed. A rate of 38 would have been stage 3B, so the client has moved well past stage 3. Stage 4 spans 15 to 29, which 12 falls below. A falling rate reflects worsening rather than improving function."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q02", "exam": 6, "topic": "CKD causes", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is teaching a community group about preventing chronic kidney disease. Which two conditions should the nurse emphasize as the main causes leading to dialysis or transplant?", "options": ["Hypothyroidism and anemia", "Hypertension and diabetes mellitus", "Osteoarthritis and gout", "Asthma and seasonal allergies"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Hypertension and diabetes mellitus are identified as the main causes of chronic kidney disease leading to dialysis or transplant, and both appear on the risk factor list along with heart problems or stroke, family history, obesity, smoking, age 60 or older and a history of acute kidney injury. Asthma and allergies are respiratory conditions without a direct role in nephron destruction. Osteoarthritis and gout can involve medication effects on the kidney but are not the main causes. Hypothyroidism and anemia are associated conditions - anemia in fact results from chronic kidney disease through loss of erythropoietin - rather than causes of it."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q03", "exam": 6, "topic": "Kidney function", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with advanced chronic kidney disease has multiple abnormalities. Which of these result directly from loss of normal kidney functions? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Respiratory alkalosis from loss of carbon dioxide regulation", "Anemia", "Elevated serum amylase from pancreatic injury", "Hypocalcemia with hyperphosphatemia", "Metabolic acidosis"], "correct": [1, 3, 4], "rationale": "The kidneys maintain acid-base balance, water and electrolyte balance, toxin and waste removal, blood pressure control, erythropoietin production and vitamin D metabolism. Losing these produces metabolic acidosis because hydrogen ions cannot be excreted, anemia because erythropoietin is not produced, and hypocalcemia with hyperphosphatemia because vitamin D is not synthesized for calcium absorption. Severe hypertension follows from dysfunction of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, in which reduced renal blood flow triggers renin, angiotensin constricts vessels and aldosterone causes sodium and water reabsorption. An elevated amylase from pancreatic injury is not a consequence of kidney failure. The acid-base disturbance of kidney failure is metabolic acidosis, because hydrogen ions cannot be excreted; the lungs then compensate with Kussmaul respirations, so any respiratory change is compensation rather than a primary alkalosis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q04", "exam": 6, "topic": "Uremia", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which findings in a client with chronic kidney disease are classic signs of uremia? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Polycythemia with an elevated hemoglobin", "Fatigue with weight loss and anorexia", "Nausea and vomiting", "Pruritus", "Polyuria with dilute urine"], "correct": [1, 2, 3], "rationale": "Uremia means the kidneys cannot filter waste from the blood, so urea and creatinine accumulate - literally urine in the blood. The classic signs are nausea, vomiting, fatigue, weight loss and anorexia, muscle cramps, pruritus and a change in mental status. Polyuria with dilute urine is not a sign of uremia in advanced disease; as function declines the client typically develops decreasing urinary output, and oliguria is the finding that signals worsening function. Polycythemia is the opposite of what occurs: the failing kidney stops producing erythropoietin, so these clients develop anemia with a low hemoglobin and hematocrit."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q05", "exam": 6, "topic": "Kussmaul respirations", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with end-stage renal disease is breathing deeply and rapidly. Arterial blood gases show pH 7.25, PaCO2 29 mm Hg, HCO3 13 mEq/L. How should the nurse interpret this?", "options": ["Respiratory acidosis from fluid overload in the lungs", "Primary respiratory alkalosis from anxiety about the diagnosis", "Kussmaul respirations compensating for metabolic acidosis caused by the failing kidneys", "A normal breathing pattern for a client on dialysis"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "In chronic kidney disease the kidneys cannot remove hydrogen ions because ammonium and bicarbonate are needed for that process, so metabolic acidosis develops; the respiratory system compensates with Kussmaul respirations, deep and rapid breaths that blow off carbon dioxide. The low pH with a low bicarbonate confirms metabolic acidosis and the low PaCO2 confirms respiratory compensation. If anxiety were causing a primary respiratory alkalosis, the pH would be elevated rather than acidotic. Respiratory acidosis would show an elevated PaCO2. A pH of 7.25 is never a normal finding."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q06", "exam": 6, "topic": "CKD laboratory", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "The nurse reviews laboratory results for a client with stage 5 chronic kidney disease. Which pattern is most consistent with this diagnosis?", "options": ["Elevated BUN and creatinine, potassium 5.9, phosphorus 5.8, calcium 7.6, hemoglobin 9.2", "Elevated BUN and creatinine, potassium 2.9, phosphorus 1.8, calcium 11.4", "Normal BUN and creatinine, potassium 5.9, phosphorus 5.8, hemoglobin 9.2", "Normal BUN and creatinine, potassium 3.2, calcium 10.8, hemoglobin 15.8"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "End-stage renal disease produces elevated BUN and creatinine from retained waste, hyperkalemia because potassium excretion falls, hyperphosphatemia with hypocalcemia because vitamin D is not synthesized and phosphorus is retained, and a low hemoglobin and hematocrit from the anemia of reduced erythropoietin. The pattern of elevated BUN and creatinine with a potassium of 5.9, phosphorus of 5.8, calcium of 7.6 and hemoglobin of 9.2 shows every one of these. Normal BUN and creatinine with a high calcium and normal hemoglobin describes intact kidney function. Elevated waste products paired with hypokalemia, low phosphorus and high calcium reverses the expected electrolyte directions. Normal BUN and creatinine cannot accompany stage 5 disease, since the retention of those wastes is what defines it."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q07", "exam": 6, "topic": "ESRD diet", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with end-stage renal disease is learning about dietary restrictions. Which foods should the nurse teach the client to avoid? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Apples and green beans", "White bread and rice", "Potatoes and tomatoes", "Oranges and bananas", "Processed meats and soft drinks"], "correct": [2, 3, 4], "rationale": "The end-stage renal disease diet avoids foods high in potassium such as oranges, bananas, potatoes, tomatoes and brown rice; foods high in phosphorus such as processed meats, dairy and soft drinks; deep-fried foods and those high in saturated and trans fats; large amounts of meat and poultry; and prepared or packaged foods, which are typically high in sodium. Apples and green beans are among the fresh fruits and vegetables specifically recommended, along with apples, peaches and carrots, so they are encouraged rather than avoided. White bread, pasta, rice and wheat cereals are specifically recommended as low-potassium, low-phosphorus starches, so they are encouraged rather than restricted."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q08", "exam": 6, "topic": "ESRD diet", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with end-stage renal disease selects a lunch tray. Which selection indicates the client understands the dietary teaching?", "options": ["White bread sandwich with a small portion of tofu, rice and an apple", "Baked potato with cheese sauce and a banana", "Processed deli meat sandwich with tomato soup and a cola", "Fried chicken with brown rice and a glass of milk"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "White bread, rice and other low-potassium, low-phosphorus starches are recommended, along with moderate amounts of protein from plant-based options such as tofu, nut butter and soy yogurt, and fresh low-potassium fruits such as apples. A baked potato and a banana are both high-potassium foods to avoid, and cheese adds phosphorus. Fried chicken is a deep-fried food and a large portion of poultry, brown rice is high in potassium and milk is high in phosphorus. Processed deli meat is high in phosphorus and sodium, tomato is high in potassium and cola is a high-phosphorus soft drink."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q09", "exam": 6, "topic": "Emergent dialysis", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which findings would indicate that a client needs emergent hemodialysis? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Ingestion of a dialyzable toxin", "Severe metabolic acidosis with a pH below 7.2 unresponsive to therapy", "Irreversible acute kidney failure when other therapies are ineffective", "Fluid overload with pulmonary edema that does not respond to diuretics", "Symptomatic hyperkalemia with ECG changes"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Emergent dialysis is indicated for severe metabolic acidosis that is below a pH of 7.2 or does not respond to therapy, fluid overload unresponsive to diuretics including pulmonary edema or pericarditis, symptomatic hyperkalemia with ECG changes, toxin ingestion from a dialyzable drug overdose or poisoning, and irreversible acute kidney failure when other therapies are ineffective. A stable glomerular filtration rate of 28 with no symptoms places the client in stage 4 and calls for continued conservative management and planning, not emergency treatment. Irreversible acute kidney failure when other therapies are unacceptable or ineffective is the fifth listed indication, so all of these findings call for emergent treatment."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q10", "exam": 6, "topic": "AV fistula", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client has a newly matured arteriovenous fistula in the left forearm. Which nursing actions are correct? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Post a sign that no blood pressures are to be taken in the left arm", "Teach the client to avoid heavy lifting with the left arm", "Assess distal pulses and capillary refill in the left hand", "Palpate for a thrill and auscultate for a bruit over the access", "Avoid venipunctures and IV insertions in the left arm"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Protecting the fistula is central: the nurse palpates for a thrill and auscultates for a bruit to confirm patency, prohibits blood pressure measurement and venipuncture or IV placement in that extremity because both can clot the access, and assesses distal circulation through pulses, capillary refill, warmth and color since the anastomosis can reduce flow to the hand. The client is also taught to avoid heavy lifting with that arm and to avoid sleeping with full body weight on it. A snug elastic bandage would compress the access and risk thrombosis, which is the very outcome the other measures are designed to prevent. Teaching the client to avoid heavy lifting with that arm, and to avoid sleeping with full body weight on it, protects the access in the same way, so all five actions belong in the plan."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q11", "exam": 6, "topic": "Vascular access", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with end-stage renal disease needs to begin hemodialysis this week but has no permanent access. Which access should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["An arteriovenous graft, which can be used immediately after placement", "An arteriovenous fistula, which can be used immediately after surgery", "A peripheral IV in the forearm, which provides adequate flow for dialysis", "A temporary dialysis catheter, which can be used immediately after insertion and x-ray confirmation"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "A temporary dialysis catheter has separate lumens for blood outflow and inflow and is usually placed in the subclavian, internal jugular or femoral vein; it can be used immediately after insertion and x-ray confirmation, which is what a client starting dialysis this week requires. An arteriovenous fistula is an internal anastomosis of an artery to a vein and cannot be used for 2 to 3 months while it matures. An arteriovenous graft uses looped plastic tubing tunneled under the skin and requires 1 to 3 weeks. A peripheral IV cannot support the blood flow rates hemodialysis requires."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q12", "exam": 6, "topic": "Vascular access", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client is scheduled for creation of an arteriovenous graft. The client asks how soon it can be used. Which response is accurate?", "options": ["\"A graft takes 2 to 3 months to be ready, like a fistula\"", "\"A graft is never used for dialysis; only fistulas are\"", "\"A graft can be used immediately after the surgery\"", "\"A graft can usually be used 1 to 3 weeks after it is placed\""], "correct": [3], "rationale": "An arteriovenous graft is looped plastic tubing tunneled beneath the skin connecting an artery to a vein, usually in the forearm, upper arm or inner thigh, and it can be used 1 to 3 weeks after surgery. Immediate use describes a dialysis catheter, not a graft. The 2 to 3 month wait applies to a fistula, which requires the vein itself to enlarge and thicken. Grafts are one of the two permanent access types, so saying they are never used is simply incorrect and would mislead a client making an access decision."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q13", "exam": 6, "topic": "Disequilibrium syndrome", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "During the first hemodialysis session for a client with a markedly elevated BUN, the client develops a headache, nausea, restlessness and confusion. What should the nurse suspect?", "options": ["Air embolism from the dialysis circuit", "Disequilibrium syndrome", "An allergic reaction to the dialysate", "Hypoglycemia from the dextrose in the dialysate"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Disequilibrium syndrome is characterized by mental status changes and can include seizures or coma, with symptoms of nausea, vomiting, headache, fatigue, hypotension and restlessness. It is most likely during initial treatments in a client with a very high BUN, as rapid solute removal creates an osmotic gradient. An allergic reaction would typically involve rash, bronchospasm or hypotension with other allergic features. Air embolism produces sudden respiratory distress and chest pain. The dialysate contains dextrose among its constituents, so hypoglycemia is not the expected consequence of exposure to it."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q14", "exam": 6, "topic": "Hemodialysis complications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which complications should the nurse monitor for in a client receiving hemodialysis? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Hypotension and hemodynamic instability", "Hyperkalemia immediately following the treatment", "Muscle cramps and back pain", "Peritonitis from contamination of the dialysate", "Hypercalcemia from the dialysate"], "correct": [0, 2], "rationale": "Hemodialysis complications include disequilibrium syndrome, muscle cramps and back pain, headache, itching, hemodynamic and cardiac adverse events, infection, increased risk for intracranial hemorrhage because heparin is used to prevent clotting in the circuit, anemia, and access site complications of bleeding, infection and clotting. Hypercalcemia is not a listed complication; clients with chronic kidney disease typically have hypocalcemia with hyperphosphatemia, and the dialysate is formulated to resemble plasma rather than to raise calcium to toxic levels. Hyperkalemia is what dialysis corrects - potassium falls rather than rises after a treatment, and it is a missed session that produces dangerous accumulation. Peritonitis is a complication of peritoneal dialysis, where dialysate enters the abdomen; hemodialysis uses a vascular access and an external circuit, so the peritoneum is never involved."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q15", "exam": 6, "topic": "Anticoagulation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client receiving heparin during hemodialysis develops bleeding from the access site that does not stop with pressure. Which medication should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Flumazenil", "Naloxone", "Protamine sulfate", "Vitamin K"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Anticoagulation with heparin is maintained during hemodialysis to prevent clots in the circuit, and the reversal agent for heparin is protamine sulfate. Vitamin K reverses warfarin, which is a different anticoagulant with a different mechanism. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and naloxone reverses opioids; neither affects coagulation. Pairing the correct antidote with the correct drug matters because giving vitamin K to a client bleeding from heparin would leave the heparin effect entirely unopposed."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q16", "exam": 6, "topic": "Dialysis care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client receives hemodialysis on a Monday, Wednesday and Friday schedule and takes several daily medications. Which nursing action is most appropriate?", "options": ["Hold all medications on dialysis days", "Coordinate with the nephrologist to determine which medications should be held before dialysis and given after", "Give all medications at the usual times regardless of the dialysis schedule", "Double the dose of all medications on dialysis days to compensate for removal"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Because dialysis removes water-soluble drugs and some medications would be cleared before they could act, the nurse coordinates with the nephrologist to determine which drugs can be given before and after dialysis - antihypertensives in particular are often held, since clients are closely monitored for hypotension during treatment. Giving everything on the usual schedule ignores drug removal and hypotension risk. Holding all medications would withhold drugs the client needs. Doubling doses without an order is outside nursing scope and could produce toxicity in a client who cannot excrete the excess."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q17", "exam": 6, "topic": "Post-dialysis assessment", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which assessments should the nurse perform after a client's hemodialysis session? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Inspection of the peritoneal catheter exit site for drainage", "Weight compared with the pre-dialysis weight", "Serum electrolytes and whether the BUN has been reduced", "Mental status", "Vital signs, particularly checking for hypotension"], "correct": [1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "After dialysis the nurse monitors vital signs with particular attention to hypotension from fluid removal, daily weights and intake and output to evaluate the volume removed, electrolytes including potassium and sodium and the client's dietary intake of each, mental status because of the risk of disequilibrium syndrome, and whether the BUN has fallen as evidence the treatment was effective. Every one of these parameters is part of the post-dialysis evaluation."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q18", "exam": 6, "topic": "Missed dialysis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client on hemodialysis missed two consecutive sessions. Which assessment finding should concern the nurse most?", "options": ["A hemoglobin of 10.4 g/dL", "Peaked T waves on the cardiac monitor with a potassium of 6.8 mEq/L", "A 1-pound weight gain since the last recorded weight", "A blood pressure of 138/82"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "When a session is missed there is a high risk of accumulating electrolytes and waste in the blood, particularly potassium, and symptomatic hyperkalemia with ECG changes is one of the indications for emergent dialysis. Peaked T waves with a potassium of 6.8 is an immediate threat of lethal dysrhythmia. A 1-pound weight gain is minimal for a client who has missed two treatments and would not be the priority. A blood pressure of 138/82 is relatively controlled for a client with end-stage renal disease. A hemoglobin of 10.4 reflects the expected anemia of chronic kidney disease and is not an emergency."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q19", "exam": 6, "topic": "CRRT", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A hemodynamically unstable client in the intensive care unit on vasopressors requires kidney replacement therapy. Which therapy should the nurse anticipate and why?", "options": ["Standard hemodialysis, because it clears wastes fastest", "Continuous renal replacement therapy, because it removes solutes and fluid continuously and is better tolerated in critical illness", "No therapy, because kidney replacement is contraindicated with vasopressors", "Peritoneal dialysis, because it can be done on any medical-surgical unit"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Continuous renal replacement therapy is used for critically ill clients, is performed only in the intensive care setting, requires venous access in the internal jugular, subclavian or femoral vein, uses extracorporeal circulation through a small-volume low-resistance filter, and provides continuous removal of solutes and fluid - a gradual approach suited to an unstable client. Standard hemodialysis does clear wastes more efficiently in a shorter time, but that rapid fluid and solute shift is poorly tolerated by a client already requiring vasopressors. Peritoneal dialysis is not the described therapy for this situation, and continuous therapy is confined to the ICU rather than available on any unit. Withholding therapy entirely is not appropriate."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q20", "exam": 6, "topic": "HD versus PD", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is deciding between hemodialysis and peritoneal dialysis. Which statement accurately reflects an advantage of peritoneal dialysis?", "options": ["It eliminates all dietary and fluid considerations", "It clears wastes more efficiently and takes less time overall", "It offers a flexible schedule for exchanges with few hemodynamic changes during and after treatment", "It requires no training because it is performed entirely by staff"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Peritoneal dialysis offers a flexible schedule for exchanges and produces few hemodynamic changes during and following exchanges, which is a major advantage for clients who tolerate the blood pressure swings of hemodialysis poorly. More efficient clearance of wastes in a shorter duration is the advantage of hemodialysis, not peritoneal dialysis. Peritoneal dialysis requires extensive client teaching in aseptic technique precisely because the client performs it. No form of kidney replacement therapy eliminates dietary and fluid considerations."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q21", "exam": 6, "topic": "PD contraindications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which conditions are contraindications to peritoneal dialysis? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Severe hemodynamic instability requiring vasopressors", "Active inflammatory gastrointestinal disease", "Serious bleeding disorders", "Extensive peritoneal adhesions or fibrosis", "Ascites or massive central obesity"], "correct": [1, 3, 4], "rationale": "Peritoneal dialysis is contraindicated with extensive peritoneal adhesions, fibrosis or active inflammatory gastrointestinal disease, with ascites or massive central obesity, and after recent abdominal surgery - all conditions that compromise the peritoneal membrane or the ability to instill and drain dialysate safely. Serious bleeding disorders are a contraindication to hemodialysis rather than peritoneal dialysis, because hemodialysis requires systemic anticoagulation; in fact peritoneal dialysis is specifically indicated for clients who cannot tolerate anticoagulation. Hemodynamic instability is a contraindication to hemodialysis, not peritoneal dialysis; peritoneal dialysis is actually favored in unstable clients because it produces few hemodynamic changes during and after exchanges."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q22", "exam": 6, "topic": "PD indications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "For which clients is peritoneal dialysis particularly indicated? Select all that apply.", "options": ["A client with vascular access problems", "A client with extensive abdominal adhesions from multiple surgeries", "A client who is unstable and cannot tolerate anticoagulation", "A client waiting for an arteriovenous fistula to mature", "A client who wants more flexibility in their daily activities"], "correct": [0, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Peritoneal dialysis is indicated for clients who are unstable and cannot tolerate anticoagulation, those with vascular access problems, those waiting for a functional fistula or graft, and those who want more flexibility in their activities. Extensive abdominal adhesions are a contraindication rather than an indication, because adhesions interfere with instilling and draining dialysate across a functional peritoneal surface."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q23", "exam": 6, "topic": "PD procedure", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse is describing one peritoneal dialysis exchange to a client. Which sequence is correct?", "options": ["A continuous simultaneous infusion and drainage with no dwell period", "Infusion of 1 to 2 L of dialysate by gravity, a prescribed dwell time, then drainage by gravity into a bag", "Drainage of effluent, then infusion of dialysate, then a dwell period", "Infusion of dialysate under pressure from a pump, immediate drainage, then a rest period"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "One exchange consists of three phases: infusion or fill, in which 1 to 2 L of dialysate is infused by gravity over 10 to 20 minutes according to the client's tolerance; dwell, in which the fluid remains in the peritoneal cavity for a time prescribed individually by the nephrologist; and outflow or drain, in which the peritoneal effluent flows out by gravity into a drainage bag. Starting with drainage reverses the order. The fluid is instilled by gravity rather than pumped under pressure, and a dwell period is essential because diffusion and osmosis across the peritoneal membrane take time. Simultaneous continuous infusion and drainage would allow no equilibration at all."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q24", "exam": 6, "topic": "PD nursing care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Before initiating a peritoneal dialysis exchange, the nurse warms the dialysate. What is the rationale for this action?", "options": ["Warming promotes client comfort and supports dilation of the peritoneal vessels for exchange", "Warming increases the dextrose concentration of the solution", "Warming prevents the catheter from becoming occluded by fibrin", "Warming sterilizes the dialysate before instillation"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Warming the dialysate is listed among the preparatory steps before an exchange, along with assessing baseline vital signs, weight and electrolytes. Instilling cold fluid into the peritoneal cavity causes cramping and discomfort, and warmed fluid supports the vasodilation that aids exchange across the peritoneal membrane. Warming does not sterilize the solution - sterility comes from aseptic preparation and handling, which is why aseptic technique teaching is emphasized. Warming does not alter the dextrose concentration, which is fixed by the prescribed solution. Fibrin occlusion is not prevented by temperature."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q25", "exam": 6, "topic": "PD fluid balance", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A nurse instills 2,000 mL of dialysate and drains 1,600 mL at the end of the dwell. How should this be recorded and interpreted?", "options": ["The client should immediately receive a diuretic to clear the retained fluid", "The 400 mL difference is counted as fluid intake, and the nurse should assess for causes of poor outflow", "The exchange was successful and the difference should be disregarded", "The 400 mL difference is counted as output, and no further action is needed"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "After a peritoneal dialysis exchange the nurse measures the total outflow, and when the outflow is less than the inflow the difference is counted as fluid intake, because that volume has been retained in the client. A 400 mL deficit also prompts assessment for causes of poor flow - constipation is the usual culprit, so high-fiber diets and stool softeners are encouraged, and positioning is checked to facilitate inflow and outflow. Recording retained fluid as output would falsify the balance in the wrong direction. Disregarding the difference misses both the fluid retention and its cause. A diuretic is not the remedy for retained dialysate in a client whose kidneys have failed."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q26", "exam": 6, "topic": "Peritonitis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client on peritoneal dialysis reports periumbilical tenderness. The nurse notes the drained effluent is cloudy and opaque. What is the nurse's priority action?", "options": ["Send a specimen of the effluent for culture and sensitivity and notify the provider", "Flush the catheter with sterile saline to clear the sediment", "Increase the dwell time to allow the cloudiness to clear", "Document the finding and continue with the next scheduled exchange"], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Cloudy or opaque effluent is the early sign of peritonitis, and the instruction is to monitor for it and, if peritonitis is suspected, send a specimen for culture and sensitivity. Other signs include a rigid abdomen, abdominal pain, lack of gas and absent bowel sounds. Documenting and proceeding with the next exchange delays treatment of an infection that can progress to sepsis, which is the highest risk for a client diagnosed with peritonitis. Lengthening the dwell time does nothing about infection and leaves infected fluid in the abdomen longer. Flushing the catheter is not a nursing intervention and risks introducing further organisms."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q27", "exam": 6, "topic": "Peritonitis", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client on peritoneal dialysis is diagnosed with peritonitis. Laboratory results show WBC 14.22 x 10^9/L, neutrophils 89%, and C-reactive protein 61.5 mg/L. For which complication is this client at highest risk?", "options": ["Abdominal abscess", "Pyelonephritis", "Sepsis", "Cellulitis"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A client with peritonitis and a markedly elevated white blood cell count with neutrophilia and a high C-reactive protein is at highest risk for sepsis, as the peritoneal infection has a direct route into the bloodstream, and the teaching for peritoneal dialysis specifically includes recognizing the signs of sepsis as well as peritonitis. Cellulitis is a localized skin infection that could occur at the exit site but is a lesser threat than systemic spread. Pyelonephritis is a kidney infection, and this client's kidneys have already failed; the infection here is in the peritoneum. An abdominal abscess is possible but is a more localized and later complication than the systemic deterioration sepsis represents."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q28", "exam": 6, "topic": "PD catheter teaching", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "The nurse teaches a client about peritoneal dialysis catheter exit site care. Which client statements indicate correct understanding? Select all that apply.", "options": ["“I will put antibiotic cream on the skin around the catheter with a cotton swab every time I change the dressing”", "\"Before cleaning the area, I will wash my hands with soap and water and put on clean gloves\"", "\"I will not use any creams with petroleum because they can damage the catheter\"", "\"I should remove crusts or scabs at the exit site before washing the site\"", "\"I will hold the catheter in place during cleaning to prevent injury to the skin\""], "correct": [1, 2, 4], "rationale": "Correct exit site care includes hand hygiene with soap and water followed by clean gloves, stabilizing the catheter during cleaning so traction does not injure the skin or the tunnel, avoiding petroleum-based creams because they damage the catheter material, and leaving the site open to air or covered only by loose clothing. Removing crusts or scabs before washing is incorrect - disrupting them breaks the skin barrier and creates a direct route for organisms into the tunnel and peritoneum. Routinely applying antibiotic cream around the catheter with a swab is also incorrect, so that statement indicates the teaching was not understood."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q29", "exam": 6, "topic": "PD poor flow", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client on peritoneal dialysis has had progressively poorer dialysate outflow over several days. Which assessment should the nurse perform first?", "options": ["Obtain blood cultures and begin empiric antibiotics", "Increase the volume of the next infusion to force the outflow", "Ask about the client's bowel pattern and last bowel movement", "Prepare the client for catheter removal"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Poor dialysate flow is usually related to constipation, so the nurse assesses the bowel pattern and encourages a high-fiber diet and stool softeners. A loaded bowel mechanically obstructs the catheter ports, and the remedy is simple and effective. Blood cultures and antibiotics address infection, which would present with cloudy effluent, abdominal pain and fever rather than a gradual flow problem. Catheter removal is a drastic step before the common and reversible cause has been checked. Increasing the infusion volume adds more fluid the client cannot drain, worsening the discomfort of carrying 1 to 2 L in the abdomen."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q30", "exam": 6, "topic": "PD complications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which complications should the nurse monitor for in a client receiving peritoneal dialysis? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Protein loss", "Respiratory distress", "Air embolism from the dialysate circuit", "Peritonitis", "Disequilibrium syndrome from rapid solute removal"], "correct": [0, 1, 3], "rationale": "Peritoneal dialysis complications include protein loss across the peritoneal membrane, peritonitis, respiratory distress from the pressure of instilled fluid against the diaphragm, inflammatory bowel disease, bowel perforation, infection, and weight gain with discomfort from carrying 1 to 2 L in the abdomen during dwell. Air embolism is a hazard of extracorporeal therapies such as hemodialysis and continuous renal replacement therapy, where blood circulates outside the body; peritoneal dialysis does not use a blood circuit. Disequilibrium syndrome is a hemodialysis complication, arising when rapid solute clearance across the dialyzer creates an osmotic gradient; peritoneal dialysis removes solutes gradually, which is one of its advantages."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q31", "exam": 6, "topic": "Delegation", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "The nurse leads a team including an LPN and unlicensed assistive personnel for a client on peritoneal dialysis with peritonitis. Which task is appropriate to assign to the unlicensed assistive personnel?", "options": ["Teaching the client peritoneal dialysis catheter care", "Administering intraperitoneal gentamicin", "Obtaining vital signs and recording daily weights", "Performing the dialysate exchange"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Vital signs and daily weights are routine measurements within the scope of unlicensed assistive personnel, with the nurse interpreting the results. Administering intraperitoneal gentamicin is medication administration into the peritoneal cavity and requires the appropriate licensed provider. Client teaching about catheter care cannot be delegated, as it requires nursing assessment of understanding and clinical judgment. The dialysate exchange is a sterile procedure with clinical judgment about inflow, dwell and outflow volumes, and is not delegated to unlicensed personnel."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q32", "exam": 6, "topic": "Dialysis principles", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client asks how dialysis actually removes waste from the blood. Which explanation by the nurse is most accurate?", "options": ["\"Your remaining kidney function is stimulated by the dialysate to make more urine\"", "\"The machine filters the blood using pressure to push waste out through a pump\"", "\"The dialysate chemically dissolves the waste products in your blood\"", "\"Waste moves from the blood, where it is concentrated, into the dialysate, where there is less of it\""], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Diffusion is defined as movement from an area of high concentration to one of low concentration, and in hemodialysis blood passes through a dialyzer, an enclosed semipermeable membrane, where it meets the dialysate and waste moves out of the blood by that gradient; peritoneal dialysis works by diffusion and osmosis across the peritoneal membrane. Describing it as pressure pushing waste through a pump misstates the mechanism. The dialysate is a balanced solution of electrolytes and water resembling plasma; it does not chemically dissolve wastes. Dialysis replaces kidney function rather than stimulating failed kidneys to produce urine."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q33", "exam": 6, "topic": "Transplant rejection", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Thirty-six hours after kidney transplantation, a client develops fever, elevated blood pressure, pain at the transplant site and anuria. What should the nurse anticipate?", "options": ["Acute rejection treated with increased doses of immunosuppressive drugs", "Chronic rejection managed conservatively until dialysis is required", "Expected postoperative findings that require only continued monitoring", "Hyperacute rejection requiring immediate removal of the transplanted kidney"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Hyperacute rejection occurs within 48 hours of surgery and presents with increased temperature, increased blood pressure, pain at the transplant site, and oliguria or anuria; the treatment is immediate removal of the transplanted kidney. Acute rejection occurs days to weeks after surgery, usually within the first week, with a temperature above 100 degrees Fahrenheit, an enlarged tender kidney, lethargy and rising creatinine, BUN and potassium, and is treated with increased immunosuppression. Chronic rejection develops gradually over months to years with a gradual rise in creatinine and BUN. Fever with anuria 36 hours after transplant is never an expected finding."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q34", "exam": 6, "topic": "Transplant rejection", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which assessment finding is the best early indicator that a transplanted kidney may be undergoing rejection?", "options": ["Mild incisional discomfort", "A single temperature of 99.1°F (37.3°C)", "A hemoglobin of 10.8 g/dL", "Oliguria"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Oliguria is identified as the best assessment finding to suspect rejection, because a kidney under immune attack stops producing urine. Rejection is the most serious complication of transplantation and results from a reaction between the tissues of the transplanted kidney and the antibodies and cytotoxic cells in the recipient's blood. A temperature of 99.1 is below the threshold described for acute rejection and is nonspecific on its own. Mild incisional discomfort is expected after surgery, whereas pain specifically at the transplant site accompanied by other findings is concerning. A hemoglobin of 10.8 reflects the anemia common in this population rather than rejection."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q35", "exam": 6, "topic": "Transplant medications", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client is being discharged after kidney transplantation on immunosuppressive therapy. Which teaching points should the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Take the medications exactly as prescribed, for life", "Report any sign of infection promptly, including low-grade fever", "Use strict hand hygiene", "Avoid crowds and people with known infections", "Report any decrease in urine output promptly"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "rationale": "Immunosuppressive medications - cyclosporine, sirolimus, tacrolimus, prednisone, azathioprine, mercaptopurine, mycophenolate mofetil and antibody preparations - are lifelong, because transplantation is a life-sustaining treatment rather than a cure and the immune system will attack the graft whenever suppression lapses. Clients taking these drugs are at risk for infection, so primary nursing care focuses on preventing infection through hand hygiene, avoiding exposure and reporting early signs. Stopping the medications when function normalizes would invite rejection, which is precisely when the graft is working and most worth protecting. Reporting a decrease in urine output is essential because oliguria is the best assessment finding for suspecting rejection, so all five instructions belong in the teaching."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q36", "exam": 6, "topic": "Transplant care", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client is 6 hours post kidney transplant. The blood pressure falls from 128/76 to 88/50. Why is this change particularly concerning?", "options": ["Hypotension confirms chronic rejection has begun", "Hypotension is expected and indicates the transplanted kidney is removing fluid", "Hypotension means poor perfusion and can compromise circulation to the new kidney", "Hypotension indicates the immunosuppressants are working too well"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Hypotension means poor perfusion and can compromise circulation to the newly transplanted kidney, which depends on adequate arterial pressure through its new anastomoses to the vessels in the lower abdomen. Restoring perfusion protects graft function. Immunosuppressants prevent rejection and do not lower blood pressure as a marker of efficacy. A falling blood pressure is not an expected sign of graft function; urine output is the measure watched for that. Chronic rejection develops gradually over months to years, not within 6 hours of surgery."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q37", "exam": 6, "topic": "Transplant surgery", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client asks whether their own kidneys will be removed during the transplant. Which response is accurate?", "options": ["\"One kidney is always removed to make room for the donor kidney\"", "\"Your own kidneys are usually left in place unless they are causing complications\"", "\"Your kidneys are removed and the new one is placed in the same location\"", "\"Both of your kidneys must be removed before the new one is placed\""], "correct": [1], "rationale": "During kidney transplantation the donor kidney is placed in the lower abdomen, its blood vessels are attached to vessels in the lower abdomen just above one leg, and its ureter is connected to the bladder; the client's own kidneys are left in place unless they are causing complications. Removing both kidneys routinely is not done. Space is not the constraint, since the graft occupies the lower abdomen rather than the renal fossa. Placing the new kidney in the original location is not the described technique."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q38", "exam": 6, "topic": "Transplant criteria", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which client best meets the selection criteria for kidney transplantation?", "options": ["A client with stage 2 chronic kidney disease who prefers not to modify their diet", "A client with advanced kidney disease, a reasonable life expectancy, who is medically and surgically fit", "A client with advanced kidney disease who declines all follow-up and medication", "A client with advanced kidney disease and an active untreated systemic infection"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "The selection criteria are advanced kidney disease, a reasonable life expectancy, and being medically and surgically fit. Stage 2 disease is early, with a glomerular filtration rate of 60 to 89, and is managed conservatively rather than with transplant. A client must be afebrile and free of infections before transplantation, since lifelong immunosuppression would allow an untreated infection to become overwhelming. A client who declines medications and follow-up could not sustain the lifelong immunosuppressive regimen the graft requires, which bears directly on medical fitness for the procedure."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q39", "exam": 6, "topic": "Post-transplant care", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which nursing actions are appropriate in the immediate postoperative period after kidney transplantation? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Assess for signs of rejection, thrombus and wound infection", "Monitor vital signs, intake and output, weight and laboratory values", "Continue hemodialysis until kidney function is maintained", "Discontinue all immunosuppressive medications once urine output is established", "Restrict fluids to less than 500 mL daily to protect the new kidney"], "correct": [0, 1, 2], "rationale": "Postoperative care includes monitoring vital signs, intake and output, weight and laboratory values; assessing for rejection, thrombus and wound infection; continuing hemodialysis until the graft maintains kidney function; and routine postoperative care including activity, diet, coughing and deep breathing, and monitoring for flatus and bowel movements. Discontinuing immunosuppressants once urine output is established would precipitate rejection; these medications are lifelong and urine output is evidence the graft is working and worth protecting. A severe fluid restriction is not part of postoperative transplant care; hypotension means poor perfusion and can compromise circulation to the new kidney, so adequate volume is protective rather than harmful."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q40", "exam": 6, "topic": "Nephrectomy", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client has returned from a nephrectomy. Which assessments are priorities? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Assess for flank pain and ecchymosis", "Monitor intake and output", "Monitor BUN and glomerular filtration rate for acute kidney injury", "Assess for signs of transplant rejection", "Assess for bleeding"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 4], "rationale": "After nephrectomy the nurse assesses for bleeding, which is the major early risk given the kidney's rich blood supply; for flank pain and ecchymosis, which can signal retroperitoneal bleeding; intake and output; and BUN and glomerular filtration rate for acute kidney injury, because the client now has only one kidney. Assessing for transplant rejection is not applicable - no donor organ has been placed, so there is no graft for the immune system to reject."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q41", "exam": 6, "topic": "Nephrectomy teaching", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client is being discharged after a nephrectomy. Which teaching point is most important?", "options": ["Resume unrestricted use of over-the-counter medications since one kidney compensates fully", "Expect urine output to be about half of the previous amount permanently", "Avoid all physical activity indefinitely to prevent injury to the remaining kidney", "Protect the remaining kidney by reporting urinary symptoms and discussing all medications with the provider"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "After nephrectomy the client has only one kidney, so protecting it becomes the central teaching point: reporting urinary symptoms early and reviewing all medications, including over-the-counter drugs, with the provider, since many are nephrotoxic or require renal dose adjustment. Resuming unrestricted over-the-counter medication use is the opposite of safe practice and risks the acute kidney injury the nurse is already monitoring for. A permanent halving of urine output is not expected, as the remaining kidney compensates. Indefinite avoidance of all activity is unnecessary and harmful; activity guidance is specific and time-limited rather than permanent."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q42", "exam": 6, "topic": "Conservative management", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "Which finding indicates a client with chronic kidney disease can no longer be managed with conservative therapy and should be considered for kidney replacement therapy?", "options": ["The client reports mild fatigue after exertion but is otherwise well", "The client's blood pressure requires two antihypertensive medications", "The serum creatinine has risen slightly over the past year", "Pathologic changes are life threatening or cause continuing discomfort and diet, drugs and fluid restriction are no longer effective"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "Kidney replacement therapy is considered when the pathologic changes are life threatening or pose continuing discomfort, when conservative therapies of diet, drugs and fluid restriction have become futile, and when the glomerular filtration rate is low and the client is symptomatic with fatigue, nausea, decreased appetite and shortness of breath. Requiring two antihypertensives is common in this population and is itself conservative management. Mild exertional fatigue in an otherwise well client does not meet the threshold. A slight creatinine rise over a year reflects the expected progressive course and prompts continued monitoring rather than initiation of dialysis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q43", "exam": 6, "topic": "GFR", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client asks what the glomerular filtration rate measures. Which explanation is correct?", "options": ["\"It measures the amount of protein leaking into your urine\"", "\"It measures how well your bladder empties\"", "\"It measures how much urine you produce in a day\"", "\"It measures how much blood your kidneys filter in a minute\""], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The glomerular filtration rate is how much blood the kidneys filter in a minute, with a normal value of 90 to 120 mL/min; it is the measure used to stage chronic kidney disease and to determine end-stage renal disease below 15 mL/min. Daily urine volume is a separate measurement that does not capture filtering capacity, since a client can produce urine while filtering poorly. Protein leaking into the urine is measured by urinalysis and reflects glomerular damage but is not the filtration rate itself. Bladder emptying is a lower urinary tract function unrelated to glomerular filtration."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q44", "exam": 6, "topic": "Types of PD", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client wants a peritoneal dialysis option that allows exchanges to occur automatically overnight. Which type should the nurse describe?", "options": ["Hemodialysis with a temporary catheter", "Continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis", "Continuous renal replacement therapy", "Automated peritoneal dialysis"], "correct": [3], "rationale": "The three types of peritoneal dialysis are continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis, automated peritoneal dialysis and intermittent peritoneal dialysis; the automated form uses a machine to perform exchanges, which suits a client who wants them done while sleeping. Continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis involves manual exchanges performed by the client through the day. Hemodialysis is an external therapy using a vascular access and a dialyzer, not a peritoneal option. Continuous renal replacement therapy is confined to the intensive care unit for critically ill clients and is not a home modality."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q45", "exam": 6, "topic": "Prioritization", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Evaluation", "stem": "The nurse is working on a renal unit. Which client should the nurse assess first?", "options": ["A client scheduled for hemodialysis this afternoon reporting mild pruritus", "A client 2 days post-nephrectomy requesting pain medication rated 5 out of 10", "A client on peritoneal dialysis with a rigid abdomen, absent bowel sounds and opaque effluent", "A client with chronic kidney disease whose phosphorus is 5.4 mg/dL"], "correct": [2], "rationale": "A rigid abdomen with absent bowel sounds and opaque effluent are the signs of peritonitis, and that client is at highest risk for progressing to sepsis, making this the most urgent assessment. A phosphorus of 5.4 is elevated and needs management with phosphate binders and diet, but it is not an emergency. Pruritus is a listed complication for clients on dialysis and a symptom of uremia; it causes distress but poses no immediate threat. Postoperative pain rated 5 out of 10 requires prompt attention but does not outrank a developing intra-abdominal infection with systemic consequences."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q46", "exam": 6, "topic": "Fluid management", "type": "sata", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "Which nursing interventions support the planning and implementation goals for a client with chronic kidney disease? Select all that apply.", "options": ["Preventing injury and minimizing psychosocial compromise", "Improving cardiac function", "Enhancing nutrition within prescribed restrictions", "Encouraging a high-protein diet to replace renal losses", "Managing fluid volume and monitoring serum electrolytes"], "correct": [0, 1, 2, 4], "rationale": "Planning and implementation for chronic kidney disease covers managing fluid volume, monitoring serum electrolytes, improving cardiac function given cardio-renal syndrome, enhancing nutrition, preventing injury, minimizing psychosocial compromise, medications and kidney replacement therapy. A high-protein diet is not encouraged, because protein metabolism generates nitrogenous waste the failing kidneys cannot clear; the diet calls for moderate amounts of protein, favoring plant-based options such as tofu, nut butter and soy yogurt."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q47", "exam": 6, "topic": "Dialysis schedule", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client newly starting hemodialysis asks what the schedule will be like. Which response accurately reflects typical outpatient scheduling?", "options": ["\"Treatments are usually three times a week, either Monday, Wednesday and Friday or Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday\"", "\"Treatments are performed once a month in the hospital\"", "\"Treatments are performed daily, seven days a week, for about an hour each\"", "\"Treatments are performed only when your laboratory values become abnormal\""], "correct": [0], "rationale": "Clients are prepared for a schedule of either Monday, Wednesday and Friday or Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday, with the first run usually at 6 a.m. Daily hour-long sessions do not describe this schedule. Monthly treatment would be far too infrequent, since missing even one session creates a high risk of accumulating electrolytes and waste, particularly potassium. Treating only when values become abnormal would mean waiting for the client to become uremic or hyperkalemic before each session, which is the situation the regular schedule exists to prevent."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q48", "exam": 6, "topic": "Patient selection", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Analysis", "stem": "A client with declining kidney function develops intractable nausea and vomiting, confusion and bruising with minor contact. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's planning?", "options": ["These symptoms indicate the client needs a higher protein intake", "These are uremic symptoms accompanying a falling glomerular filtration rate and indicate hemodialysis should be started", "These symptoms indicate a gastrointestinal illness unrelated to the kidney disease", "These symptoms are expected and do not change the plan of care"], "correct": [1], "rationale": "Hemodialysis is started for clients with uremic symptoms - intractable nausea and vomiting, confusion, seizures, or severe bleeding from platelet dysfunction - accompanied by a decreasing glomerular filtration rate. Easy bruising reflects the platelet dysfunction of uremia. Attributing the picture to an unrelated gastrointestinal illness would miss a constellation that fits uremia precisely. Increasing protein would add nitrogenous waste to a client already poisoned by retained waste products. Treating these as expected findings requiring no change ignores the specific criteria for initiating dialysis."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q49", "exam": 6, "topic": "Palliative perspective", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with end-stage renal disease asks whether dialysis will cure the kidney disease. Which response by the nurse is most accurate and therapeutic?", "options": ["\"Dialysis cures the disease as long as you follow the diet exactly\"", "\"Yes, with regular dialysis your kidneys will gradually recover function\"", "\"Dialysis replaces the filtering work your kidneys did; it manages the disease and symptoms rather than curing them\"", "\"No, and there is nothing further that can be done for you\""], "correct": [2], "rationale": "Dialysis is the process of replacing the kidney's filtering and excretion functions; in end-stage renal disease it is described as palliative in the sense of managing symptoms, providing holistic care and improving quality of life, rather than reversing irreversible nephron damage. Telling a client the kidneys will recover is false, since chronic kidney disease is progressive and irreversible. Saying nothing further can be done is both inaccurate and destroys hope, when dialysis and transplantation are both available. Claiming diet adherence makes dialysis curative misrepresents both the therapy and the role of the diet."},
  {"id": "n504e2s6q50", "exam": 6, "topic": "Anemia management", "type": "mcq", "bloom": "Application", "stem": "A client with chronic kidney disease has a hemoglobin of 9.4 g/dL and is prescribed epoetin alfa. Which explanation should the nurse provide?", "options": ["\"This medication prevents your body from destroying red blood cells during dialysis\"", "\"This medication helps your kidneys filter better so the anemia resolves\"", "\"Your kidneys normally make a hormone that tells the bone marrow to produce red blood cells, and this medication replaces it\"", "\"This medication adds iron directly to your blood to raise your hemoglobin\""], "correct": [2], "rationale": "One of the kidneys' functions is secreting erythropoietin to stimulate the bone marrow to produce red blood cells; its loss produces the anemia seen in chronic kidney disease, and epoetin alfa replaces that stimulus. The medication is not iron, although iron stores must be adequate for it to work. It does not prevent red cell destruction during dialysis - the anemia stems from reduced production rather than hemolysis. It has no effect on filtration, and the anemia will not resolve by improving kidney function, since the damage is irreversible."}
  ]
};
